Various people said one or more of the following:

>> > Am I the only one around here who hates frames and layout
>> > hacks that make me horizontally scroll on EVERY SINGLE LINE?
>> > Needless to say, I did not gain the benefit of Terry's wisdom.
>> > I left that website, never to return again.

No, you are not alone.  I have been running into an increasing number
of web sites where the web master appears to think that the purpose
of the web site is to demonstrate as many 'features' as possible,
or to promote one or another browser: and looses track of the fact
that the web site is probably supposed to be there to supply information
to customers (or potential customers).

I have been sending mail to many of those web sites, but unfortunately
the address for feed back is usually to the 'web master', the person
who designed the site in the first place.  What chance is there that
this person is going to let his management know that he did his job
wrong?

A number of them have tried to tell me I must upgrade to newer software,
or that people have told them they like their site.  I don't buy it.
While it is true that the newest web browser that supports frames
is available for OpenVMS (VAX and Alpha), that's not the point.  The
point is what is most important: demonstrating features or conveying
information?  I maintain  that the latter is the most important
purpose of most web pages: therefore, they should be designed to
fit the lowest common denominator.  (If possible, they should display
as much as possible even on character cell browsers.)  It's gotten
to the point where I plan to send mail (on paper) to some companies
to let them know they're loosing customers because of unfriendly (in
a few cases, downright hostile) web pages and web masters.  I urge
anyone who encounters a web page they don't like (even Digital's)
to do the same.

The other point is that frames appear to me to be unneccessary.  In
a number of cases I've needed the information badly enough to run
a frames-capable browser.  So far, in EVERY instance, the information
could have been presented just as well without frames.  I have never
yet seen an instance where frames really accomplished anything in the
way of information presentation or management.  I suppose somebody
could come up with such an instance if they really tried, but again,
let us consider the primary purpose of a web site.  If your purpose
is to share information, do you want to go out of your way to use
a feature like frames to drive away people?

>>         So whether we like the bloat-ware  that is Netscape, this is one
>>     battle  that I think is already lost.  The only defense is to put an

Not if the users say otherwise.  There is still time for people to
let web providers know when they are driving away customers.

>
>Incidentally, would downloadable PDFs be of interest to anyone? I'm
>trying to cost-justify the expense of Adobe Distiller.

Please don't. That is yet another step in the wrong direction: putting
information in a proprietary format which requires additional software
to interpret, and often cannot be read on-line.  I've run into this
in quite a few places as well.  Once again, in most cases there is
no information in the PDF file that couldn't be in more easily read
text and image (JPG or GIF) files.  In one instance, I've seen
a public transit time table which contained only colums of text
(stations and times of departure) which was put into a PDF file.
All this did was make the information harder to retrieve and harder
to read than plain text.  In a few other cases I've seen text and
graphics mixed in a way that could have been a bit more difficult
to present without a PDF file, but it could have been done in
a more user friendly way.  Yes, there are PDF readers (like XPDF)
that run on OpenVMS, but again, why do this to your audience?

