In article <SUSUKITA.97Jun30112442@postman.riken.go.jp>,
Susukita Ryutaro <susukita@postman.riken.go.jp> wrote:
>I add important information.
>
>In article <5p0ts7$eak@panix2.panix.com> tls@panix.com (Thor Lancelot Simon) writes:
>
> >Also, see which Bit 3 PCI expansion unit you have; they make one with an IBM
> >bridge chip and one with the Digital bridge chip.  I bet you'll have better
> >luck with the one using the DEC part.
>
>The expansion unit uses Digital Semiconductor 21152. The firmware of
>the AlphaStation 255 detects three "DECchip 21152-AA"s.

The 21152 is a very new part.  Perhaps the problem is that the firmware or
operating system on your AlphaStation doesn't understand the 21152 yet?
Certainly, the original firmware your 255 shipped with might not, since the
21152 wouldn't have existed yet when that firmware was written.

Have you tried updating to the newest firmware revision?

I'm also somewhat curious about whether your expansion box is being probed
correctly.  Does the box *really* have *three* bridge chips in it?  That
doesn't sound quite like the Bit3 expansion units I've seen in the past.  If
there really aren't three bridge chips, then whatever's there is being probed
wrong, and again, I'd say it's worth seeing what happens with new firmware.

Have you discussed this problem with Bit3?  Perhaps they'll have some useful
suggestions.

