> DEC 3000 model 400.  The machine is running OSF/1 v3.2.

Aha, had one of those Alphas on my desk for several years. Those were
the days (of crap money but a decent computer on my desk). Mine was
running VMS, though we had a couple with unix too.

> 
> Not having done much hardeware work on DEC equipment lately, I'm
> baffled.  The drive is connected correctly but I see no mention of it
> among the SCSI devices when I boot the machine so I can't even set the
> drive up.

Do you mean you can't see the disk at the console prompt or after 
OSF has booted? ie when you issue a

>>> show device 

command at the console prompt, do you see the disk or not? 

If you do see the disk at the console, but not after booting Unix,
you will have to label the disk and possibly make the device control
files in /dev. Sorry its been a while. I suggest looking in the System
Administrator's manual, its covered there.

If you CAN't see the disk at the console level, check you cabling. 
Power AND SCSI connected? Termination?

Hope this helps some 

