;>from Unix to NT, and the driving force is not engineer's perference, but
:>corporate cost cutting incentive for all the expensive EDA tools. 
;>
:>Understandably, the tool vendors would prefer to support a single unified
;>NT market vs the highly fragmented UNIX market.  The compromise, and the
:>way Unix workstation makers can get to keep more of their Unix boxes on
;>our desk, is to arrange so that we can have a NT box on the network acting
:>as remote-accessed compute servers.
;
:I can see no reason why (from the point of pcb routers) there should be
;a highly fragmented UNIX market. Those programs are mainly opening files
:reading/writeing files and doing computations. The X interface is used
;to display the result and the same on all platforms.
:
;If a company das to include any Platform specific code into their source
:they are most likely to sell you a piece of shit instead of a piece of software.

In an ideal world, perhaps.  But in reality all commercial program packages
are loaded with bugs that manifest themselves differently on different
platforms, even if they were compiled from the same source.  Bugs having
to do with uninitialized variables, or array bound errors, are very system
dependent. It is vendor's job to fix bugs, but fixing bugs is not a money
making operation.  Providing more features in new versions of the software
is, but it also virtually guarantees that the softwares will never be bug
free.  Since users will report bugs only if they manifest themselves,
fewer supported platforms means fewer resources devoted to fixing bugs. 

I have actually seen this happen several times.  I would take a CDROM
from a very reputable tool vendor, extract SunOS and HP binaries from
them and install them, and operations that would cause a segmentation
fault on one system would work perfectly normally on another.



