Bob DeBula wrote:
> Over the course of my computing career, I've learned pretty much that
> saying "we'll never need" or "they'll never use/make/develop something
> greater than X" is one of those things folks will inevitably trot out
> of the closet to hang in front of the erroneous computing pundit it
> future decades, much to their embarrassment. :-)

The bottom line is that the increasing bitness of computers is not comparable
to the increasing speed and capacity of computers. Let's take the oft-used
heuristic that memory sizes double once every 1.5 years (it doesn't actually
matter what the doubling time is; the point is, memory sizes are increasing
exponentially). So every n years, computer memories double in size.

For every bit you add to the bitness, we double the memory we can address,
so every n years, we just add a bit. It's not so simple though; it's super
handy to have the computer have a power of two size for its datapath, 
registers, bus, etc. So, we only want to bump the bitness when we've 
outgrown the current bitness. So, if we have a 32-bit computer, and 
want to address 8 gigs, we make a 64-bit computer, rather than a 33-bit
computer. That means that, once we've gone from a 16 bit address space to
a 32 bit one, it will take 16n years to outgrow the 32-bit address space.
Then, it will take 32n years to outgrow the 64-bit one. Then 64n years
for the 128-bit one... etc. So, barring some advance in computing that
causes the current (astoundingly fast) rate of progress to accelerate 
sharply, none of us will live to see a 1024-bit computer.

(Also, as bitness increases, the virtual memory system must add more and more 
indirect page-tables; I'm not sure how deep the Alpha's page tables go, but 
I'd be surprised if it were less than four-level paging. So high-bit chips 
would not only need a huge TLB, TLB misses would be super exspensive.)

It is worth mentioning that talking blandly about a "n-bit" computer has
at least two meanings. I'm taking it to mean a computer that uses n bits
to address memory. It can also mean a computer that does its arithmetic/
logical and move operations in units of n bits. Many computers are 32-bit
or 64-bit in both senses. It is conceivable to me that many scientific
users might have the need for 128-bit precision floating point numbers.
For most users, however, a 128-bit computer would be wasting most of its
bits. Don't believe me? How often do you need to count up to more than
4 billion, even in a program? How often do you need to be able to uniquely
represent more than 4 billion objects? If the answer is "really infrequently,"
you're wasting at least half of the bits in your 64-bit machine.

Keith Adams
kma@cs.brown.edu



