wen-king@myri.com (Wen-King Su) writes:

>I have actually seen this happen several times.  I would take a CDROM
>from a very reputable tool vendor, extract SunOS and HP binaries from
>them and install them, and operations that would cause a segmentation
>fault on one system would work perfectly normally on another.


It's the hardest part in porting software.  When porting code from
SunOS 4.x to Solaris 2.1, once upon a long ago, the SunOS 4.x->Solaris 2.x
code changes were easy, once you got into it.

By far the most time I spend on debugging executables that after porting
plain didn't work (dumped core; funny graphics output).  The majority
of those problems turned out to be off-by-one malloc() errors or
uninitialized variables.  While lint would turn up a lot of suspicious code,
malloc errors require runtime tools.  (Another big one was passing NULL
pointers to *printf(); SunOS 4.x would print "(null)" and Solaris prints
"Segmentation fault - core dumped".  Or the fact that Solaris wanted all
the fields in sockaddr_in to be correct, SunOS 4.x couldn't care less about
sin_zero or sin_family).

The valuable lesson I learned was that allowing people to get away with
stuff will only hurt you in the end.  DWIM is nice for user interfaces,
but bad for APIs.

Casper

