Mark E. Levy wrote:
> 
> Bojan Trafela wrote:
> >
> > I'm trying to load DECserver 90TL MOP from a 286 PC with a pathworks
> > v4.1a without VAX or other Decnet Server.
> > How must I configure PWRKS in config and autoexec.bat,
> > must I use NDIS or LANMAN,
> > when I type MOP on PC's command line I receiver following Error:
> > ?Portal open failed, DLL status: 3
> 
> Pathworks does not support MOP downline loads.  You'll need a host to do
> that load (not a PC).

Well, at one point PATHWORKS *DID* support MOP downloads. This was done
for the DEC EtherWORKS Router/DECnet (DE206), which was a DECnet router 
on a PC board (16-bit AT-bus).

Usually one installed the EtherWORKS Router in a PC with an Ethernet
adapter, and the PC that hosted the board was through the Ethernet
connectiona load host for the router (but it could've also been
any other PC on the LAN).

The plan was (possibly it was even implemented) that a newer
release would allow the software to be loaded through the PC
bus (before that the board only cared for the bus as a provider
of electricity, and to keep it from toppling over ;-).

Anyway, I'm pretty certain that it could be used to provide
a boot image for any MOP host and not just the EtherWORKS Router 
(as long as you get it over to the PC correctly first).

I don't remember how DECnet/MOP was configured, though, but
it must written down somewhere.

This was probably not carried over to the 32-bit protected mode
versions of PATHWORKS components, but the real-mode (DOS) bits
probably still has the code (somebody correct me if I'm wrong).

regards,
