In article <5p4bjg$eju@cocoa.brown.edu>,
Keith Adams <kma@doorknob.cs.brown.edu> wrote:
>(Also, as bitness increases, the virtual memory system must add more and more 
>indirect page-tables; I'm not sure how deep the Alpha's page tables go, but 
>I'd be surprised if it were less than four-level paging. So high-bit chips 
>would not only need a huge TLB, TLB misses would be super exspensive.)

Be surprised. Both Digital UNIX and Linux use three level page
tables on Alpha. With 8K pages (the default), that gives 43 bits
of address space. Digital UNIX uses bits 41 and 42 to make three
segments (seg0, kseg and seg1). Linux uses the same kseg but I
haven't looked closely to see if the kernel/user page-table-mapped
seg0 and seg1 are handled the same.

If you up the page size 64K, you get 55 (virtual) address bits.

--Malcolm

