>
>.... Unless it's an already multithreaded (Solaris) app.  In that case,
>no re-write is necessary for the performance boost.

I'd be careful about making that kind of claim.  Solaris is
SMP which is a very different animal from MPP.  Multithreaded
applications on SMP machines assume all of the address space
of the process is quickly available to any thread (processor) -
this is definitely not the case with MPP architectures.  In a MPP
design, memory (and processors and I/O) in different nodes is
*much* "farther" away - access has to go through whatever
"high-speed" interconnect they are using (but this interconnect
is still far slower than local memory).

Because MPP interconnects are fairly slow, the applications
written for them use the exchange of small messages between
nodes to coordinate the loosely-coupled resources.  The new
clustering technology coming from Sun in the next year or
so uses a interconnect channel that does 200 MB/sec bi-directional
(both directions at the same time).  Imagine that interconnect
being what is between *EVERY* processor, instead of the
multi-GB/sec of their SMP machines.  That is essentially
a MPP approach.


-- 
