>Let's consider a system which can access memory at a rate of
>16GB/sec.  I think we can all agree this would describe a "high
>end system"!  It would take 2^30 seconds to access 2^64 bytes
>of memory.  If my math is correct that would be around 38 years!

It has been demonstrated time and time again in the computing field
that it is always a mistake to evaluate future needs on the basis of
todays thinking or needs. I *remember* when folks (in the computing
professions) were thinking/saying that noone would ever need more than
1MB of memory. Some *will* come up with a use for >64 and someone
*will* develop a 128-bit implementation (unless the sun novas or
something equally catastrophic happens, like Wintel imposes an
artificial technology freeze on everything if they ever manage to
completely monopolized the industry). There are things in common
use today which require more memory than anyone was really thinking
of twenty years ago (except for a visionary few).

>It's gonna be a _LONG_ time before the technology required to
>efficiently access greater than 2^64 bytes of address space is
>developed and marketed!!!

If things remained STATIC, you'd be right.  But look how far we've come
in a short time and remember that folks were thinking 64K (then 640K)
would be enough to handle anything a few short decades ago....

>That said, I think that I will see in my lifetime 128bit systems
>in use, if for no other reason than DEC will want to say they
>have the first 128 bit implementation!  ... 

I think we'll see 128-bit systems within a decade (maybe not common,
but I bet they'll exist). Check back in ten years and see how I did
on my prediction. :-)

Over the course of my computing career, I've learned pretty much that
saying "we'll never need" or "they'll never use/make/develop something
greater than X" is one of those things folks will inevitably trot out
of the closet to hang in front of the erroneous computing pundit it
future decades, much to their embarrassment. :-)




