David P. Rabahy wrote:
> 
> The critical thing here is sparseness.  I think 80 or so bits is plenty
> for a dense address space in this universe and am perfectly willing to
> settle for 64.

No, for a dense addressing of each particle in the universe, you need
roughly estimated 80 digits (decimal), thus 256 bits are enough (10^77).
A computer that simulates the whole universe needs more (that's "God's
computer", running one of these freaky 10-dimensional fractal programs
;-), but for us, it's enough.

