X-Deja-AN: 253036591
References: <5mrt16$eo3@percy.cs.bham.ac.uk> <20JUN199705131884@gerg.tamu.edu> <33AAB31E.64786744@amcfac.enet.dec.com> <33B3FCD9.6113@nas.nasa.gov>
Organization: Cygnus Support, Mountain View, CA
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.arch


In article <33B3FCD9.6113@nas.nasa.gov> Hugh LaMaster <lamaster@nas.nasa.gov> writes:
>
>I still don't understand why this needs to be done by the
>hardware's virtual address mechanism, as opposed to
>being handled by a data structure which maps the
>aforementioned "address" onto the hardware's virtual
>address.  How many such addresses would there be, 
>how sparse would they be, etc. etc. - in other words,
>what is the application and what are *its* requirements?

  There are a few reasons..  It's slower, for one, to use 
virtual memory which was implemented in software.  It also 
requires that the programmer uses some sort of API instead 
of simply writing code which reads/writes memory locations, 
unless your compiler "knows" about your special memory access 
semantics and writes in the necessary code at compile-time.  
C++ can partially hide this, but not entirely.  If a technique 
isn't convenient for the programmers, they won't use it, it 
will never catch on as a de facto standard, and you will not 
see software appearing on the shelves which benefit from the 
technique.

  On the other hand, if you really want to use an N-bit virtual
memory space for some ADT, and the hardware you're working on 
doesn't support it, or you're writing platform-independent code
which has to run on the lowest common denominator, then rolling
your own virtual memory in software is a fine idea.  You can
even implement additional features which wouldn't be practical
to implement in hardware.  If you hide the details of the imple-
mentation behind a well-generalized interface then it will be 
more readily used by other programmers, too.

  -- ttk@cygnus.com

-- 




