Oh, what the heck, I will put in my $0.02 cents.  I am or use to be a
VAX/VMS guru - most languages, Sys Admin stuff - some very esoteric stuff,
blah - blah.

In 1987, I worked as a Cobol Programmer at $65/hour.  This was a big - the
biggest on the East Cost hardware wise - shop.  A big IBM Cobol shop -
where the IBM Cobol contractors were getting $35/hour.

Management needed a good multi-user machine so they bought this big VAX -
and a special $1 million dollar financial system (in binary only) - for
this VAX.  

OOPS, no one who had any experience in VAX/VMS at all.  No one who had any
experience in VAX/VMS Cobol.  Guess what - even then and likely today as
well VAX/VMS Cobol programmers are almost non-existent - very, very, very 
rare.  

This was NOT a straight simple minded Cobol job.  Nope, I had to trick this
$1 million dollar binary system into doing some crucial stuff it was not
designed to do.  Hmmmm.  

Of course management was clueless and did not treat me well in IMHO. It is
not nice to PO your only technical recourse.  What I needed to be was a
VAX/VMS systems programmer - which, as it turns out, I am.
weren't they lucky.  Did they appreciate that I was doing this so cheaply -
nope.  They hassled me - and hassled me to do it more quickly.

Hey, I can move fast when I have to - even when the work is the equivalent
of brain surgery.  It is that when I have to do brain surgery quickly I
tend to make more un-intentional mistakes.  My operating my be a success as
doctors are won't to say but the patient may die or be brain dead.

Anyway, that was in 1987 and I concluded that they didn't pay me high
enough to pay me for all the hassle - plus they terminated me because they
didn't budget this job for more than 3 months - so the job was left
incomplete.  Not my problem - contract work is T & M . 

So what happened to them?  I really do not care. I am not paid high enough
to care - especially when clients obviously do not care about me.

My best guess is they spent millions of dollars and it didn't work.  Who
else could do this work?

Now as you may note the IBM Cobol contract programmers were making about
$35/hour - ten years ago.  During those last ten years, the price for cars
has doubled - but the buying power of those IBM Cobol contract programmers
did not double - it is still around $35/hour.

So this means that since the hourly rate did not move upward to at least
keep these programmers with at least a break even buying power for today
they effectively now have half the buying power they had in 1987 - and can
only afford  to buy a car that is half the price they are today - um, now
they have to buy used cars - 3 - 5 years old.  Similarly for their homes or
apartments where they live, etc.

A very sad state of affairs for these IBM Cobol programmers - and it must
grind on them that they are forced to live with state of affairs when
during those same last ten years American Corporations have amassed huge
profits - every single year for the last ten years.  

We all know, or should know,  that Corporate America has made it very clear
they do not think it is important to share those corporate very high
profits with their workers - with any one who works for them.  

And we also now know - if there was any doubt before that corporate America
is NOT loyal to their workers - any of them - and 
will terminate any of  them if it means higher profits - without so much 
of a 'has this employee been a good worker for us".  It doesn't matter to
them - they are only interested in quickly amassing more profits -
regardless the social consequences in America.

In brief, corporate America doesn't think they have to hire American
workers.  Why should they when they can bring in foreign workers for coolie
wages and force them to live at poverty circumstances in America.   

Or, better they buy foreign companies so those foreign companies can hire
their own people for coolie wages and treat them badly in their own
countries.

Is it possible - maybe - that corporate America DOESN'T meet America's
bottom line - to hire American workers so these American workers can earn a
income for which they will pay American income taxes and those income taxes
will be used to keep the infrastructure of America healthy.

Well I for one think this stinks - not only for the American workers but
for the foreign workers as well.  

If you agree with me I want to tell you there is something POLITICALLY
POWERFUL we American workers can do.   

We can write to all our Federal and State representatives and state our -
especially our State representatives and tell them that we want the
following new conditions built into our *State* corporate franchise Laws:

1. American corps MUST hire American workers and if they want to hire
foreign workers it must be with special permission of  a civilian review
board.  If they do not do this their American Corporate franchise will be
revoked.  They won't be able to operate as a prestigious American Corp.

2. American corps MUST outsource their work to American Corps in     good
standing as per #1 - or get their American Corp status revoked.

We tell out State legislators in all fifty States that if they do not do
this they can join the rest of America's un-employed - and then follow
through.

This will work.  Gov. Pataki caved in when just a few small group of public
demonstrators said they would vote him out if he abandoned the people who
depended on living in rent control housing.

I welcome any discussion on this matter and welcome better ways of dealing
with American corps who have abandoned American workers.

Come one - come all.  

Regards, -= Lou Rizzuto =-

