Bob Hoekstra <HOEKSTRA_B@CFT.PHILIPS.NL> wrote:

>Larry Kilgallen wrote:
>> ...
>> Some say Linux will rule the world.  Personally I don't think any OS
>> will rule the world, but to say that the trend is inexorably toward
>> Unix is as wrong as any other prediction.

>Sadly, I agree.

>> A major flaw of "open systems" is that the concept flies in the face
>> of the free market economy where manufacturers try to _differentiate_
>> their products (else it is hard to say "my Unix is better than yours").
>> To look at a more mature industry, consider that automobiles built in
>> the United States have no common standard regarding placement of the
>> headlight switch and windshield wiper controls.  Methinks passage
>> of time will not unify the software industry either.
>> ...

Depends which 'Open System' to which you are referring. The *nix Open
System bunch were NOT a standards making body to begin with. The ISO/
CCITT (now ITU) Open System was a world standard (I was editor of the
ITU version from 1980 to 1984 and partially responsible for getting
the two versions [ISO and ITU] merged into common text.) A very common
mistake many people have made is to assume that the standard required
all vendors to code their stuff exactly the same - NOT TRUE!

You used an SAE reference and chose a trivial and irrelevant  example.
Why not pick tires? Imagine if each manufacturer could chose the tire
size - imagine what you would be paying for tires! Would you have us
believe that an 6.50 x 14 tire from some auto store house brand is
the equivalent of say a top of the line Goodyear 6.50 x 14? If so I
won't be driving in your car anytime soon. yet the 6.50 x 14 is a
"standard" (shudder) tire size. Even the windshield wipers come in
"standard" lengths.

>I think the flaw in your argument is that the future sale of cars is not
>critically dependent on third-party add-ons or the ease with which
>inexperienced drivers can switch from one car to the other.

As a road warrior I can tell you that switching gets to be a lot 
easier than most people think. No the UK and a sterring wheel on
the right side is slightly different :-)

>Examples:
>	(i) If most drivers were to require the replacement of the front
>passenger seat with an ejection seat (come back James Bond, all is
>forgiven) and there were a limited number of ejection seat manufacturers
>who agreed on the basics of seat fittings, pretty soon all cars would
>have their seat fittings correspond with the ej-seat standard or lose
>sales.
>	(ii) My wife and I each have car and whenever I drive her's, I go
>through an adjustment period. If I had to do this regularly, I would
>seriously consider replacing one of them so that they had more similar
>handling characteristics.

>Translating to OSses: 
>	(a) One of the main reasons an OS sells is the number of apps
>available. The decision to port application XYZ to a new OS is affected
>by the difficulty and the size of the target market (catch 22, I know).
>If a particular OS has similar (software/programming) interfaces, the
>port is easier, XYZ's manufacturer is more likely to port, hence more
>apps (of all sorts) will be available for that OS and more OSses (and
>apps) will get sold, resulting in price drops (we live in hope) and an
>upward spiral of sales.

The whole ISO/ITU OSI idea was to standardize the interfaces between
layers "where those layer interfaces were visible", i.e., if I build
a networking ASIC that interfaces with the leased line and with the
Transport Layer at the other end, those are the only two IFs that I
need observe the standard with. What's inside the ASIC is my affair.

>	    Unfortunately, most UNIXes are not like this. Porting a complex app
>from one to another is not trivial. I see that there will be a narrowing
>of differences in this area between different UNIX OSses (adapt or die).
>	    The fact that NT was at least partially compatible with earlier MS
>OSses counted greatly in its favour - most apps didn't have to be ported
>immediately.
>	(b) At work I look after a mixture of Sun, HP and SGI machines. For the
>inexperienced user, moving between HPVUE under HPUX and CDE on Solaris 2
>is no sweat, and means that it is much easier to justify a
>new/replacement Sun or HP. A new SGI (with a significantly different
>GUI) is more difficult to justify as it is more difficult for an
>occassional user. As a result, there are far fewer of these...

Bob


