Russell Hammer <russ@perneus.shore.net> writes:


> 
> Let's consider a system which can access memory at a rate of
> 16GB/sec.  I think we can all agree this would describe a "high
> end system"!  It would take 2^30 seconds to access 2^64 bytes
> of memory.  If my math is correct that would be around 38 years!
> 
16GB/sec is at most going to be mid-level by next year.  The current
high end systems are doing the following, all in MB/s:

Cray_T932_321024-3E         32 310721.0 302182.0 359841.0 359270.0
Cray_T3E-900               512 240428.0 233501.0 243368.0 265803.0
NEC_SX_4                    32 434784.0 432886.0 437358.0 436954.0
SGI_Origin_2000_2          120  19772.6  21483.7  24244.2  23369.8
Sun_UE_10000_assembly       63  10307.0  10180.0  10181.0  10107.0
Tera_MTA_simulated          16  30101.5  30033.3  30551.6  30509.8

The T3E & O2000 are based on commodity processors which have pretty
bad memory bandwidth.  The next generation of these things should be 
at least greater than a GB/s of memory bandwidth and by 2000 4-5 GB/s
probably won't be all that unheard of from any of the main uP vendors.

So assuming 2x every 18 months... I predict that within 18 years there
will be a machine that could go through 2^64 bytes in under a year.
It may be expensive, it may be used directly by only a few people, but
it will exist.  I don't think that memory bandwidth is really going to
be the problem, I think storing that much data is going to be the
biggest bottle neck.


> It's gonna be a _LONG_ time before the technology required to
> efficiently access greater than 2^64 bytes of address space is
> developed and marketed!!!

Yeah, but at least we're on the front edge of the conversion now.  
Remeber the nasty 16->32 bit convertion of yester year.  Hell, a lot
of people still are stuck with 16bit addressing.  I think that its
better to prepare for the future than let the future prepare to call
you a fool, ala bill gates and 640k.



