Ian G Batten (I.G.Batten@batten.eu.org) wrote:

> I don't know.  Let's see.  Let's take the maxium plausible resolution of
> a CRT as being 1600x1200.  OK, Let's go 16:9 wide-screen and make it
> 2200x1200.  Let's go to 24 bit colour, which is more than the human eye
> can distinguish.  And let's do 50 non-interlaced frames a second, for no
> particular reason aside from it whacking the numbers up a bit.  That's
> 377 MBytes/sec.  Neatly glossing over the backplane, disk and/or
> networking technology required to handle that --- Sun have _just_ got an
> OC-12 card that'll deliver about 65MBytes/sec, but you'd need quite a
> few spindles to service it --- that means a 2 hour film requires
> 2719080MBytes, or 2655TBytes.  Let's call it 3 Petabytes, shall we?  OK,
> 4 Petabytes!  That's 2^42.

Well, you've done some bad math there at the end (maybe just a mis-use
of "TBytes" and "Petabytes"), and aren't using realistic numbers for
the video rate.  Here's a summary of real HDTV (this is high
definition TV, not the original digital TV that will be implemented in
the next year or so).  Your point is still valid, but I thought it
would be interesting to re-make your argument using real numbers.

The image resolution is going to be 1920x1080 at either 24 or 30 fps.
That's a huge amount of data, but it's encoded using MPEG-2, and the
target bitrate is 19.28 Mbps (it might be twice that --- it's not
entirely clear to me).  Even at twice that, it's less than 40 Mbps, or
5 megabytes per second.  A 2 hour film will then take up 36,000
Mbytes, or about 36 GBytes.  That's about 2^35.

> So, you can fit 2^22, or 4194304, films encoded like that in a 2^64 byte
> filesystem.  

So if you could really make a server with a full 64-bit address space
(not a sparsely populated virtual address space), you could then fit
2^29, or almost a billion, films on such a system.  There's not much
chance of that being a big issue....

