In article <lZahpuoWWlrT079yn@pluto.njcc.com> hjohnson@pluto.njcc.com
(Herbert R Johnson) writes:

>I happen to know that the Decsystem-20 also had a Unibus interface (called the
>DTE or DEC ten-to-eleven if I remember right), but that was for peripherials
>only I think. Seems to me an 11/03 was in the '20 as well, I think in the KL
>mainframe, and *possibly* in the KS too, as a bootup and microcode loader....
>no, the KS used an *8080* processor as it's "front end" for bootup and simple
>tests! (shudder)

The Unibus in a DEC-20 was *only* used by the -11/45 front-end to talk to the
RP06 boot disk; the KL10 processor never saw the Unibus itself.

The DTE was a DMA device that allowed the -11 to write into and read from the
-10 memory; the -11 had its own memory as well.  Terminal I/O via DH-11 cards
was handled by the -11, with data passing over the DTE; the DTE was also used
in booting the -10, by having the -11 shove the BOOT program into memory.

Those who insist that the KL10-based systems had Unibi are missing the point
that the PDP-10 side could *not* make use of them.
