Rob Funk <rfunk@peri.acs.ohio-state.edu> wrote:
>In article <5os82b$43d@camel4.mindspring.com>,
>News Reader <news@aslan.mindspring.com> wrote:
>>In article <5ordhc$3bs$1@charm.magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>,
>>Rob Funk <rfunk@peri.acs.ohio-state.edu> wrote:
>>>Ah, you want to limit it to "most applications"?  OK, 16 bits is a lot
>>>for most applications
>>
>>Really?  16 bits wasn't enough for me on the 8086 and was not when I was
>>programming on my (now deceased) 8bit Commodore 64.  I often had to use
>>floating point which was slow.  Today, only occassionally do I need more than
>>a 4 byte integer.
>
>Really.  After all, most applications today are the same applications
>that used to run on the 8086 and C64.  They're just fancier now.

They do considerably more than they do today.  While their basic purpose is
the same, the means is far more advanced and taxing.  GUIs, for instance, 
need more power than older CPU's could provide.  Try running a modern GUI
based OS on a 8086 or C64 and watch it crawl (remember GEOS or Geoworks )

[snip]
>>>, yet we've found plenty of excuses to fill that up.
>>
>>"need" would be a better word.  We fill up what we have as we have a
>>need to, not just because it's there.
>
>My fundamental point is that you're wrong about that.
              
I'm not trying to say that we don't push things just because it's there, but
that need is what *primarily* drives development, not just the existance of
some technology.  Have you ever heard the phrase: "Necessity is the mother
of invention"?  How many people are running around and saying, 'hey, we've
got X bits in this CPU's address size, lets' max it out!'  Not many.

>If it's so
>necessary, how in the world did we humans survive during the centuries
>before we had computers, and the decades after that before we had
>32-bit computers?  If it's so necessary, how did you survive your C64?

We simply used alternatives or didn't do them at all.  Granted, the
existance of more powerful widgits allow for us to do things that we
couldn't do before, but it varies with different aspects and applications.
64 bit power exists today, but very few are trying to push beyond.  32 bit
power is king today, yet most applications don't push it beyond it's breaking
point, not because they don't want to, but because they don't need to (yet).

>I think we tend to perceive a need for some higher technology only
>when it becomes theoretically possible for that "need" to be
>fulfilled.

Yes, digital HDTV is an example.

>Soon enough, people will be saying they "need" more than 64 bits for
>their application..... Eventually somebody will "need" to store a mole
>of bytes (79 bits) to model something or other.  And eventually
>somebody will "need" to create a virtual Earth (or galaxy, or
>universe), attempting to get all the detail of the real thing to make
>sure nothing is missed.  As long as there's a limited number we can
>count to, we can only approximate the infinite detail of reality, so
>there will always we demand for more.

True, but my comments don't apply to specific cases as you mentioned, but to
the general software market today.  It is almost always possible to find
needs outside of what is.  My point is: how many need it today?
Technology is mostly driven by what the market needs, not by visionaries
wanting more than what is capable.

Aside: One could also argue that faster processors, buses, and the like, and
not CPUs with more bits is what is really important.


