Uwe Leinberger GSI Darmstadt wrote:

> Just assume another leap in storage technology and then calculate for yourself,
> how long such an experiment (usually running round the clock several months a
> year) to fill 2^64bytes, when we would choose to have all the data available
> for off-line scrutinizing.........

I'm somewhat involved in an experiment (http://www.rhic.bnl.gov/) which
will collect and store (and hopefully analyse) 1 Peta Byte of data /
year. 

That's 2^40 bytes / year.  So, I guess, to answer your question, that
would take about 2^24 years, or about 16 million years, to get 2^64
bytes.

64 bit addressing is helpful in that one can use some sparse addressing
techniques to access that data...or one can map the same data into a 64
bit address multiple times.

2^40 bytes / year is a lot...we have a O($million) budgeted just for
blank tape.

2^64 bytes is 16 million times more data....the storage medium would 
O($billion) in cost.

Note also that its real easy to make believe that working with a few
Gbytes gives one some experience in working with "large" file systems. 
Working with a few Tbytes is _much_ different....and we need to handle
that 1000 Tbyte number by late 1999.

Note we already use 23 GB disk drives, have purchased OC-12 ATM cards
and are awaiting Gigabit Ethernet equipment from Alteon.  We're already
assuming some leaps in storage technology.  2^64 bytes is still a ways
away...

--
