In article <866807269.womack@ox.compsoc.org.uk>
	womack@ox.compsoc.org.uk (Tom Womack) writes:

Tom> Carl Perkins (carl@gerg.tamu.edu) wrote:

Carl> 128 bit addressing is obviously needed- that's how many bits are
Carl> (supposed to be, at this point) in the new IP addresses, and it
Carl> wouldn't do to be able to address more computers on the network
Carl> that you can address in bytes of memory (as that would make it
Carl> impossible to store even 1 byte of info in memory for every
Carl> address)...  ;-)

Tom> Ooh, a paradigm. I'd like 256; allows me conveniently to
Tom> memory-map both the RAM and the mass storage (that's what the top
Tom> bit means) of every machine on the planet. Take shared memory to
Tom> its illogical conclusion.

Actually, 128 bits would be enough.  Use 64 bits to identify a machine
and allow each machine to allocate its local 64 bit address space as
it sees fit.  You've got enough bits for 2^64 (16 * 10^18) machines
each with 16 exabytes of memory.  Put another way, this supports more
than 10^9 computers per person, each with an obscenely large amount of
storage.  If we assume the average computer today has a storage
capacity of 1 TB (OK, so it's a bit more than average :-) and that
capacity doubles every 18 months, it'll take 24 * 1.5 = 36 years to
reach an average capacity of 16 exabytes.  At that point, we'll have
to start assigning multiple machine "names" to each computer, but we
do have 10^9 computers per person (doubling time for human population
is, fortunately, longer than that for storage capacity).

Suppose we get molecular storage devices.  If we store each bit in a
molecule of molecular weight 100 (a relatively small organic
compound), 2^128 bytes would mass about 100 * 8 * (256 * 10^36) / (6 *
10^23) grams, or 3.4 * 10^14 kg.  Assuming 10 billion (10^10) people,
each person's share of this data would weigh 34 metric tons.  This
doesn't include circuitry to read, write or process the data - it only
includes the storage medium. I guess it's possible that we'll need
more than 128 bits, but it'll sure take a while to get there.

For what it's worth, 256 bits is more than enough to give every
subatomic particle in the Milky Way its very own address.

