>Ah, you want to limit it to "most applications"?  OK, 16 bits is a lot
>for most applications

Really?  16 bits wasn't enough for me on the 8086 and was not when I was
programming on my (now deceased) 8bit Commodore 64.  I often had to use
floating point which was slow.  Today, only occassionally do I need more than
a 4 byte integer.

Considerably larger integers fit into 4 bytes as opposed to 2 bytes.  As a
result, fewer applications need more than 4 byte integers than applications
needing more than 2 byte integers.

A bit comparison:
2^8 = 256

2^16 = 65,536  which is (256 x 2^8)

2^32 = 4,294,967,296  which is (65,536 x 2^16)

2^64 = 18,446,744,073,709,551,616  which is (4,294,967,296 x 2^32)

{numbers generated by the cool 'bc' program} 

As one can see, it takes exponential increasingly larger numbers to match
the capacity of the word size.  Occasionally, numbers larger than 2^64 are
needed, but floating point approximations are enough for most applications,
though not all (science and mathematics applications in particular).   In
the case of non-integers, floating point must be used anyways.

[snip]
>, yet we've found plenty of excuses to fill that up.

"need" would be a better word.  We fill up what we have as we have a
need to, not just because it's there.


