>Why anyone wants to port slowlaris to the DEC Alpha's is beyond me, 
>especially since a "ready to run" 64-bit OS already exists for this platform 
>(I'm refering to Linux, of course :-)

Oh I'm sorry, you must have that backwards.  

Besides, it's not up to DEC to "have" Solaris or not, it's up to Sun.
I agree with Sun's policy on this two.  I think it would be a bad idea
to make your own OS available to one of your competitors that's not a
clone system.  

BTW, Concept problems this Linux exist.  The idea that no company is
responsible for this OS brings up some important questions.  There is
no one who is responsible if anything goes wrong.  With Solaris or
DEC-Unix (very childish) or any other company made Unix, there is a
company responsible for any big problems.  I'm not talking about
things faced by individuals, individuals only face problems of
price/perfomance ratios.  Big companies face problems that come with
large-scale implimentation of products.  Companies also face problems
with security.  When Sun find's a security whole, the ship an update
ASAP, when someone finds a security whole in Linux, they have to wait
for a progamer who "feels" like fixing the problem.  

I don't, by any means, mean this as a Linux-bashing article.  I think
Linux is great, and I'm glad to see it's out there and kicking hard,
but I did want to bring-up some other points.  Linux is not the
solution to the world's problems, but for an idividual who needs/wants
to run a real OS, not Micky-Soft stuff, it's a great tool.  Thanks for
listening to me.

