In article <5oqeda$rhs$1@news.th-darmstadt.de>,
Uwe Leinberger GSI Darmstadt <leinberger@axp621.gsi.!!no-spam!!.de> wrote:
> 

[[ I don't have Rob Funk's response here yet, so I'll respond to it as
well. ]]

> >>Hmm.  MPEG2 Film for VoD or Digital TV delivery runs at about 2MBit/sec,
> >>which is about the best you're going to get over ADSL in general anyway.
> >>That means a two-hour film requires 1800 MBytes
> >
> >Last I checked, an MPEG2 film didn't approach the quality even of
> >VHS, let alone real film.  If more computer resources are available,
> >somebody will design a format that uses those resources to get better
> >quality.  How much space will a 2-hour MPEG7 film use?

I don't know.  Let's see.  Let's take the maxium plausible resolution of
a CRT as being 1600x1200.  OK, Let's go 16:9 wide-screen and make it
2200x1200.  Let's go to 24 bit colour, which is more than the human eye
can distinguish.  And let's do 50 non-interlaced frames a second, for no
particular reason aside from it whacking the numbers up a bit.  That's
377 MBytes/sec.  Neatly glossing over the backplane, disk and/or
networking technology required to handle that --- Sun have _just_ got an
OC-12 card that'll deliver about 65MBytes/sec, but you'd need quite a
few spindles to service it --- that means a 2 hour film requires
2719080MBytes, or 2655TBytes.  Let's call it 3 Petabytes, shall we?  OK,
4 Petabytes!  That's 2^42.

So, you can fit 2^22, or 4194304, films encoded like that in a 2^64 byte
filesystem.  And since you can hang multiple filesystems off a Unix box,
you could have a few dozen of filesystems like that on your server.
Just how many films do you think the average VoD server will carry?
Have 4 million feature films even been made?  If you assume the film
industry is 100 years old, which it isn't, that still means you'd need
to have been making 40 thousand feature films a year to get to 4
million.

Can assume that your projected MPEG7 does some sort of compression?

> Many high-energy physics experiments digitze raw data in the 10's of 
> GB/sec range.
> 
> The limits in current technology (mainly data handling & storage) force us 
> to try really hard to pick the good from the bad recorded events on the fly,
> with the danger that we throw away interesting information.
> 
> Just assume another leap in storage technology and then calculate for yourself,
> how long such an experiment (usually running round the clock several months a 
> year) to fill 2^64bytes, when we would choose to have all the data available
> for off-line scrutinizing.........

10GBytes/sec for a year is 315360000GBytes, or about 300 PetaBytes.
Call it 512 Peta Bytes and it's 2^49 bytes.  So the answer is ``2^15
years'', which is 32768 years.

Next objection?

ian
