News Reader wrote:
> 
> In article <33AE5F95.DFA@CFT.PHILIPS.NL>,
> Bob Hoekstra  <HOEKSTRA_B@CFT.PHILIPS.NL> wrote:
> ...
> >Is the only reason for wider architectures the amount of addressable
> >memory?
> 
> Certainly not.  The Pentium II is a 32 bit CPU but can address more than
> 2^32 bytes of memory.  I was trying to avoid focusing on the memory addressing
> capabilities of a CPU.  The 8086 was a 16bit CPU but could address more than
> 64K of RAM.

I know this, but I've also heard it said that the way these processors
do this is not ideal. However, you answered the inverse of my question
-- I did not want to know if there was a way to address more memory than
2^x, but whether increasing from x bits to 2x bits brought benefits
other than the addressability of very much more memory. It seems to me
that this is all the discussion has focussed on so far...

So let's have some pie-in-the-sky:

Not that lots of memory is bad in itself -- performance problems (for
most apps) centre on disk, so if we replace disks with fast,
non-volatile memory, we could get a massive increase in speed for these
apps (at some cost increase, of course, but chips are getting cheaper
all the time, and I see the potential for chips to become cheaper than
the mechanical devices). Could we in future see companies offering
external NVRAM boxes like they now offer external disk arrays? Machines
with Terabytes of memory and no disk? Hot-swappable memory? Triple
mirrored memory (bye-bye backup <music in background>)? Could removable
memory be more reliable than mag tape?

Or, on another tack, as a crackpot example, how about a 128 bit bus with
three 32bit processors, each going full blast (no time slicing) and
communicating over a 32 bit section of the bus, with the remaining 32
bits used for inter-processor comunication? Note that the memory
addressable here would not be 2^128, but (at most, without Intel-style
kludges) 4(2^32), i.e. very much less. Perhaps a step forward in
multi-processor machines? Of course you could run 3 separate OSses
simultaneously, displaying on 3 separate monitors... :-)

Or how about pipelining code and data over different sections of the
bus? Perhaps something like this has been done?

Go ahead -- shoot me down -- I'm having fun ;^)

The real point I'm trying to make is that most people seem to feel that
widening the bus is not necessary because we can already address enough
memory. We seem to be discriminating between "high-end" and "desktop"
machines. I don't believe in this distinction - the high-end of today is
too slow for your desk tomorrow.

