Cray uses the same iron VMS does...alpha. (Since SGI bought them
they may switch/have switched, but not to faster chips...).

I'm not familiar with what NCR uses.

BTW, re mr Watson's claim the unix spec is open.
Maybe so, but that isn't terribly helpful to someone who wants to
implement code that needs to know how it was implemented. Source listing
access, on the other hand, is. Numerous such cases exist, and where
one asks "how can I get the best performance to do X" in a particular
system, choice of programming technique can depend on such knowledge.

This sort of thing normally gets isolated into a separate level of
program abstraction, but one has to implement it. Or alternatively
wonder if the public implementation will be competing against some
better performing secret internal call that the vendor's apps might
be able to use.

When you can see the internals detail, these decisions and worries
are not problems.

Unix vendors, to be fair, have had problems showing listings before,
since their code was licensed from an external source (originally ATT)
under conditions that required trade secret protection. DEC didn't
have that problem with VMS.  However, to someone trying to develop, say,
an HSM system, that's cold comfort. Most unix vendors in fact did get
source, not just specs, from ATT or its successors, and are thus not
totally free. Linux was a total rewrite, and NetBSD et alia evolved
into total rewrites, so that with the last bit of ATT code gone, the ATT
restrictions could also go. The closed unix versions still have these
problems, which of course tend to look rather different while you're a
student (student access being relatively free). Graduation puts a different
face on things...



