>>That's a lot of data-entry.  I'm not saying ``2^64 is more than anyone
>>will ever need'', but it's a _lot_ of data-entry.
>
>It is a lot -- now.  2^32 used to be a lot too.

And the term "data-entry" is probably wrong in this context. More
nearly it will be "data-acquisition" or generated data.  These massive
amounts of data are seldom acquired via the visualization that the term
"data-entry" conjures up (for me anyway).  I agree with Bill Yang and
Rob Funk that it may be hard to envision the exact "how" of >64-bit
*now*, but it was *also* hard for the average computing professional
(much less the public) to envision the need for >16-bit and >32-bit in
their early days as well. It is hard to say at this point specifically
how it will be used (though the folks in the scientific fields who've
posted in this thread seem to already have definite ideas), but human
nature and technology being what they are, I'm confident that data
space exceeding that mappable by 64-bits will be in common use right
after it becomes a technical and economic feasibility. The evolutionary
history of computing would seem to indicate (dictate) that it is a near
certainty.  I would think that eventually, if nothing else (as a 
pie-in-the-sky) that someone will get around to developing the 
completely "virtual" actor for the film/entertainment industry and
that type of thing will require the ability to map *massive* amounts
of data.




