everhart@arisia.gce.com (Glenn C. Everhart) writes:
> Linux, NetBSD, Hurd etc. sources are available, but by the criterion of
> affordable source availability, I think it is just to remark that 
> commercial unix offerings are not open, nor is VMS closed.

The Spec is open, and companies like Tandem, Sequent, etc. were able
to write Unices. Linux, NetBSD, etc. are manifestions of the fact that
Unix is really a *specification* for an OS, not really an operating
system, in the first place. And that *specification* is now maintained
as a resource. Understanding of this fact, that there were companies
that wanted to get into the business, and needed an OS, is fundamental
to understanding why Unix spread. That is also true of NT, BTW, but in
that case, there was already a low-cost "standard," so no one had to
write an OS.

