In article <33AE5F95.DFA@CFT.PHILIPS.NL>,
Bob Hoekstra  <HOEKSTRA_B@CFT.PHILIPS.NL> wrote:
> But how big do dbs get? I'm sure there are dbs out there that break your
> arbitrary 2^32 limit and if 2^64 hasn't been broken yet, it won't be
> long -- think for a minute: how about a simple db where each record is a
> film - how much space does a film take (even fractally compressed)? how
> many do you need to break your limits? (BTW, I don't know the answers)

Hmm.  MPEG2 Film for VoD or Digital TV delivery runs at about 2MBit/sec,
which is about the best you're going to get over ADSL in general anyway.
That means a two-hour film requires 1800 MBytes (yes, I'm playing fast
and loose over the choice between 1024*1024 and 1000*1000 for a Meg.  So
sue me).  2^64 is 18446744073709551616.  That means you can get
9773436691 2 hour films into 2^64 bytes of storage.  Looked at another
way, that's 2231378 _years_ of film, unless I'm driving bc wrong.

The population of the USA is 250-odd million.  2^64 bytes allows each
individual person to have 39 films in RAM, just for them.  Or,
conversely, the men with black helicopters can store 70-odd Gigabytes on
each American citizen with one 2^64 byte filesystem.  Suppose we're
storing records on everything I do.  Even if I make a loggable
transaction a minute, and the black helicopters record a K of data from
each event, and every other person in a 250 million population country
does the same, I and everyone else would need to live to 137 to use up
my 70 GBytes.

That's a lot of data-entry.  I'm not saying ``2^64 is more than anyone
will ever need'', but it's a _lot_ of data-entry.

ian
