In a previous article john@koala.scott.net () writes:
:
:: Because it is cheap, and if you already have Unix in your work group, you
;: can get access to all the Unix functionalities - printer, NFS, X-window
:: applications, etc, for next to nothing.  Thus you lose nothing if you buy
;: NT.  The reverse isn't true, and if you buy UNIX you will be limited by
:: what you can run. Unless you are willing shelling out $10K for softwares
;: that allows you to run NT applications on NT machines remotedly, and have
:: the window displayed on the UNIX workstation on your desk.  When it
;: comes to buying new machines for your work group, there is a tremendous
:: pressure to buy NT because of the cost and scarcity of software for Unix.
;
:: Unix is better than NT, but proponents of Unix need to understand that
;: the rule of the game is not to exclude the another.  In fact we need to
:: make it cheap/free to incorporate NT compute engines into our networks.
;: Only then, will the decision to buy be based on how users like each OS.
:: Otherwise, Unix will continue to be squeezed out from the market.  We
;: need to have a free version of this enabling software that allows you to
:: run NT applications remotely and have the window displayed on your Unix
;: workstateion. 
:
;NT is cheap?  I guess that is true but you can get Solaris x86
:for the same or less money than NT and it comes with Wabi to
;run the MS applications. www.etools.com/gold.html has the Desktop
:for $149.

I meant to say the total cost.  A decent pcb router package costing $50,000
US for unix platforms may cost only $30,000 US in the NT version. You
can't even get Solaris x86 version of almost most any pcb router at all.
The trade mags are talking about the mass migration in the EDA industry
from Unix to NT, and the driving force is not engineer's perference, but
corporate cost cutting incentive for all the expensive EDA tools. 

Understandably, the tool vendors would prefer to support a single unified
NT market vs the highly fragmented UNIX market.  The compromise, and the
way Unix workstation makers can get to keep more of their Unix boxes on
our desk, is to arrange so that we can have a NT box on the network acting
as remote-accessed compute servers.



