In article <33AAD704.36A46E1C@usdesign.com>, Chuck McCrobie <mccrobie@usdesign.com> writes:
:Now you know this was coming:  What revisions of the following Digital
:cards will I need for the KA655:
:
:     DELQA (EPROMS have DEC '88 LM8920 335E5 and DEC'88 LM8923 334E5)
:     DHV11 (? white numbers stamped at "top" of card list 21587)

  DIGITAL hardware revision codes are usually a letter and a number, 
  such as "A1", or "B2".  The revision code is usually either stamped
  or stuck onto the module, _usually_ near the serial number on newer
  modules.  On older modules, look around.

:     QDSS VCB02 (Currently do NOT have, but I am considering purchasing
:the 8-plane, 3 card version.  Is this known as the VCB03? )

  The VCB03 is the video controller for the `Firefox' series (the
  VAXstation 3520 and VAXstation 3540) SMP workstations, and is
  rather different from the VCB01 and VCB02 controllers.  For your
  stated application, you want the VCB02.

:I've heard the KA655 has a some type of "QIC" bug.  What's the
:revision(s) should I look for when buying the KA655?

  I am not aware of this bug.

:Finally, is there a DEFINITIVE method for obtaining detailed information
:like this?

  The "lowest acceptable revision" information is generally something
  one acquires from the local hardware service organization -- I am
  aware of no publicly available hardware revision matrix.  (I would
  expect most service vendors would consider this matrix proprietary,
  and I would also expect it would change markedly over time...)

:  The MVII FAQ site does NOT list information to this detail.

  The DIGITAL Assisted Services (also known as Self-Maintenance Services,
  or DECmailer) will provide you with the information on the lowest
  acceptable module etch.  (This is the program where folks maintaining
  their own hardware can acquire controller repairs and swaps, hardware
  diagnostics, etc.)

