On 22 Jun 1997, Bryan Althaus wrote:

> 
> Ben Chase (bbc@vnet.net) wrote:
> : Bryan Althaus (bryan@panix.com) wrote:
> : : VMS is well documented but UNIX isn't/wasn't?  Let me guess your a VMS
> : : person? :)
> : 
> : That's _not_ what he said, although if you consider The Source to be
> : the ultimate documentation, then any honest person would answer "yes"
> : to your question.
> : 
> : He said that on a particular operating system which is castigated in
> : the marketplace for being proprietary (ie. VMS), the almost complete
> : source code has been available for modest sums of money, for many,
> : many years.  
> 
> I didn't think VMS source code was available so I must have read past
> that.  When did the source code to VMS/OpenVMS first become available?
> When I was in college in the late '80's I never heard of VMS source
> being available, but UNIX source was.

VMS from version 1.0 till 4.7 INCLUDED a full set of listings on fiche.
OK, full handwave that the listing of netacp had one edge cut off so
as to require slight skill if you wanted to upgrade your end-node
system ;) But, OTOH, it did include the listings of VMS development
group tools  that did not get included on the kit.

From 5.0 on, these became CD listing kits, and are a couple of hundred
dollars. A source licence was, last time I looked, about $13K. Again,
a few things are excuded, ( LMF... ), but it's pretty much all there.
If you *REALY* need it, DEC has sold systems with full eschrowed sources
etc. I have also been told that some sites get everything in source
and do a full VMS build in house.


> If VMS code is so readily available, why aren't there different variants of
> VMS?  Why doesn't UCB or CMU have a special version of VMS if the code
> is available? Just wondering... 

To understand this, you need to look back at history. When the 780 was
released, DEC pulled the EDU discounts for the OS to a small level, so
putting VMS up cost serious money. For $~200, via AT&T and the peoples
republic of berkley, you dropped BSD on them instead.
  
Not withstanding that, there is a HUGE amount of user written drivers,
kernal mode code, interupt mode code out there. The abscence of `variants'
of VMS is I think rooted in two things; not wanting to get left in
the time warp if new versions of VMS change radically internally, and
the more engineered structure of VMS.

Add to that `The Book', VMS Internals and Data Structures, and several
3rd party books, the DECUS stuff, and you have a pretty complete pile.
Oh, that is in addition to `The Wall'.

~Paul




