R!ch (richardt@clem) wrote:
: On 20 Jun 1997, Huw Davies wrote:

: > Well, there are real advantages in segmented addressing, if it's done 
: > properly. Take a model with say 512 segments each with 64bit addresses
: > (the design for AXP+ maybe :-). Then it's really easy to implement things
: > like stacks which have different properties to code or data. 

: All this is well and good, but what do you think an MMU is for?

But I want segments and an MMU. Take an typical example program. It requires
program, data, a stack and possibly a heap. In a 'typical' system, all these
are mapped into the one (possibly sparse) address space. Now if one of the
dynamic areas gets "full" then you have to spend time moving things around
(the you might be your program, but more typically the operating system).
If on the other hand, each part was in a different segment, this can never
be a problem. I know that the PowerRISC architecture has segments, but I've
never cared enough to find out if AIX takes advantage of them in this manner.
