In article <5oiedr$7e7@panix.com>, bryan@panix.com (Bryan Althaus) writes:
>
>I didn't think VMS source code was available so I must have read past
>that.  When did the source code to VMS/OpenVMS first become available?
>When I was in college in the late '80's I never heard of VMS source
>being available, but UNIX source was.

We had MicroFiche for our first VAX 11/780, which was 1981 or 1982.  Your
use of "source code was available" means one thing to you (and is the
common meaning in the internet dominated now), but it meant something else
back then (and still does, for OpenVMS OS source code.) 

>If VMS code is so readily available, why aren't there different variants of
>VMS?  Why doesn't UCB or CMU have a special version of VMS if the code
>is available? Just wondering... 

Because having read access to the code listings is not necessarily the same
thing as having the right to modify it and reuse it.  I suppose Digital
would have let end users modify some driver or another for their own ends
(although, at the time, they would have had to type it all in - remember,
MicroFiche), but I'm 100% sure that if an end user had tried to distribute
the modified code without a legal agreement Digital's lawyers would have
been all over them.  Digital owned the code, but they made it available so
that anybody who needed to know something about OpenVMS internals could
find out every last detail, which was important when writing something
running way up high in the guts of the OS, perhaps running in Kernel mode. 

Digital's source code policy for OpenVMS is actually a very good one.  They
aren't about to give away their property rights, but they also aren't
afraid to let the end users obtain listings inexpensively. 

>That's what I think is OPEN.

Open this, open that.  At the OS level that's all opinion and marketing
hype anyway. 

Most of the time portability is an easier concept to quantify, so that I
can say that a strictly ANSI C compliant program is very portable, and will
likely run on any OS with an ANSI C compiler (which is any OS); that a C 
program which does not use ANSI C features is much less portable; and that
"ANSI" C++ is not very portable at all, since programs written in that
language rarely work when moved from one compiler to another, due
apparently to a huge amount of wiggle room in the language "standard", and
ongoing evolution of that standard.   This is for user level programs only.
Code, regardless of the language it is written in, that runs at high privs
and depends upon intimate knowledge of the underlying OS is inherently OS
specific.  That such code can often run on multiple Unices is a direct
consequence of the similarity of these closely related OS's, and no other
feature or capability, whatsoever. 

Regards,

