In article <33A90AD7.3F54BC7E@lehman.com>, Paul David Fox <pfoxSPAMOFF@lehman.com> writes:
> R!ch wrote:
> > A segmented architecture is one of the most stupidest ideas in
> > CPU design, IMHO.  Give me a flat addressing space any day.
> > 
> 
> Oops. Be very careful R!ch. If you look at Sparc assembly
> language it is not that dissimilar to x86 large-model 16-bit
> code.
> 
> Why? Well you cannot load a 32-bit constant into a 32-bit
> register in one instruction and you have to do it in two 'halves'.


Nonsense.  The appropriate comparison point to a flat address space is
the so-called x86 `huge' model. A comparison of the code required to
increment a pointer will make the difference glaringly obvious.

On a sparc, or a mips, once you've loaded the upper and lower halves
of 32-bit constant address, you can increment that address all the way
through a 32-bit virtual address space using a single add insn for
each pointer increment. (or 64-bit, these days, of course).

That's what ``flat addres space'' *means*, and you can't do it on a
286, or other x86 in 16-bit mode without doing ugly, hideously slow
segment-register manipulation, to propagate bits from the `address'
register to the `segment' register each time the former overflows.
(And that's ignoring, for the sake of argument, the 286's much smaller
virtual address space.)



