Bryan Althaus (bryan@panix.com) wrote:
: VMS is well documented but UNIX isn't/wasn't?  Let me guess your a VMS
: person? :)

That's _not_ what he said, although if you consider The Source to be
the ultimate documentation, then any honest person would answer "yes"
to your question.

He said that on a particular operating system which is castigated in
the marketplace for being proprietary (ie. VMS), the almost complete
source code has been available for modest sums of money, for many,
many years.  In comparison, for many other operating systems which are
touted as being "open" (eg. all Microsoft operating systems, most if
not all vendor-supplied versions of Unix), source is either
unavailable, or available only for very large sums of money.  I think
it is important to identify marketeers' spewage whenever possible.  If
_any_ of the mentioned operating systems deserved the appellation
"open", it was probably VMS.

If you don't think the availability of the source code is important to
many people, then explain the popularity of Linux.  (FYI: I am _not_ a
Linux zealot, nor a VMS zealot for that matter.)

 - Ben Chase



