In article <5oiedr$7e7@panix.com>, bryan@panix.com (Bryan Althaus) writes:
> 
> Ben Chase (bbc@vnet.net) wrote:
> : Bryan Althaus (bryan@panix.com) wrote:

> : He said that on a particular operating system which is castigated in
> : the marketplace for being proprietary (ie. VMS), the almost complete
> : source code has been available for modest sums of money, for many,
> : many years.  
> 
> I didn't think VMS source code was available so I must have read past
> that.  When did the source code to VMS/OpenVMS first become available?
> When I was in college in the late '80's I never heard of VMS source
> being available, but UNIX source was.

When VMS was first released, the source listings were provided on
microfiche.  That later became an optional extra-cost option.

In recent years the distribution mechanism has been CD-ROM.

> If VMS code is so readily available, why aren't there different variants of
> VMS?  Why doesn't UCB or CMU have a special version of VMS if the code
> is available? Just wondering... 

Because the license to get the listings CD does not allow you to
distribute it to another machine.  It is quite effective for hacking
together something on your own machine (or getting your existing code
to work when you neglected something) and then distributing your own
work (but none of DEC's) to others, for free or for money.

That fact that it is not legal to build your own copy of VMS and
distribute it to elsewhere is why there are few complaints about
it being in listings format rather than pure source.  Anyone who
wants to try to build one component from the listings should be
readily able to do it if they are competent to be doing such things
at all.

A full source license used to be $25,000, but most people settle
for the source listings.  In fact, for most seriously working with
VMS the goal is not so much to change what DEC has provided as to
one's own stuff work with VMS.  I am talking, of course, only about
inner-mode interactions with the guts of the operating systems where
one wants to depend on undocumented subject-to-change behavior.  The
average site has no need for the source listings.

This in fact is much the same as the Unix market.  Although there
are Unix-style operating systems where you can freely rebuild the
components and redistribute them (with source) such as FreeBSD
and Linux, it is not possible to do that with commercial-grade
Unix variants such as Solaris, AIX, HP-UX and DEC Unix.

> If you write code for VMS, you are running DEC hardware.  On UNIX

There have been some clone vendors offering VMS-compatible Alphas.
Of course Alpha chips come from DEC...
...whoops, Alpha chips come from DEC, Samsung or Mitsubishi (if
I got the names right).

> I've seen projects moved off one vendors UNIX to another vendors
> UNIX because hardware was better.  Your not locked in.  That's what
> I think is OPEN.

At the level where one needs to resort to source listings on VMS, the
comparable Unix code would differ considerably from vendor-to-vendor,
so I really doubt the alterations you made would port unmodified.

Larry Kilgallen



