In article <33AD26A1.5305@ACM.ORG>, "Mark E. Levy" <LEVY.M@ACM.ORG> writes:
> Anders Wilger wrote:

>> 1. PowerPassWord synchronize passwords on Vax and Alpha computers
>> running OpenVms. An OpenVms user only needs one password instead
>> of many. No training of users is needed. The users just changes
>> the password the way he always has done it and PowerPassWord will
>> immediately replicate it. This program is availible as a
>> shareware version.
> 
> What's the purpose of this?  If the VAXen and Alphas are clustered, and
> running a common SYSUAF which is typically the case, there is no need
> for password synch.

For the good guys:

	If machines are widely separated they might not be clustered.

For the bad guys:

	Synchronizing passwords allows someone who breaks into one machine
	to read the plaintext passwords used to access that machine and use
	them to break into another machine.

At least this offering is only for VMS, so there is at least a chance
that all machines are well secured.  Things go rapidly downhill if you
synchronize passwords between operating systems, thus making machines
from one vendor vulnerable to OS bugs from another vendor.

Larry Kilgallen

Disclaimer:  I have a commercial interest in not synchronizing passwords,
	     but on the other hand I felt this way long before I had that
	     commercial interest.



