R!ch wrote:
> 
> All this is well and good, but what do you think an MMU is for?
> 
> The difference is that as an applications programmer, I don't
> have to worry/care about segments.  In a segmented world without
> an MMU, I do.  To me, that's a big lose.


Sometimes it would be nice to have the power of an MMU in user space -
thats what segregs do for you.

E.g. consider all those discussions about avoid kernel copies
when doing system calls or 'zero-checksum TCP' implementations.

With segregs, you (in theory) can have more reliable code, because
your app can partition data sets (a bit like mmap-pages but
with mmap-pages you soon run out of room or have things
bumping into each other, which is why everyone wants to move
to 64-bits).

E.g. consider this. Under TOPS-10 (and possibly VMS) you had
two segments - one for user code and one for the O/S. Things
like getpid() would be implemented by just reading the appropriate
part of memory - no user->kernel traps involved. (i386 SVR3
did this - the u-area was always visible).

Now imagine the above where you can move pages or 'segments' from
one app to another, e.g. for message passing.

The things you can do are endless. The problem is that those 'long'
addresses can impact performance when not really needed so everything
slows down (e.g. 286 & 16-bit code on a 386).

Generally segments should be zero-overhead to be desirable but
I dont think any CPU architecture has ever achieved that in a way
that made people rush to write apps for it.
 
