re:
>VMS was always a good but very closed system with a few deficiencies...


There is one way that makes sense to distinguish open from closed systems
(unless you want to discuss NetBSD, Hurd, Linux and maybe one or two others
vs. everything else): that is, can you get internals information and
information on how to do things with the system that is reasonably
complete, timely, and inexpensive.

VMS has always been open by this criterion. Before there were listing CDs
(admittedly, the cost of a few hundred per yr. is high for hobbyists,
but is within the reach of a garage shop), there were fiche listings,
all the way back, that covered just about the whole system. No large
mystery APIs. No difficulty finding what internal routines are, how to
call them, and exactly what they do. No secret high-performance calls
that only the manufacturer knows about.  A very few bits that were not
shared...typically stuff like the license system and the common compiler
code generator...but the rest of it, everything shows, features, odd bits,
bugs, and all. Listings have been available machine readable for what...a
decade or so now? Machine searchable, yet!

If you were trying to do commercial development in one of the commercial
unix variants, this was not true...unless you could afford to spend maybe
a quarter million for sources, for those outfits that would sell them at
all. NT is just as bad.

So don't let's hear any more of this drivel about VMS being or having been
a closed system, eh? If you want to tar someone with that brush, tar someone
who deserves it.

'nuff said.
Glenn Everhart



