John Rowe wrote:
> Tsck, tsck, you should read people's posts before disagreeing with
> them, Alexis. The AS/400 isn't a mini-supercomputer it's a business
> server, it runs accounting packages. And with uncanny prescience I did
> say that corporate servers were still a viable market.

That would be super-minicomputer,  a mini-supercomputer is something
else entirely.  They took over from the first generation minis like the
PDP-8, PDP-11 and DG Nova.

I used to work for PRIME.  People used PRIME for what they use AS/400's
for,  primarily to run accounting and database work.  There were some
scientific and engineering users as well.  The intersection between the
super-mini's and todays server machines is rather large.   

Let's put it this way,  PRIME's #1 application was PIC (sp?) which is a
standard programmable database system.  People wrote applications in
PIC's basic language.  Pretty much a business server.

> How about explaining how *mini-supercomputers* didn't get killed off
> by the workstations.

Some did of course.  Certainly the scientific/engineering folks went to
workstations,  but alot of them were using PRIME's cad stations many of
which were small minis with graphics screen.  We also resold the new SGI
boxes which came out of merging SGI's graphics technology with MIPS's
computers.  SGI was using 68020's at that point.  Of course others where
using Evans and Sutherland or other workstations solutions.

Another factor is that you don't need as much server since you're
running all the applications and graphics remotely.  On the other hand, 
people want more out of the computers now than 80x24 character displays
and *that* takes alot of horsepower (even for the non-3D stuff)...

Regards,
Doug
