In article <33A7D565.106ED10B@usdesign.com>, Chuck McCrobie <mccrobie@usdesign.com> writes:
:I currently have a BA123 cabinet with the KA630 CPU (16 MB of RAM). 
:This is a QBUS machine (obviously).  I'm interested in upgrading the
:machine to a faster CPU (and corresponding memory).

:The FAQ I have lists the VAX 4000 series as being QBUS machines.  Is it
:possible to put those CPUs in the BA123 cabinet?

   [list of CPUs expurgated -- the upper-end MicroVAX systems have a
   Q-bus, but these systems use a private backplane for communications
   between the processor and memory -- they do not use a Q-bus (Q-22/CD)
   CPU design used on the KA660 and "down".]

   Upgrade path: KA650, KA655, KA660, VAX 4000-10x.
	The first three were sometimes refered to as the MicroVAX
	III, the MicroVAX III+, and the VAX 4 upgrades.

   The VAX 4000-10x series can be connected to a Q-bus for I/O,
	replacing the existing CPU and memory with an external box.
	(This is likely the best upgrade path.  Make sure your
	Q-bus widgets are still supported by/on the new box.)

   The KA50 (VAX 4000-50; "VAXbrick") and the KA640 would work, but
	they won't fit in BA23/BA123 -- these are (only) for the
	S-box systems.

   The other CPUs are not compatible with the Q-Bus backplane in
	the BA23 and BA123 enclosures -- the upper-end members
	use a private processor backplane adjacent to the Q-bus.
	Some (many?) require specific BA4xx-series enclosures.

   Be aware that any upgrade past the KA630 can require ECOs to
	the installed Q-bus controllers -- the QDSS VCB02, among
	other Q-bus modules -- requires a "recent" ECO for use on
	faster CPUs.

