> I guess the original poster was thinking about the VAX's, the PR1ME's, the

No, I meant what exactly I said. For the benefit of the historically
challenged, in the 1980s there existed a type of computer known as
"mini-supercomputers" which were essentially a poor man's Cray. A
Convex C1 gave you a massive 8 MFLOPS at a time when an IBM AT only
gave you 0.01 MFLOPS and a VAX 11/780 minicomputer 0.1 MFLOPS. You
could do things on a mini-super that you couldn't conceive of doing on
a personal computer or even a VAX. They made money.

Then came RISC. Suddenly an IBM RS/6000 320 gave you about 1/3 of the
processing power of a single CPU mini-supercomputer at a tiny fraction
of the cost. The mini-super vendors talked about how workstations are
OK for small jobes but for big jobs you need more than the CPU, you
need the memory and IO systems.  The workstations steam-rollered over
them without noticing they were there.




