>>256*256*16*64 = 67,108,864
>
>a pentium to do your math

My math is exactly correct.  That we failed to communicate my idea to
you is apparent.

I am proposing a virtual address that looks like the following;

level-name_1.level-name_2. ... .level-name_256.address

where each level-name is a string of upto 256 characters.  The lower
numbered levels are more significant than the higher ones.  This is to
say, identical level-2 names may appear within different level-1 ranges
-- think of directory trees or IP names.

Being concerned with internationalization, that 16-bit per character
character set might be good.  The address portion is a
simple/traditional flat 64-bit value specifying a byte within the
address range.

I would expect an established context to specify the most significant
levels and many, if not most, of the least significant to be omitted
altogether defaulting to nulls or some such thing.

So, for example, conveniently limiting myself to ASCII for the level
names and hex for the address range, there might be the following;

	the-world.my-oompany.nodeA.2A0000
	the-world.my-company.nodeB.300100
	the-world.your-company.USA-region.nodeA.300100
	another-world.my-company.nodeB.partition-nightly.56BE00
	...nodeB.Dave's-game.position-14.8FF00100

There'd be no need to clutter such virtual addresses with the trailing
white space of each level name.  There are a couple of nits to pick
around the delimiter character but this is more a problem for the
external representation as opposed to the internal.  If variable length
strings are desired then the classic choices of representation come up.



