Paul David Fox (pfoxSPAMOFF@lehman.com) wrote:
: With segregs, you (in theory) can have more reliable code, because
: your app can partition data sets (a bit like mmap-pages but
: with mmap-pages you soon run out of room or have things
: bumping into each other, which is why everyone wants to move
: to 64-bits).

You're saying that segregs will happily let me deal with a
(monolithic) >4GB data structure (array)?  Or that mmap will waste
"room"?  I do not think that the whole world is made up of nails, even
if a hammer is your favorite tool.  Yes, I deal with that much data
(>32bit), and have had to rearrange things (in unnatural fashions) to
avoid having data structures that large, and have had to reign in the
size of datasets/problems to avoid going over the 32 bit limit.  I
consider some of that time I spent to be time wasted by the lack of
64-bit pointers, file offsets, and files.  (No, I probably haven't
needed _all_ 64 bits; only maybe 36 of them.)

Do not forget, that today, for not very much money, you can buy a
single hard disk, which holds more than 2^32 bytes.  If I buy such a
disk, select an OS that will let me format it as a single partition
and fill it with a single file, are you still going to tell me that
I'd be better off using segregs?

-- 
 Ben Chase



