I'm maintaining an application with multiple threads that communicates with
a mainframe using the Brixton SNA Server library.  I have a problem that I
suspect is a bug with the DEC C++ compiler, but I'm not entirely sure yet
and I'd like some advice.

The program has four threads: the main thread, a thread that accepts
requests from our other applications, a thread that organizes the requests
depending on their mainframe destination [MvsInter], and a thread that
talks with the Brixton software [AppcComm].  Thread #4's work used to be
done in the main thread, but we needed the ability to communicate with
multiple mainframes so I made it multithreaded.  When this program is
operating, thread #3 gets a request to send something to a specific
mainframe.  It locks a mutex and puts the request on thread #4's queue,
then does a pthread_cond_timedwait to get the response.  After thread #4
sends the request and gets a response from the mainframe, it signals thread
#3's condition variable (using pthread_cond_signal, of course).

This appears to work properly in our current version of the software,
compiled using GNU g++.  However, when I compile this using DEC C++ (for
our upcoming version), thread #3's stack is apparently corrupted after I do
the pthread_cond_timedwait call.  I get a SEGV, and using the "where"
command in ladebug tells me that I'm at location 0x3ff00000000 or something
similar.  It can't produce a reasonable stack trace for me because the
stack is corrupted.  This only happens when I compile the program
debuggable (using -g); if I don't make it debuggable, I don't get a SEGV.

I'm starting to suspect that this is caused by the DEC compiler's
instructions that it inserts so you can view the state of a mutex or a
condition variable from the debugger.  I've tested this program for memory
overwrites (using Insight) and it doesn't tell me that I have any memory
corruption in the affected areas.  I am using the DEC C++ compiler version
5.5-004 on Digital Unix 3.2D, with version 3.07 of the runtime libraries.

Does anyone have any theories about why I only get this SEGV from compiling
this debuggable using the DEC C++ compiler?  (I would have tested this
using a smaller test program, but I've been under the gun to get this
project finished by today -- and I barely made it.)  I know that the excuse
"It's a compiler bug" has been used often enough before, but I can't think
of any other reasons.  :-)  If you have any suggestions, E-mail me at
tam@visi.com.  Thanks.




