In article <5o9ehl$8np@cocoa.brown.edu>,
Keith Adams <kma@doorknob.cs.brown.edu> wrote:
>Going from 32 to 64 bits is not comparable to going from 32 megs of ram 
>to 64 megs of ram.

I don't think it was ever claimed otherwise.

> Even given the
>exponential growth of computers' memories, very few people will be feeling
>cramped in an address space this enormous anytime in the vaguely near future.

How near is "vaguely near"?  The one computer industry trend that
really stands out to me is the if a resource is available, some
programmer will use it and someone will demand that it be used... then
the limits of the resource will be pushed until the next level of
resource is demanded.  And yes, this is accelerating.  People see
loads of memory (or speed, or disk) available, and first decide not to
optimize, then decide to add all the bells, whistles and 10-ton gongs
they (and their management and customers) can think of.  Just look at
MS Office...

>Furthermore, even if there were this much ram sitting in a machine, it 
>would eventually be a bad trade-off to make words 128 bits long.  The vast, 
>vast majority of integers that programs want to store fit comfortably in 
>32 bits; this would mean that applications would tend to waste more and more 
>of their storage as the word size increases.

The same argument could have been made about smaller bit sizes at
earlier times.  And remember that the most common integers used in
programming fit in 8 (maybe even 4) bits.  Even at 32 we're wasting
lots of space, and in fact I remember that being brought up as a major
reason for object code bloat in the 16-32 transition.

>Rather than just bumping the bit-ness further, and increasing
>exponentially the difficulty and cost of actually building such a chip,
>it would make more sense to do what the beloved 8086 did; ie, have 
>memory addressed by more than one word.

This is the first time I've EVER heard the 8086 and its segmented
architecture referred to as "beloved".  It was a kluge, and I think
that was known at the time.



Let's face it, we don't know what the future will bring, but the trend
is accelerating resource availablility, along with demand (keeping
together fairly well, interestingly enough).  Maybe in 30 years we'll
be using something we won't even be able to describe as an N-bit
system.  If we are, it wouldn't surprise me a bit if N>64.

I'm really curious about how laughable and meaningless this debate
will look in 30 years.  I'm trying to find an ENIAC-era quote
predicting the size of future computers....
