In one sense a backplane is not like any thing like a mother board. You
plug boards into a backplane that consists of any number of (board) edge
connectors WIRE WRAPPED together. The back plane is most like a modern
board without the chips. When back planes were introduced devices were
constructed of individual chips that carried very few active devices by
current standards. Maybe 8 logic gates of one kind or another, or so.
Backplanes were used in all sorts of devices what were not even
computers.

The UNIBUS was used in the PDP computers while the QBUS was the next
generation of the UNIBUS. The UNIBUS in its physical manifestation was
literally the backplane in the PDP-11's for instance. There was no
"motherboard" except that the board itself (again without the chips) is
analogous to the backplane. Typically in the early DEC systems
(non-PersonalComputer type) - (A.K.A. Mini's ) There is a slot for the
CPU board, each peripheral and the memory.

I believe that=A0 all PDP's used the UNIBUS and could use a QBUS adapter.=

While the VAX's used the QBUS (but there were special UNIBUS VAXes).
Thus the Qverter and the Univerter.

The adapters existed so that=A0 DEC could supply a common line of
peripherals for both system architectures.

Sorry. IBM invented the "PC" DEC developed their own Personal Computer
that sprang from the LSI-11 Chip set that allowed the later micro-PDP's
to fit on the desk. DEC's Personal Computers did not run DOS. They ran
their own operating system based on the file system developed for RSX-11
called the Files11 format.

Cheers.

David O.

