In article <33AA7AA0.59E2B600@lehman.com>, Paul David Fox
<pfoxSPAMOFF@lehman.com> wrote:

> Sometimes it would be nice to have the power of an MMU in user space -
> thats what segregs do for you.
> [snip]
> E.g. consider this. Under TOPS-10 (and possibly VMS) you had
> two segments - one for user code and one for the O/S. Things
> like getpid() would be implemented by just reading the appropriate
> part of memory - no user->kernel traps involved. ...
> ...

Well, I guess this is going astray a bit, but as an old TOPS-10 monitor
hacker I have to clarify/correct the above some.  TOPS-10 traditionally
implemented two segments, but in userland one was for [sharable] code
and one was for [nonsharable] data.  A wee bit of the nonsharable segment
(JOBDAT) was more or less constantly reinitialized by the monitor to
contain some OS related numbers, but it wasn't segmented off.
In supervisor-land, the KA10 had two segments as well, which (I blush)
I don't remember exactly how got used;  the KI and KL had page tables
that looked similar to any other paged MMU.  On the KI/KL, the two
segment userland view was OS convention, not hardware.
No syscall was implemented by mapping OS areas to be user-visible,
not that I ever heard of anyway.
That sounds more VMS-like to me.  (I never did anything real on VMS).

Karl



