What's missing from this discussion is the importance of the mass
market. It can cost literally billions to make the first chip, the
ones after that are almost free, so if you are going to sell tens of
millions of a chip you can give people something *very* fast *very*
cheap.

Workstations killed the mini-supercomputers because they had a
relatively mass market (doing mainly graphics) so they could sell fast
CPUs cheap. My guess is that graphics are starting to go the same
way. You're able to talk about a high end PC in the same breath as a
low end SGI, which would have been inconceivable a few years ago.
Graphics are going to be important to the mass market (read games) and
manufacturers will spend billions developing fast chipsets that they
can produce for peanuts.

SGI will talk up their product just like the manufacturers of
mini-supercomputers did in their death throes: "xxx are fine for the
small jobs but there will always be jobs that they can't handle".  But
not enough to pay for development of products a lot faster than those
that have had billions of dollars thrown at them. The difference
between doing something that PCs cannot do at all, which is were SGI
were a few years ago, and just being able to do it faster, which is
where they soon will be, is the difference between life and death. In
ten (five?) years time PCs will be doing real time 3-D.  SGI will
either be dead, a software house, using mass-market chips in parallel
for high end work, or making buisiness servers if Microsoft can't make
NT into a corporate standard OS.

SGI will discover it's tough being just above the mass market.


John



