In article <33a7fb2b.7808638@newshost.us.oracle.com>, rji@us.oracle.com (Richard Ji) writes:

>	I have a question regarding DecStation 5000/244.  What kind of
>CPU does this thing has?  And how does compare with 486 or Pentium?

There never was a 244, but it is probably a Model 240.  It uses a
40 Mhz MIPS R3000A.  Other things equal, x86 family processes will
tend to get more work done than a RISC chip running at the same
clock speed.  It depends on the load of course.  A CPU or system
may be very good at one thing, but do poorly at another.  An
application may take advantage of a larger cache in an otherwise
slower system and end up running faster.  And, CPU speed is only 
part of overall system performance.  You also have to consider the
I/O subsystem and memory design.

That said, most Pentium systems will probably be faster, if only
because few if any ran at a slower clock speed than the Model 240.
The 486 systems will probably be closer in performance and it depend
on the application.

When in doubt, benchmark.
  
