In article <5obada$cnr@deepika.squonk.net>,
	tm@squonk.net (Trip Martin) writes:
>R!ch <richardt@clem> writes:
>
>>On 18 Jun 1997, Keith Adams wrote:
>
>>> Rather than just bumping the bit-ness further, and increasing
>>> exponentially the difficulty and cost of actually building such a chip,
>>> it would make more sense to do what the beloved 8086 did; ie, have 
>>> memory addressed by more than one word.
>
>>Beloved by whom?  I've always thought it was a crock-o-shite.
>
>>A segmented architecture is one of the most stupidest ideas in
>>CPU design, IMHO.  Give me a flat addressing space any day.
>
>Well, it depends on what you do with the segmented architecture.
>An example of a good use of a segmented architecture is the old
>Multics system.  It made the entire filesystem accessible through
>memory, with each segment being a different file.

Primos had a segmented architecture too (no mapped files - at
least not when I last touched one over 12 years ago).  But then
a "word" on Prime was 16 bits.  The newer instruction modes
put together a segment + offset to form a 32 bit pointer.

For historical curiosity, there were only 12 bits of
segment.  The other 4 bits were a fault bit, 2 "ring" bits,
and a bit that was used for different things depending on
the addressing mode - in one form when it was a '1' it
meant that another 4 bits followed the 32 with a "bit offset"!;
in other modes it meant that the pointer was referencing
8 bit bytes rather than 16 bit words.  To say the least it
was pretty gross.

How about the AS/400?  It has a "uniform" address model with
all files mapped into the address space (64 bits now, I think).
Of course this is all the "intermediate code" that
gets washed down to raw code for whatever processor it is
running on.

>I do agree with you to the point that the use made of a segmented 
>architecture by PC hardware and MS software has been utterly lousy.

