> However, the
> VMS lock manager is much more general (if I understood the papers I
> scanned through quickly, the Sysplex lock manager has just shared and
> exclusive modes)

This is true so far as it goes, but the (Sysplex's) Coupling Facility is
far more general than VMS's, or any, lock manager.  The supplied lock
manager you mention is about as simple as possible, and is enough for
most things short of database work. 

Building a lock manager comparable to VMS's (six? levels with a
non-trivial exclusion graph) with the CF has been done (by Oracle) with
little difficulty; I don't doubt the DB2 team did something similar.

The impression I got is it's a dedicated B*Tree manager OS, running
alone on an S/390 connected to the rest of the sysplex via special
purpose (notably all but interrupt-free) hardware. Very fast, with a
very capable API, and RAS features up the wazoo.

