[snip]
>> 
>> I doubt it.  The amount of memory or data sizes accessible with 64 is
>> ridiculously high.  
>
>Granted, 2^64 is a really big number.  Frankly, it's a number so big I
>can't even begin to imagine it.  But that's also irrelevant.

But it is relevant.

>> The number 2^n increases exponentially, not linearly.
>
>That *is* the point.  Just as addressable space has expanded
>exponentially, our (perceived) computing needs have similarly expanded
>at about the same rate.

Hmmm, not quite.  Even with early PC's, there were applications that
strained to fit into the existing 640K limitation.  Most stuff was text
based with limited functionality so could fit into 640K.  But even on those
early systems, the 16bit architecture was rather limiting.  Today, 32bit
systems have been around for a while, but the PC world is only now just
getting around to utilizing it.  Before, there just wasn't the need (or was
there a need and Microsoft and IBM were just dragging their feet?)

>It really seems short-sighted to claim that just becuase you don't see
>an immediate application today means there won't be a vital
>application tommorrow.  That's the same kind of short-sightedness that
>lead to the the 640k problem on PCs.

That short-sightedness was because of a market in personal computers that was
new, and no one was even sure if it would ever catch on.  Few had any idea
that personal computers would become as popular as they did.  Nor were they
sure what trends the computer boom would lead to.  Today, experience is
considerably more then it was then.  We have a much better idea of what trends
may come and what the computing needs will be.  It will take much longer to
outgrow the 32bit architectures on the desktop than it did the 16bit (assuming
Microsoft moves with the times when the time comes).

>What it really comes down to is that no matter how big the system can
>get, we are going to do our darnedest to outgrow it.
>

Maybe, but as an example: Macs have been around since 1984 and ran on a
32bit CPU.  Today, Macs still run on 32bit CPUs and still have not exceeded
their capacity and won't any time soon.  The 16bit archecture limitations
were encountered soon after those systems came out but were not severe
enough.  Clever engineers worked around the problems and pushed back the
need to move to a 32bit architecture.  Considering how long it is taking
to reach the limitations of the 32bit world, it will take far longer to
reach the limitations of the 64bit world.  Maybe not even in our lifetime.
(NOTE: I'm not talking about super computers and such here, but mainly
personal computers, which are by far the vast majority of systems out there.)


