That's 10 times more file and print clients, before you start adding any
applications to the NT server.  What people don't realise is that if you
want to run an Oracle server which is resilient and performs well, the
only platform to run it on is Unix - would you seriously run a
production Oracle system on an NT server which is also serving about 50
PCs, and which has the overheads of a GUI on the console, and which can
be brought to its knees by a telnet session to port 139 by anyone on the
Internet?

As for running Oracle on a Netware server, the question has to be:

	Do I look insane?

Would you run the Z80 in your washing machine at home as your file
server?  Probably not, so why attempt to turn a file server into an
application server?  Would you want to NFS-mount the hard disk used as
spooling/RIPping space in your colour laser printer so that it could be
used as data storage to get that extra 200Mb space on the network if it
was possible?  Again, probably not.  So don't complain that your file
server can't run applications...

