}> : In article <339F9F56.1E4D4CFD@lehman.com>,
}> : Paul David Fox  <pfoxSPAMOFF@lehman.com> wrote:
}> 
}> : > 64-bits is the last step before heaven (or hell).
} 
}A much much larger sparse hierarchical virtual address space could be
}highly useful.  I'm thinking along the lines of 256 levels, each level
}named by a 256 character (maybe that 16-bit international character set
}standard) string, specifying a 64-bit byte address range.  With my
}scheme, the total number of bits required for the longest virtual
}address would be 256*256*16*64 = 67,108,864 bits.  Today's 64 bit
}virtual address spaces pale in comparison.  Naturally, fast translation
}to a holed flat physical address space is crucial.

Except that you appear to have used a pentium to do your math - what you
specified would actually "only" take 2097152 bits. (256 levels can be
specified in 8 bits, unless you want to make it a bit mask for some improbable
reason. Of course, if you weren't insane we'd have to wonder why you use the
level name as address bits therefore making every possible combination of
bits in the string into memory locations and using both the level ID number and
the level name name you said should be given to the level - each level will
have every possible name so level 1 with the name FOO<253 spaces> is a
spearate thing than level 2 with the name FOO<253 sapces> - but as I say,
you are obviously insane so we don't have to wonder why you want to do it
that way. Doing it the non-insane way makes it a paltry 72 bits.)

128 bit addressing is obviously needed- that's how many bits are (supposed
to be, at this point) in the new IP addresses, and it wouldn't do to be able
to address more computers on the network that you can address in bytes of
memory (as that would make it impossible to store even 1 byte of info in
memory for every address)...  ;-)

