Larry Kilgallen <kilgallen@eisner.decus.org> wrote:

>A major flaw of "open systems" is that the concept flies in the face
>of the free market economy where manufacturers try to _differentiate_
>their products (else it is hard to say "my Unix is better than yours").

Making an 'open system' and making a 'better system' aren't necessarily
conflicting goals.  The 'open'ness establishes certain constraints, but
there is plenty of room for improvement within those constraints.  

A non-computer example: you can buy a VCR from any manufacturer, plug it
into any TV from any other manufacturer, connect them both to a cable box
from a third manufacturer, and control them all with a remote from a
fourth manufacturer.

A simple computer-related example is mailers.  You can send and receive
mail between pine, elm, mutt, exmh, and rmail with no problems.  The mail
exchange protocol is open and establishes a number of constraints, but
there is plenty of room for clients to improve within those constraints.



