Chuck McCrobie <mccrobie@usdesign.com> writes:
> The FAQ I have lists the VAX 4000 series as being QBUS machines.  Is it
> possible to put those CPUs in the BA123 cabinet?
>
>   KA660 (VAX 4000 M200)

  This is a Q-bus processor. You may have a problem getting a cabinet kit for
it, as the most common version was in a BA440 cabinet, where there's a chrome-
pated door about 5 boards wide with the distribution panel, etc. There was also
a kit for the older BA-series boxes, but it is *very* uncommon. I know that Tom
Loonan at Compunet (800-767-9779) got one for a customer 3 or 4 years ago, so
he should be familiar with the part.

>   KA690 (VAX 4000 M400)
>   KA69D (VAX 4000 M50x)
>   KA700 (VAX 4000 M600/M700)

  These are all a new backplane style. While the expansion slots are Q-bus, 
the CPU/memory bus isn't, so you'd need a BA440.

>   KA1303? (VAX 4000 M10x)

  These were VAXstation-like boxes with 2 shielded cables coming out for 
attaching to an existing Q-bus chassis via paddleboard. This was DEC's solution
for customers who needed to keep existing Q-bus peripherals.

