Mike Fischbein wrote:
> Eh?  I was under the impression that I could only
> get NT from Microsoft or from a reseller thereof.
> Where are these non-Microsoft NT manufacturers?

Well, if you do not like your reseller, you can find another one. There
are plenty of folks selling NT. And when you buy a turn-key system
(hardware + NT), you have many vendors to choose from. So in terms of
business practices for the initial purchase, there are various sources.

But in the case of Digital (in the past), it was the only guy in town
for both hardware and software. Now, DEC no longer has a sales force, so
you can buy from various resellers.

> The contrast, in this thread, is with Unix and VMS.
> VMS, equally, is only available from DEC, and runs on
> different platforms (e.g., VAX and Alpha).

That VMS ran on VAX and runs on ALPHA is not quite relevant: they are
both effectively proprietary SYSTEMS from Digital. (Digital has failed
to attract significant manufacturers of ALHPA clones even though a few
do use the alpha chip for specialized applications).

So, while you can buy VMS systems from various resellers, it is still
pretty well tied to a single hardware vendor. Hence, NT is more open
than VMS in that sense.

Does it make a real difference to educated folks, perhaps not. But to
the sheep of the world who worship trade rags and jump when a dog barks, 
it does make a difference. 


>  Unix is
> available from many vendors (e.g., SCO, SunSoft, BSDI
> ...) and on many platforms (x86, VAX, Alpha, Cray, ...).

Educated folks would now that 
DEC UNIX != HP-UX != Solaris != DG-UX != ULTRIX != LINUX etc. Each has
enough "extras" that they should be considered proprietary.



