Larry Kilgallen wrote:
> Now Windows NT is as "proprietary" (favorite cuss-word of those who
> use "open systems" for praise :-) as they come.

You can buy NT from just about any manufacturer, from just about any
computer store. NT runs on different platforms, and runs on platforms
built by many different people (8086 architecture).

It used to be that VMS was sold only by DEC and ran only on DEC gear.
Without its sales force, we can now only say "VMS only runs on DEC gear
(ok, Tadpole's laptop being the exception). And you can buy VMS systems
from resellers (since you can't buy from Digital directly in most parts
of the world anymore).

Still NT is "mainstream" with lots of competition for applications (Word
vs Wordperfect, Lotus vs Exchange etc etc). So applications on NT are
not proprietary.

For VMS, it is basically "build your own app" systems with middleware
such as Oracle being available.

DEC used to provide a lot of software for VMS ranging from messaging to
EDI to network management, but few know exactly what is left of DEC's
software applications for VMS.



