On 17 Jun 1997 18:31:46 GMT, Li Pui Sai <h9417345@hkusua.hku.hk> wrote:

>Dear all,
>
>We have a pretty old Alpha workstation (3000 model 300) which only has
>32 MB of memory. It's quite slow as OSF/1 takes 2X MB already. We want
>to put some cheap SIMM memory to it. Is it possible to use the RAM for
>PC in Alpha station? It should have parity, right? How about EDO? Should
>I look for something without EDO?
>
>Thanks!
>
>Daniel
>
In general, you must have Parity memory for AlphaStations and
AlphaServers.  Depending upon the vintage of your systemboard,
EDO memory is not applicable.  As my Alpha hardware is about 1 1/2
years old, I am not sure if the newer models make use of EDO RAM
or still require Partity SIMMs.

However, I do believe that the
DEC 3000 and others hardware of that ilk need memory as supplied by DEC.
(If this was an AlphaStation 200/400, for example, you
could install any high-quality, parity, 70ns SIMM modules in the
approved combinations...)

My advice is to contact a DEC hardware reseller or DEC Technical
Support and check into the specifics of the 3000 hardware.





