 <wyang@ktel.freenet.columbus.oh.us> wrote:
>In article <339F9F56.1E4D4CFD@lehman.com>,
>Paul David Fox  <pfoxSPAMOFF@lehman.com> wrote:
>
>> 64-bits is the last step before heaven (or hell).
>
>Kinda reminds me of a similar line... "They'll never need more than 640k."
>
>It's probably short-sighted to make claims like "this is the end" --
>it seems that we are now only beginning to scratch the surface of what
>can be done with computers and information processing.  To claim that
>64-bit systems are going to be "big enough" for pretty much everyone
>is to ignore the cold, hard reality of the world we live in.  Our
>(perceived) needs EXPAND as the computing power available to us
>expands.
>
>I'd guess is that in 30 years, when I'm about to retire, they'll be
>rolling out the next generation of 2^n bit (that may be 128, 256,
>512... or even 1K) OS's....  not to address perceived problems, but to
>improve on the functionality of the systems we're dealing with.

I doubt it.  The amount of memory or data sizes accessible with 64 is
ridiculously high.  The number 2^n increases exponentially, not linearly.
32 bit systems will still serve most purposes for some time to come.  But, as
expected, some systems have outgrown 32 bits today, and need more.  The
jump from 8 to 16 bit systems (diff of 8 bits = 256) is quite small.  The
jump from 16 bits to 32 (diff of 16 bits=65536) is noticably higher, but still
fairly small.  The jump from 32 to 64 bits (diff 32 bits=4 billion+) is
astronomical.  As we double the bits, it becomes harder (and takes longer) to
reach the limit.  This trend will most likely continue.

Now data bus width is a different matter...


