It always amuses to me hear theories about "The immense decay occurring
within our society".  Especially when it comes after a MS
world-domination-theory to capture the souls of everyone their influence
touches.
:-)  I'm surprised you didn't claim that MS would soon be buying the Middle
East so it can leverage the price of oil in a way that OPEC never could. 
:-)

I see lots of problems too, but this society has always faced lots of
problems.  And we're at a stage when there are so many news magazines on
TV, CNN, etc., that the media has to bend over backwards to get your
attention, so now everything is a "crisis".  And if we didn't have
Medicaid/Medicare to run up the debt, the "crisis" would be: the elderly
poor, or the underserved lower socioeconomic classes.  And this "crisis"
would then drive us to make the same mistake.  Everything is always going
to be a "crisis" because that's the way it has to be in order for the media
to sell their product to you in the modern world.  The media itself has
even done some reporting on this "crisis".  :-)  The computer media, which
used to be much smaller and dedicated to technical matters, has taken off
with this, and now much of what is reported is written like some sort of
"Battle of the Titans" screenplay that never made it through the "process"
in Hollywood.  So now everything is DEC vs. Intel.  MS vs. Borland.  MS vs.
Netscape.  Whatever.  It's a bad screenplay, for the most part.  Godzilla
vs. Lizard Man.  Sure, it sells.  There are only about 30+ Godzilla movies.
 But they don't give much perspective on reality--although Godzilla films
do provide some interesting insights about the Japanese psyche.

If anyone has been sold into slavery to an industry, it's the American
public to the news media.  But it's not really slavery, because you can
just say "No" it.  I had some guy try to get me to accept 4 free weeks of
the New York Times a few weeks ago, if that's any measure of how desperate
these people are to get you to buy.  I live in Denver.  I said "No".  

It's as simple as that.  If you give these fine folks too much intellectual
trust, you lose perspective and start talking about MS dominating the
universe.  Then rant about the debt.  "These fine folks" are very often
"Communications" majors in college.  I find it very sad that we are living
in a country where public opinion is shaped by "Communications" majors--one
in which medical decisions are frequently made by someone holding an MBA,
where doctors' contracts include signing a NDA to prevent leakage of
information to patients about how individual decisions are made about care.

The media is like "The little boy who cried wolf".  And it's important to
realize that "Crisis" and "problem" are two different words.  I see lots of
problems.  Very few crises.





