wyang@ktel.freenet.columbus.oh.us wrote:
: In article <339F9F56.1E4D4CFD@lehman.com>,
: Paul David Fox  <pfoxSPAMOFF@lehman.com> wrote:

: > 64-bits is the last step before heaven (or hell).

: Kinda reminds me of a similar line... "They'll never need more than 640k."

....
: I'd guess is that in 30 years, when I'm about to retire, they'll be
: rolling out the next generation of 2^n bit (that may be 128, 256,
: 512... or even 1K) OS's....  not to address perceived problems, but to
: improve on the functionality of the systems we're dealing with.

Going from 32 to 64 bits is not comparable to going from 32 megs of ram 
to 64 megs of ram.

With a 32-bit machine, you can uniquely represent ~4.3 billion numbers. 
Assuming addresses are word-sized, you can address 4 gigs of memory. Doubling
the word-size doesn't make it possible to address 8 gigs of memory; it
makes it possible to address 4 gigs * 4 gigs of memory. Even given the
exponential growth of computers' memories, very few people will be feeling
cramped in an address space this enormous anytime in the vaguely near future.

Furthermore, even if there were this much ram sitting in a machine, it 
would eventually be a bad trade-off to make words 128 bits long.  The vast, 
vast majority of integers that programs want to store fit comfortably in 
32 bits; this would mean that applications would tend to waste more and more 
of their storage as the word size increases. You could use special instructions for addressing partial words, but alligned accesses are always faster. 

Rather than just bumping the bit-ness further, and increasing
exponentially the difficulty and cost of actually building such a chip,
it would make more sense to do what the beloved 8086 did; ie, have 
memory addressed by more than one word.

--Keith Adams
kma@cs.brown.edu



