Greg Pfister wrote:

> Larry Kilgallen wrote:
> [snip]
> > I don't know a whole lot about LPARs, but based on semantics, the
> > "Adaptive..." would configure itself on the fly.  I had thought that
>
> > LPARs were always set up by an administrator.
>
> That's true.  If Galaxy (apparently the singular is more appropriate)
> can do it on the fly, that would be new.  I'll be interested to see if
>
> that's true.

I thought that was true,  but on checking with Mike Sheets, the czar of
partitions, it turns out not to be so.  LPARs don't dynamically
re-configure themselves, but they are adaptive to workload and other
considerations. From Mike:

> [These] comments about LPAR are accurate with respect to LPAR
> DEDICATED partitions (i.e., partitions which have a "1 to 1 and onto
> mapping" of logical to physical CPs).  Dedicated partitions are, in
> general, not recommended.  The last survey I saw (1991) showed 10%
> of all customer partitions as dedicated.

> But [it] is NOT accurate with respect to SHARED partitions. Shared
> partitions can respond to dramatic changes in load dynamically
> without re-booting. I will outline three ways...

> 1) Normal event-driven scheduling:  If you define two logical
> 10-ways shared on a physical 10-way, and if each has complementary
> peak loads, each can take over the entire physical 10-way as the
> workload load shifts without a re-boot or operator intervention..
>
> 2) Logical CP Vary:  Partitions' logical CP's can be varied -off or
> -on dynamically via normal OS/390 operator commands without re-boot.
> The only limitation is that the number of CPs on-line at IPL time is
> the maximum.
>
> 3) Weights: In cases where the aggregate workload demand of all
> partitions is more than can be delivered, LPAR weights define how
> much CPU resource is given to each partition. These weights can be
> changed by operators on-the-fly with no disruption.
>
> As far as references, most of this was done so long ago that
references
> exist only in hardcopy. For example, "Multiple operating systems on
one
> processor complex", by Borden, Hennessy, and Rymarczyk
>  in IBM Sys J. V28 No. 1, P104 (1989); "PR/SM in LPAR Mode
> Performance and Capacity Planning Characteristics", by me in
Enterprise
> Systems Journal, October 1992, P31; or, of course, the PR/SM Planning
> Guide (GA22-7236).

