Nah, Affinity is mostly a marketing ploy.  You have to have a data center,
serious bucks, and a huge staff to make use of any part of it.

If you want something useful, here's a secret - OpenVMS is a better
Windows fileserver than WNT. You can set up a 365 X 24 OpenVMS cluster with
Pathworks and cluster aliases, and if one of the nodes crashes (once in a
blue moon), all of the Windows clients will fail over silently, mostly
without problems.  (There is apparently a limit on this failover set at the
client end, some versions of WordPerfect for Windows will notice the
failure and require a manual reconnect, but in general, you're golden. Even
OpenVMS cluster technology can't overcome client limitations.) 
If a WNT "cluster" has a node failure (perhaps somebody accidentally pumps
the wrong three bytes into it over the net) every single client will have
to manually reconnect to the other node, once it picks up the service.  I
suppose that's better than not having anything to reconnect to at all, but
what does it cost your company if 200 Windows clients all have to restart
whatever they were doing a couple of times a month?

Naturally, in order not to embarrass Microsoft, Digital prices Pathworks
client licenses far above what Microsoft charges for WNT access licenses
(or whatever they call them), and tactfully avoids advertising this product 
at all.  In all probability they will sell it or retire it, or otherwise
destroy it, so that they don't ruffle any Microsoft feathers.  It wouldn't
do for the press to stumble onto a nonMicrosoft solution!  Not that there 
is much chance of that with our stalwart journalistic brethren seeking out
the best solutions for us.  Go ahead, search Datamation's site
(www.datamation.com) for "pathworks" - you will find it named in
passing in one or two articles, but never so much as an explanation as to
what it is.  McGraw-Hill publications (Byte etc.)? Same deal. If you didn't
know that Pathworks existed, the rags would ever find it for you, never 
mind, perish the thought, provide a head to head comparison with WNT
server. 

>Many hope that the new guy at DEC would get Bill Gates to drop NT as a
>server and get MS to sell VMS as the server of choice for NT
>environments which would include clustering, stability etc and beat the
>pants off UNIX.

Never going to happen, BG would sooner poke his eye out with a sharp stick 
than withdraw from the server market.  The best we can hope for would be
that the next Digital management team will stop transferring key
technologies to Microsoft for squat in return, and actually position
OpenVMS (and DU) to compete with WNT, rather than shoving WNT down our
throats.  [ Mr. Palmer will then be free to move on to be CEO of GM,
where he'll sell all the factories to Ford in exchange for training the GM
service center mechanics to work on Mercuries, sell Saturns for $50000
(there is no market for a low cost Saturn, right?), and tell us that our
future is with the Taurus!]

