IMPACT.? This Indigo2 features 24-bit Z-buffer, 8-bit alpha, 8-bit
Stencil, 8-bit overlay to name some of the more quantitative features.

The 600au is also estimated at ~$30K, only 45% faster CPU. Hmmm ...

SGI charge $2150 for a 64Mb DRAM upgrade too. The extras prices are all
disgraceful.

An Alphastation 500/400 with 128Mb DRAM and a PowerStorm 4D50T card may be
owned for just $19,338 (aided by a 35% price drop by DEC).

The 4D50T has only 6-bit stencil/alpha planes but will support up to 32Mb
texture memory at (using DEC's blurb) 1/10th the cost of what SGI charge
(which is $5500 for a mere 4Mb).

The 4D60T is (on paper) comparable to the Indigo2 (and in some ways
superior - 32-bit Z-buffer) - for $12,000 {wince}. 

PC's and alphastations also benefit from an open competitive market -
there are some very competitive and more cost effective third party
solutions available than what DEC currently offers.

At the end of the day, only extensive benchmarks on each aspect of
rendering could show where the opposition's cards stand on performance
before the top-end SGI workstations beat them all.

