In article <5o4u7a$lcg@panix.com>, bryan@panix.com (Bryan Althaus) writes:

> Even if the scaling of Dr Everhart's Galaxy for VMS is awesome, if anything
> this will retain customers, I just don't see this late in the Open Systems
> game people going back the other way.  

But many others do.  There are predictions that Windows NT will wipe out
interest in Unix systems in all but academic and scientific bastions.
Now Windows NT is as "proprietary" (favorite cuss-word of those who
use "open systems" for praise :-) as they come.

Some say Linux will rule the world.  Personally I don't think any OS
will rule the world, but to say that the trend is inexorably toward
Unix is as wrong as any other prediction.

A major flaw of "open systems" is that the concept flies in the face
of the free market economy where manufacturers try to _differentiate_
their products (else it is hard to say "my Unix is better than yours").
To look at a more mature industry, consider that automobiles built in
the United States have no common standard regarding placement of the
headlight switch and windshield wiper controls.  Methinks passage
of time will not unify the software industry either.

> anything done in Galaxy, I would have to assume could be brought over
> to another OS, including Digital UNIX.

And in response to the "flaw" I outlined above, Unix vendors attempt to
prove their loyalty to the common goal by retaining a Unix-like _flavor_
to their work.  Requiring file access go through a distributed lock
manager, or imposing discipline on the storage of records in files,
would break faith with the Unix community.

So it might be that the nature of VMS is uniquely suited to optimizations
used in the Galaxy project.  If Galaxy produces strong performance boosts
for large machines that would strengthen the position of VMS in the shops
that need such horsepower.  It would not have any effect on those whose
prime requirement is a Unix interface, but from an OS perspective it is
hard to compete head-to-head with Linux on price :-).

Larry Kilgallen



