In article <33A6C452.71FB@bt-sys.bt.co.uk>,
Kessack Young-Smith  <kessack.young-smith@bt-sys.bt.co.uk> wrote:
>**QBUS(LSI11) vs UNIBUS**
>Can anyone give me a succinct description of ESSENTIAL differences.

The shortest: you don't plug a Unibus card into a Q-bus backplane
nor can you do it the other way around, unless your intended result
is a lot of sparks and smoke.  Able makes (made?)
converters between the two busses, called the Univerter and
Qniverter.

Very detailed descriptions of the Unibus and the various incarnations
of the Q-bus are available in countless DEC manuals.

>**PDP Backplane**
>Is the 'backplane' on a DEC computer like the motherboard on a PC ?

It depends on what you call "a DEC computer" and what you call a
"PC".  In particular, personal computers were around for many years
before the IBM PC ever appeared, and DEC has been in the PC-clone
arena for a long time now.  But assuming that
you are talking about traditional -11's (i.e. not the Pro) and
a typical street-vendor-assembled PC-clone, no they
aren't the same.  In particular, the backplane on a Q-bus or Unibus -11
doesn't have the CPU on it.  Note that "passive motherboard" PC-clone
systems (where the CPU is just another card in the system) have been
available for many years, too, and are quite popular in industrial
control and other areas.

Some people will claim that a "motherboard" necessarily has a
CPU on it, while a "backplane" necessarily doesn't have a CPU on it.
I personally don't make any such distinctions - in particular, S-100
backplanes are traditionally referred to as motherboards.

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



