Of course Galaxy can be brought over to something other than VMS.
However there are significant technical barriers to doing so that
have to do with infrastructure needed in the OS first.

As for the proofs, there are parts of Galaxy still (justly!) under
nondisclosure. If you wanted to see it, you should have been at
the DECUS symposium in Cincinnati and seen the ND demos on real iron
running real software. 

If you didn't make it, there's a fall DECUS symposium scheduled!
Or wait till it shows up in the market.

As for a system becoming unpopular and then popular, the popularity
of Unix has arguably waxed and waned since 1974 or so once or twice.
The popularity of an OS in my recollection depends in part on what
it can do. VMS waned and Unix waxed when VAX was way underpowered
with respect to the market and Alpha didn't exist yet. The presence
of an OS that gives performance/price a factor ~10 better than
competing OSs tends to push that OS into prominence.

It isn't the only factor; "stealth marketing" doesn't help, for example,
but is an important factor. People don't switch OSs easily for less due
to the hassles...
 



