: At least on my Multia, the processor is not socketed -- it's soldered on to
: the motherboard.  Also, you should be aware that the Multia doesn't use a
: 21064 microprocessor like the "real" DEC workstations of that vintage; it uses
: a 21066, which is a cut-down version of the 21064 with a slower bus. 

The 21066A (which is what the 233Mhz chip is) is in a ZIFF socket so it
can be replaced/removed. The 21066 (166Mhz) is soldered in place. My
21066A runs at about the equivalent of a Pentium 90 for integer 
performance and maybe Pentium 120 for floating point. But it's very
dependent on the application.

