The 200 is LAT only.  It was developed before TCP/IP came into common use.
I believe TCP/IP was not ported to it because of its limited memory.

The 200 was followed by the DECserver 300 which did not support modems but
was the first DECserver to support TCP/IP.

Both the DECserver's 200 and 300 were replaced by the DECserver 700  in
1992.  The 700 supports LAT, TCP/IP, and with software improvements over
the years, additional protocols such as Appletalk.  The 700 will also
support 115.2Kbaud just fine, thank you.  In fact, it was one of the first
products on the market to do so.  115.2K capable equipment was so difficult
to find in the early 90's that we had to modify PC hardware to get
equipment to test with.

The DECserver 900 series uses the same basic architecture as the 700's with
the advantage of supporting more ports and can be used in the DEChub 900. 
The latest DECserver 900 is the 900MC which has intergral Microcom V.34
(33.6) modems.  The 200's and 300 are no longer sold.  Both 700's  and
900's are.




