Digital used to be a leader in electronic messaging. With its Message
Router and many gateways to various extrenal e-mail systems, including
X.400, EDI etc, as well as internal gateways to VMSmail, ALL-IN-1 and a
published set of routines to write your own, they had a good solution.

Then came Alpha and a totally mismanaged migration that has moved
Digital into oblivion in terms of e-mail: They did not port message
router. Instead, they came out with a different product called Mailbus.
It took YEARS for them to come up with a migration programme which still
costs customers $$$$. They have yet to port all the gateways they had of
MR to Mailbus.

Worse yet: The one good product they had, ALL-IN-1 suffered all sorts of
image problems because of a competing product called ALL-IN-1-mail
(later renamed mailworks). But even worse, ALL-IN-1, born in the early
80s, was able to transform itslf through the times and has migrated to
client-server, support clients on PCs etc, as well as supporting
character cell terminals. Compare that to the short life of Ms-MAIL and
the troubles MS_MAIL shops will have migrating to exchange and you'll
see that stability has its advantages, especially when DEC had managed
to move to client-server while preserving the old stuff.

But WORSE yet: Digital had ANNOUNCED plans on porting the ALL-IN-1
server portion to other platforms such as UNIX and NT. A couple of weeks
later, Digital announces new depths in the "kiss Bill Gates" game and
full support for MS Exchange, with Digital adopting Exchange INTERNALLY
instead of its own products.

Guess what: the ALL-IN-1 group were then told to scrap plans for the
port to NT for the ALL-IN-1 server (Office server). Haven't heard from
these folks since.

Digital pulled out of a lucrative market of which it was a leader. Why ?
Just to give the spot to Microsoft.



