Kirk G. Saban wrote:
> 
>         I am trying to learn Alpha assembly language and am having some
> difficulties.  Does anyone have any suggestions, i.e. how they learned the
> language or any good books (other than Sikes and Witek's Alpha AXP
> Architecture book).  Specifically, I am looking at embedded application
> development for the Alpha.  I would really appreciate hearing from anybody
> with knowledge on alpha assembly!
> 
>         Thanks!
Take a look at:
	
http://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/info/semiconductor/literature/dsc-library.html#alpha


The 'Alpha Achitecture Handbook' pretty much give a complete description
of the Alpha assembly language.

There is also a most usefull book named:

	Alpha Architecture Reference Manual
	ed. Richard L. Sites
	Digital Press, Order: EY-L520E-DP
	DP ISBN: 1-55558-098-X
	LP ISBN: 0-13-033663-7

If you have VC++ 4.0A or higher, there's a lot of good AXP assembly
language 
reading in the help context.

You can also get the 'Alpha SDK' CD-ROM, which contain's lot's of
assem. source-code and debugging tools.

To my experience, the C-compilers comming from Digital(including VC++),
does a really good job, in generating effective code. I learned a lot
about writing good assembly, just by looking at disassemblied C-code.
Doing disassemblies of C-code, also helped me to understand how I would
get the best result's, writing code in C-language.
If you're not very carefull in doing optimization of you assem-code(e.g
following the Intruction Issue scheme), the C-compiler will in most 
cases generate a more effective code. 
If you're not familiar with RISC programming, it sometimes can be
difficult to
understand, that insertment's of NOP instructions will generate faster
code.

Anyhow, after a little experience, you'll find that Alpha assem
programming,
is pretty straight forward.

Happy programming

	Anders Blicher Petersen



