> everyone wants a 64bit OS now! Not everyone runs an Oracle Data Warehouse.
> 
> Its a check off idem. My OS is 64bit yours is 32bit.

I dont believe its a check item at all. Look at the mess 16->32 bit
MSDOS->Win32 got us into. It took us (maybe not you) 10+ years
to clean up all our Unix and DROSS code to make it
run in 32-bit mode. (We all know that porting s/w is
relatively straightforward, but porting a huge working app
where everyone has forgotten how it works and providing migration is
a _big_ headache).

Having suffered from late-80's to early 90's we are in exactly
the same boat again. If we can get to 64-bit without anyone
noticing then this will be a Good Thing (TM).

You and I may not care today - but we will care tomorrow when
some competitor has a 64-bit application and we are still
running in compatibility mode 32-bit. 

No-one in their right mind goes into a PC store and buys
a 16-bit application; the application may be fine, but it tends
to look and feel dated. 

The same thing will happen to people who do not migrate. 
Thats not to say that a 64-bit app is fundamentally any different
to a 32-bit one. It will be very difficult to tell the difference.
But one day you'll type:

	$ crisp big-file
or	$ emacs big-file
or	$ vi big-file
	(where big-file is a 40GB file)

and you will expect it to work.

The sooner we get over the hump (this hump is not nearly as
bad as the 16->32 one, since in 16-bit days we generally had
more than 64k code, but its unlikely we will ever have more
than 4GB of code, just more than 4GB of data).

