Nothing except Microsoft is driven by it's Marketing dept., and
they to want NT to dominate in all areas.  So they have features
going into NT to make it the best NOS over Novell, and then it
has to be the best 3D OpenGL graphics OS over SGI, and also the best 
Web server over Netscape/UNIX, and the best RDBMS server over Oracle/UNIX,
and  the best Mail server and group ware over Lotus , etc. etc.

This leads to alot of things to be added to NT which all need to be
tested, and when something is slow, it goes into the kernel for speed.

NT 3.5 was far more stable than 3.1.  NT 3.51 was more stable than
3.5 but with NT 4.0, alot of features were added to the kernel for 
speed making less reliable than 3.51.  When PC magazines tell you that 
NT 4.0 is not as stable as 3.51, you know there's a problem.

Now add into this that Microsoft after saying that NC's are useless,
now are coming out with a multi-user,  display independent version
