In article <5nq4jb$i4n$1@madrid.visi.net>, downlink@visi.net (Jason Witty) writes:
:>I *know* this sounds ignorent, but I am mostly used to working with sun 
:>systems and PC's, so *any* help is greatly appreciated.

   The console has HELP available at the >>> prompt.

:Now for a better question:  Once I got VMS started up, (keep in mind I'm 
:used to UNIX, not VMS) it did several checks, had a few minor errors, but 
:seemed to b doing okay.  I got :
:
:VAX/VMS Version V5.1-B Major Version id=1 Minor Version id=0

   This is an antique release -- current is V7.1.

..

:%%%%%%%-OP-COMM 12-JUN-1997 08:22:14 %%%%%%%%%
:Message from user DECNET on FTSRV1
:DECnet event 4.10, circuit up
:From node 1.60(FTSRV1) time and date again
:circuit SVA-0
:
:%%%%%%OP-COMMM 12-JUN-1997 time%%%%%%%%%
:Message from user DECNET on FTSRV1
:DECnet event .7, aborted server request
:From node 1.60 (FTSRV1)
:Circuit SVA-0, Line open error
:
:and it repeats over and over again.  I'm guessing that this system was 
:once in a cluster with other Vaxen and is looking for one of them maybe?

   Those operator communications messages are from DECnet -- the DECnet
   and the VMScluster protocols are quite seperate.  The above would
   tend to indicate you don't have a terminated Ethernet.

   To shut off DECnet entirely:

   $ MCR NCP
   NCP> SET EXEC STATE OFF	! "live system"
   NCP> DEF EXEC STATE OFF	! "permanently"

   If using the above, one should also remove the network startup
   procedure -- @SYS$STARTUP:STARTNET or @SYS$MANAGER:STARTNET,
   depending on who entered the command -- from the site-specific
   system startup command SYS$STARTUP:SYSTARTUP_V5.COM procedure.

   To shut off those messages, but leave DECnet running, use:

   NCP> SET CIRC SVA-0 STATE OFF	! "live system"
   NCP> DEF CIRC SVA-0 STATE OFF	! "permanently"
   NCP> SET LINE SVA-0 STATE OFF	! "live system"
   NCP> DEF LINE SVA-0 STATE OFF	! "permanently"

   See a previous discussion over in comp.os.vms -- from the last
   couple of days -- when you decide you want to change your SCS
   and DECnet nodename, and node address from FTSRV1 and 1.60.

   ps: OpenVMS questions are generally better asked over in the
   comp.os.vms newsgroup -- comp.sys.dec tends to have a rather
   wider mixture of DIGITAL operating systems and platforms...

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
