The Alphastation 200 4/100 replaced the @$10,000. DEC 3000/300LX
at a price of $5,000 to $6,000, with a 20% boost in performance.
It was touted as having almost twice the performance of the Sun SS5/70
and SGI Indy-PC at a comparable or lower price - the best price/
performance for Unix in the industry.  So, this was why it was
put on the market.  One would guess it was to move the Alphastations
to PCI from the turbochannel, as well.  This sounds more like an
overall strategy than a spur of the moment decision to find a use
for some under-achieving chips.

Both the 200 4/100 and 200 4/233 are powered by a 21064 CPU, but
the 4/233 is listed as having 16k each of I and D on-chip cache.
As far as the 4/100 goes, no on-chip cache is mentioned.  Both
Alphastations have 512k "on-board" cache.  So, the chips appear to
be different models, but, who knows?

Bill



