young_r@eisner.decus.org (Rob Young) writes:


>	UNIX is gonna get squeezed big time within a year.  With Galaxies,
>	Digital is sending VMS to places UNIX can only drool over.  By this
>	time next year, a VMS cluster will be doing over 1,000,000 tpmC.  That
>	is correct, one million.  While not stated, it is generally 
>	recognized the following 300,000 tps refers to a single system:

The type of tps quoted here are probably of the same marketoid tps
microsoft quoted "a billion a day on NT".  These are not audited results
and they're nowhere near compatable tpmC queries (which are 10s to
hundreds of times more expensive to compute).  Had Microsoft done
an industry standard benchmark then people might have taken them seriously.

The same goes for the 1,000,000 tpmC (did they really say tpmC?)  Currently,
noone does more than around 30,000.  Scaling that by a factor 30 is no
mean feat; but I don't think anyone will take such claims serious until
audited results are published.

And even then, I'm seriously disinclined to believe that the OS would be
a major role (in that you'd get 30K on Unix and 1M on OpenVMS); you can
rest assured that Unix database people tune their software to make the
hardware run flat-out.

Casper
