In article <33A01199.16F6@smarts.com>, Jerry Leichter <leichter@smarts.com> writes:

> The focus of Sysplexes and VMS clusters has been different, however. 
> For example, Sysplexes had explicit support for database transactions
> before they had general-purpose locking; VMS clusters built database
> transactions on top of a general-purpose lock manager.

The design of that lock manager, however, had heavy input from the DEC
database groups who had "rolled-their-own" in the pre-cluster days of
VMS V3.

So it would appear that the DEC decision was to avoid specialized
support and have primitives which were also useful to the record
management system, file system, etc.

Larry Kilgallen



