>  a) the specs for these things,
>  b) how to "boot" them, and
>  c) How the devil to I network them to a machine running linux?
>
> No matter how trivial your knowledge...I'd gratefully accept anything
> anyones knows as Im completely in the dark with these things! 
>
  The readership of this newsgroup is not into spamming.

  The 200/MC is a terminal server to which you may connect 8 terminals
  via RS-232C interface, possibly with modems (the MC means Modem Control -
  the ports support the needed pins for modem connection), and can speak
  the LAT protocol.

  I suppose you want to boot them from LINUX, and I dont know if that's
  possible. The 200/MC doesnt use the TCP/IP boot protocols (BOOTP/DHCP,
  TFTP, etc), but rather another "secret" DEC protocol. If I remember
  correctly, those terminal servers came with the software in ROM, and
  you had to boot only if you wanted to download to it a newer software
  version, and there was some way to set it up to load software from ROM
  or by booting it from the net (sorry - cant recall how).

  The LAT protocol was sold/handed over to merian technologies, which
  developed LAT support for various platforms (MS OSs, if memory serves),
  so you have three options :

   1. Check if meridian software (or maybe somebody else) developed the
      needed software to support LAT over Linux.

   2. Use a VMS (or Digital UNIX - it support LAT as well) as a 'gate'
      between the 200/MCs and the Linux boxes.

   3. Buy several newer terminal servers that support TCP/IP.
      Terminal servers are not expensive this days, though 14 of them
      might cost a bundle.

