Yes having recently installed Solaris 1.5.1 on an Ultrasparc, I can
confirm that it was easy, but I was extremely annoyed about two
things:

    (a) the interface merely listed the different options
    available without giving any opportunity to find out what
    difference they made apart from estimates of disk space
    required, which is a pretty abysmal basis for taking such
    a decision: you then don't have any idea what you are leaving
    out if you choose the light install, for instance.

    (b) it leaves no easily accessible record of what it has
    installed, and where it has installed it. I would have thought
    that any mildly intelligent designer would realise that the user
    would want to be able to survey what had been done, e.g. so as
    to be able to decide what to remove if space runs short, or
    having some idea of which facilities are worth examining to
    see what they do.

Of course, users can always run find, but it shouldn't be necessary.
If I were the employers of whoever designed that installation system
I would have very strong words with him or her.

(It was my first solaris installation. I had previously installed
SunOS on an IPC a few years earlier. I am a regular Unix user (Sun
and DEC Alpha, and previously HP machines, but not normally a system
manager.)

