In article <Taylor-1006972059180001@165.166.141.11>, Taylor@FMCI.Net (Bill Taylor) writes:
:I was recently given a MiniVAX II. It has VMS 5.5 software (about 1993).

   It's "MicroVAX II". 

:It has 16 terminal ports, 3 disk drives , a tk70 tape drive and a 9track
:controller card and 9megs of RAM. The questions is, where can I get an
:either net card to put it on my network? 

   Contact 1-800-DIGITAL, or a reseller, or a used equipment vendor.
   You will want a DELQA controller.

   You will also want to upgrade to 16 MB memory -- that's two 8 MB
   boards.  (You'll have to replace your existing MS630-series memory,
   if you don't have an 8 MB board.)  MicroVAX II memory configuration:
   1 MB on CPU, and up to two MS630-xx memory boards of 1, 2, 4, or 8 MB
   each.  System maximum of 16 MB -- 8 MB + 8 MB + 1 MB.  (Yes, 16 does
   equal 8 + 8 + 1, in this case.)

:Are the terminal cards worth anything?

   That's certainly a deeply philosophical question.

:and are there any outdated word processor programs out there.

   There are many outdated word processors.  

:This is completely useless to me but it does give me a change to leard a
:little about VAX/VMS. I am looking at purchasing a Alpha server for my
:internet business ( I am all 200mhz PPC now) and thought it might be worth
:a little of my time. Any assistance appreciated.

   I'd go direct to the AlphaServer or AlphaStation.  (The MicroVAX II
   system might be entertaining, but it's a ten year old design, it's
   very slow by current processor standards, and it predates integrated
   SCSI disk support.  This system was `given away' for a reason...)

