> qualify for chapter 11 in your book?
> 
> > It's obvious that Microsoft is not happy with how far Digital
> > got NT's foot in the Enterprise door, so now we have the
> > new HP/MS alliance.
> 
> You're just repeating 'parrot fashon' words that you're read in some
> over-hyped sensationalised Comp Mag report.  And frankly, you don't
> know what you're talking about.  Don't believe everything you read
> in the popular press.  Take it with the pinch of salt it deserves.
> 
> For instance, did you know that DIGIAL has now won over 1 Million Exchange
> seats making it the leading global provider of Exchange implementations.
> And 60% of that represents new business to DIGITAL.  Now what were you
> saying about Enterprise doors?
> 

DEC have not taken 1 million exchange seats, they may have provided 
servers for 1 million exchange users though knowing exchange this does 
not seem something to brag about.

But more seriously what DEC have done is substituted a high profit 
high margin sale based on All-In-One and OpenVMS for a very low
margin low value sale based on Exchange, NT and Aplha/intel while
trying to compete against Compaq, Dell etc all of who are able to
acheive
much better gross margins using a much more efficient sales channel.

Not something to crow about and if I was a Digital Stockholder which 
thankfully I am not I would be crying.

The same is happening in the DEC-UNIX market differential pricing
between the same system running UNIX and NT to ensure that customers
do not buy UNIX resulting in sales of very low margin NT/Alpha
boxes. Don't disagree with this its all in Digitals current financial
reports.

As for the HP/Microsoft Alliance if I was Digital I would be worried
HP are much more fashionable with commercial customers than Digital
the Analysts like them they hate Digital and HP are a much bigger
company.

Digital should remember that Microsoft is essentially opportunistic
they don't have long term relationships with anybody. (NT on Alpha
only exists because Microsoft thought Intel were not going to come up
with
the goods e.g Pentium Pro). Microsoft probably cannot drop Alpha 
though the volumes certainly don't warrant a port because of the
adverse marketing but this does not mean that they have to rely on
Digital for integration Support etc and they have shown this
by getting into bed with HP.

Regards

