In article <339CB593.2369@us.ibm.com>, Greg Pfister <pfister@us.ibm.com> writes:

> Workload management is *extremely* sophisticated in a Sysplex, and far
> from the "attach job to system" paradigm you imply.  Maybe VMScluster
> had that for a long time, but not Sysplex.

As I see it, the VMS developers got to their limit on workload management
policy tweaks and put the emphasis these days on controls to allow site-
specific code to take it further (unless I have been reading the wrong
books).

VMS does _not_ migrate jobs between different nodes in the cluster,
although of course it _does_ migrate jobs between various CPUs on a
node.  This all gets fuzzy though, since the addition of kernel
threads means that a single job (process) can be running on several
CPUs at the same time within one node.

> Global queues? Single system administration?  Let's compromise and say
> they're both full-featured clusters.  Otherwise we end up at "IS!" "IS
> NOT!" too quickly.

Yes, and I end up with egg on my face, as I just recommended comp.arch
today to someone as an example of dignified technical discussion.

