Alan L. Stange wrote:
> 
> Bryan Althaus wrote:
> 
> > Linux is in now, and every ex-DOS programmer or freshman college student
> > thinks he is a UNIX guru after installing and admining his machine.  I'm
> > just sick of it.  Linux is not that impressive, sorry it's not.  It offers
> > nothing new that other OS's don't already have or have had for years or
> > decades.
> 
> I have to agree with this completely.
> 
> I have yet to hear one good argument for why I should switch from
> whatever OS I currently use to linux.  Note that price isn't one of
> them.
> 

Surely all of this could be said in regards to any operating system
(sans the fact that Linux is free). 

Perhaps the onus should be placed upon you to explain what exactly Linux
lacks in comparison to other operating systems. 

Part of the charm of Linux no doubt lies in its non-orthodox upbringing
and development.  Personally, I find Linux appealing simply because of
its unique spirit and identity as an OS that seeks to offer an
alternative to operating systems produced within and by the corporate
world.  Linux is a bit unique I think, its progress and growth shaped by
programmers and computer enthusiasts who wish to share their human
creativity, not for profit maximization (this is not a jab at
programmers who work for corporate entities -- programmers whose work is
also quite impressive), but rather to excite and enliven other computer
enthusiasts like themselves throughout society.  The fact that Linux can
offer everything that the corporate operating systems offer -- and offer
it well -- is simply icing on the cake, and a tribute to the creativity
and talent of the Linux community.

Other operating systems, like FreeBSD, share much of the aforementioned
spirit of Linux, and I think this is wonderful.  Personally, I prefer
Linux -- others will prefer FreeBSD, etc.  We as humans like different
things; I don't think it's that surprising that some will love Linux and
other will dislike it with a similar passion.   

And yes, Linux is not perfect.  But, then again, what OS is?



