xdevnull@ais.net (/dev/null) writes:
> Hello...I have a CPU board in one of my PDP-11 Micros, DEC number
> M7554-02, the 11/53+ cpu board.  It is connected to the console board
> which, in the case of my 11/23s (M8189E) would have a bunch of DB25's
> for the console and whatnot.  However, this one has a bunch of wierd
> connectors, looking somewhat like an RJ-11 but with the locking tab
> offset.  My research has found that these are called either DEC422 or
> Deconnect (or both?)
> 
> My question:  what can I do with it?  Is there a {wierd connector} -->
> DB25 cable so I can plug in a serial terminal?  Are there plans for
> making one so I don't have to pay $50 for it?  I found the connector
> for sale in the Jensen tool catalog, if that is indeed it.
> 
> I notice that my new Vaxstation3100 also has two of these for its
> printer and modem ports.

  It's called an MMJ (Modified Modular Jack) connector...aka
DECconnect.  It's a 6-pin RJ12 connector that's been modified by
moving the clip off to one side.

  Connectors and crimp tools are available commercially.  Usually
anyone that sells regular RJ crimpers and connectors also sells MMJ
supplies.

  I don't have the pinout handy.  If you can't find it, send me some
email and I'll dig it up for you.

  Adapters are around.  DEC made them, as well as a few other
companies, I think.  I finally got tired of digging for them whenever
I needed 'em, so I went out and bought a crimper and a bunch of
connectors (cost me about $60) and made a bunch of MMJ-RJ12 cables.
Then I use regular MODTAP-style DB25-RJ12 hoods on the RJ12 end.  This
is handy and saves headaches.


                                -Dave McGuire
                                 mcguire@neurotica.com



