Curt Sampson <cjs@cynic.portal.ca> wrote:
>In article <5nn6oh$4rq@panix.com>, Bryan Althaus <bryan@panix.com> wrote:
>
>>It is impressive that it was written from scratch.  But FreeBSD is there
>>also.
>
>Actually, FreeBSD was far from written `from scratch'; a large
>portion of its code base was written at UCB over the previous
>fifteen years or so. And it shows. The FreeBSD network stack is
>one of the best in the industry. The Linux network stack is a
>complete disaster.

   Maybe.

   I've seen worse, and the people I've got working on hacking the stack
   to build AV firewalls with have said, even though they're coming from
   a BSD kernel hacking background, that it's a perfectly reasonable
   stack.

   It certainly runs fast enough to do the job.

   I'd certainly agree that the 99pl<whatever> stack was a complete
   disaster, and I've never looked back from 1.0.x since I upgraded.
   1.2.x has a whole bunch slower stack than BSD, but 2.0.x seems, at
   least for the hardware I run it on, to be about as fast as a
   corresponding FreeBSD router box.

   It's a nice race between the two; what Linux does well encourages
   the FreeBSD core team to speed up their code, and what FreeBSD
   does well encourages the Linux core developers to speed up their
   code.  And as Linux grows in popularity, it will drag along the
   various BSDs, because there are people who want a free Unix but
   aren't willing to put up with some of the bizarre distributions
   that Linux has.

   Everybody wins.


>The frightening thing is that some Universities appear to be teaching
>Operating Systems courses based on Linux. Someone even went so far
>as to publish an OS textbook that encourages the use of Linux as
>an example OS.

   Frightening?   I don't think so;  exposure to another large
   collection of potential coders, even if they are academic
   users (and it's not as if the academic world is a total loss;
   Berkeley produced some pretty nice code, though I can't for
   the life of me think of anything offhand.)   It's a different
   Unix, and you certainly don't lose anything by forcing people
   to look at something new.

