>However, if we move out of the realm of CPU, and into the realm of
>realtime graphics, video, etc, it gets a lot more complicated.  Since
>my OS (Linux) doesn't support hardware acceleration of OpenGL code
>yet, the O2 will beat me on anything it handles with hardware acceleration.
>I expect this to change in the near future as support for OpenGL
>cards and GLX get moved into the X server on Linux.

>My point: don't spend lots of money on an SGI unless the features the
>SGI offers are  *absolutely necessary* for the sort of work you need
>to do.  

Don't spend lots of money on any equipment unless the features are
*absolutely necessary* for the sort of work you need to do.  Or how about:
"buy what you need right now".  Good busines dictates that you don't invest
more than what you expect the return to be.

Let me know when I can a PC with OpenGL 1280x1024x24bit @ 75HZ double
buffered and 1280x480x24bit @ 120HZ quad-buffered stereo visuals (or
better).  Hardware support is nice, but it doesn't exist unless the drivers
work and the software supports it...

Any comparison of computers demands that the intended uses be considered and
that the machines involved support the desired features now.  SGI charges
$18K for an Octane because they can.  We pay $18K for an Octane because it
is better than not having an Octane.  A $3K Pentium Pro is better than a
Cray-1 for just about anything, but 20 years ago $30 million would have been
money well spent for many tasks.

