In article <339EC070.390D@brussels.lhs.be>,
	Bart D'hoore <dhoore@brussels.lhs.be> writes:
>
>Why are so many people screeming for a 64-bit OS? I am wondering how
>many users actually need this. Most of them need speed, reliability,
>etc. I am questioning whether 64bit is the magic answer. Of course, some
>need the address space, but surely not all.

I would agree here. My desktop is a (now rather old) Alpha running DU
(before it was called DU).  The 64bit-ness is merely a pain as far as
I'm concerned.

Initially, none of the public domain software would run on it (if it
compiled and didn't crash on startup, it went like a snail with
alignment errors allover). Of course, over the last three years, this
has all(?) been fixed. (It would have been _really_ nice if DEC had
arranged that 64 bit pointers with the top half either all ones or
all zeros were always invalid, to trap code which saved a pointer in
an int at some stage. Unfortunately, valid pointers appear in this
range.)

Next problem is that with 64 bit pointers and alignment padding, the
memory in the machine only goes about half as far as it would on a
32 bit system.

64bit address space and the increasing appearance of 'bloatware' will
doubtless feed each other in the future too.

64bit-ness certainly has its uses, but using it where not appropriate
has penalties, and in many if not most cases, it isn't appropriate
(yet). To be able to select which aspects are appropriate for a given
application would be nice (e.g., 32 bit address space but 64 bit file
offsets is a combination I could use in a few places where mmaping
whole/any of the file is not very appropriate).

-- 
Andrew Gabriel                              Andrew@cucumber.demon.co.uk
Consultant Software Engineer




