Steve Strange wrote:
> How many
> operating systems does IBM support?  Lots.  Are they pro-AIX?
> pro-MVS?  Which is it?  Can't be both, right? 

In IBM's case, there has been a historic and fairly clear delimitation
of the role of each operating system. MVS for mainframes, AIX for
scientific minis, AS400 for business minis, OS-2 for PC-controllers-ATMs
and DOS for games. Their missions are separate. So it is easy to aim
each systenm to different markets.

Digital's OP systems don't have clearly different missions. All claim to
have clustering with NT giving (false?) impressions that its clustering
has to potential to rival that of VMS eventually. Digital tried to
separate the markets with a pricing policy (VMS=$$$$$$, UNIX=$$$
NT=free).

Digital also tried to prevent the ALPHA from competing with its own PC
division by making sure that the ALPHA always had a clearly higher price
than DEC's PCs. It is easy to see why Alpha has failed to become an
"commodity" system since it was purposefully priced out of that market.


Now, if DEC's ALPHA hardware were truly competitive with the INtel
commodity boxes, other companies might be interested in porting their
operating system to ALPHA.

If DEC lowers its Alpha prices to a point where it does become a threath
to INtel boxes, it  will have to stick to its policy for a while before
people will really beleive it and start to find Alpha attractive enough
to port other op systems to it.



