Thomas Edelmayer wrote:
> No problem using fixed frequency monitors, even sync-on-green monitors
> like the VRT19-DA, with Pee C's.  You just need the right graphics
> card.  Let your fingers do the walking on the net (ala AltaVista) and
> you'll find a few 64-bit graphics cards for under $300 that allow you to
> configure the Windoze driver to satisfy your monitor's specs.  Check out
> http://www.ioa.com/users/mobius/monitor.html for a really detailed look
> at using fixed scan monitors with Pee C's.  Mobius isn't the only card.
> Like I said, let your fingers do the walking on the net.

I'd like to add that you don't really need to have a special graphics
card to be able to drive fixed frequency monitors.  A special graphics
card may make the job easier, but it is only really a necessity if you
want to use various DOS video modes or if you really want to see the
bootup.  Linux gives you complete control with XFree86 and SVGATextMode,
and many cards also allow you to configure the Windoze drivers.  I'm now
running a VR299 with a Mach64 card and it works great.  In my case a new
$300 card for a $16 monitor would be silly.
(I am interested in a QDSS boardset and hooking this up to my MicroVAX
II, but that's another story.)

> Now, perhaps you can help me out.  I need specs for the DEC VRT19-DA.

Well, there is a database http://www.Nashville.Net/~griffin/monitor.html
Also, check DejaNews, especially the old database, because these threads
seem to come up often.  That's where I got the specs on the VR299.
Oh yes, do compare specs from multiple sources.  They can be wrong.

