I think in the next 5-10 years you will see more and more people
need it. My living is made writing applications. My estimated/predicted
RAM size usage is increasing linearly and in a few years 4GB will not be
unreasonable.

For example, take editing files. 10 years ago on my huge 70MB
drive, creating a 2MB log file and trying to edit it was quite a task
for a 25MHz 386 with 4MB of RAM.

Today with 128MB RAM, I think nothing of creating 50-100MB log files
if need be and maybe 1GB if I really have to. Today, I dont need to
scrimp on the options that are being dumped to the file - I just
dump everything and let the machine worry about it.

In a few years when 8GB disks are the _minimum_ size you can buy
(IBM announced EIDE 8GB avail Jul?). In a couple of years at
todays rates, we will be seeing 20-30GB disks, and as RAM
prices come down, my 'low-end' 128MB will be more like
2GB of RAM. (Put it this way: 128MB cost me about 500 GBP/700 USD).
In 5 years time I will still spend about 500 GBP on RAM but I will
probably get 1-2GB of RAM for that.

At that juncture, lets say 2001, 2GB of RAM will be like putting
256k of RAM in the original PC. Not far off from the processor not
being able to make use of it.

As we all shift to 64-bit CPUs early next century, 1 GB of RAM or
1GB of process size will be irrelevant just like 1MB process sizes
today are irrelevant. 

I remember when X11 was released and SVR4 and people were astonished
that you needed _16MB_ of RAM compared to the more usual 4MB.

By the time you have 1GB of RAM in a personal machine, the O/S
better have APM and start up instantly so that everything in
/bin, /lib, etc can be left in RAM as cache.

