I was an early adopter of Alpha (I worked for an ISV at the time)
and can attest to the absolute pain that the original migration
to 64-bits was.  It was only software which had been through a 
similar transformation (16 -> 32 bits) which seemed to handle the
32 -> 64 bit translation well.  However, what saved my hide then,
as now (in those rare cases where apps are still ill-behaved) is the
compiler's -taso option, which does what I think you want:


-taso
 Directs the linker to load the executable file in the lower 31-bit
 addressable virtual address range. The -T and -D flags to the ld com-
 mand can also be used, respectively, to ensure that the text and data
 segments addresses are loaded into low memory.

 The -taso flag, however, in addition to setting default addresses for
 text and data segments, also causes shared libraries linked outside the
 31-bit address space to be appropriately relocated by the loader. If
 you specify -taso and also specify text and data segment addresses with
 -T and -D, those addresses override the -taso default addresses. The
 -taso flag is useful for porting 32-bit programs to Digital UNIX.

--
Michael Brodeur
