>Anyone know of a program earlier than WordStar that used/uses Ctrl-Y to
>delete the current line? The one keystroke that I detested, and that I
>changed (to ^Qg) as soon as I learned how (this was in the days before
>Undo), and it seems to be the most copied single keystroke of all.

  The WordStar program I used on my CP/M systems way-back-when allowed
me to replace the Ctl-Y command with Ctl-Y Ctl-Y (ie, you had to hit
it twice to erase a line).  As far as I know, WordStar originated the
use of the "wordstar diamond" and the use of Ctl-Y as erase-a-line.
  Doubling the Ctl-Y to delete was a good safeguard, and didn't slow
me down much, once I replaced my terminal with a CIT101 with its fast
key repeat rate and low repeat delay.  Holding down the key for half 
a second would (BRRRRRUP!) delete half the screen.

