> > jasza@aol.com
> 
> EGA and VGA do not mix. Using an adapter will blow either the monitoror
> the controller. You need to get VGA monitors.

> regards,
> glne kelley
> digital

Oh, dear.

No, you *DO NOT* need (or want) to get VGA monitors for _DECstations_.
DECstation video is not compatible with either EGA or VGA.  Not unless
you get new or high-end multisync VGA monitors that do sync-on-green.
(Ignoring the buitin PC-compatible video on Personal Decstations,
which are a special case.)

DECstation monitors are fixed-frequency, sync-on-green, either
1024x864 (DECstation 2100, 3100, PMAG-BA TurboChannel board), or
1280x1024 (PMAGB-BA, and PMAG-C/PMAG-D/PMAG-F boards) at 60 or 66Hz.

The fixed-frequency monitors that originally came with most
DECstations are pretty nice monitors (Trinitron tubes). They're not
especially useful as PC monitors because they're wired for fixed video
rates that don't match standard VGA rates, and for sync-on-green.  You
should be able to pick one up nearly free (especially the older
Phillips VR-290 monitors, which aren't as crisp as the Trinitrons, and
fade even more over time).

If the machines donated were DECstation 5000/20s, /25s, or /33s, then
the builting 1024x768 video on the motherboard __might__ work with a
VGA monitor.  (The monitors on those machines frequently disappear
onto PCs, so the converse probably works, as long as the sync is
compatible).

Exactly what model of DECstations are these?  (the model number is on
the front left of the box above the green power light.)

If the machines aren't 2100s or 3100s, what kind of PMAG-{A,B,C,D}
letter is on the slot on the back of the box that the monitor attaches
to?  Or if they're Personal Decstations, do they have video option
slots?  (Are the two horizontal option slots on the rear of the case
covered from the inside by blank beige fillers, or do they have
connectors that might be video?)



