Jake Hamby <jehamby@lightside.com> writes:
>[...] I remember talking to a member of the FreeBSD project
>[...] he also said that during the process of
>investigating a FreeBSD port to Alpha, they discovered that GCC
>generates truly horrendous code for Alpha (something on the order of
>*half* the speed of DEC's compiler).  He was truly surprised that no
>Linux users had ever noticed this in all of the time that Linux/Alpha
>has been available!

Show me.

Talk is cheap and spreading bogus memes is even cheaper.  I compare
code compiled with DEC cc and with GNU gcc all the time.  From tiny
inner loops in registers, to crunching hundreds of megabytes of floating
point numbers, from a hundred cycles to a hundred thousand billion.

GCC does most emphatically *not* produce "truly horrendous" code for
Alpha.  There are codes types for which it is typically 20 or 30%
slower and other types for which it is 20 or 30% faster.  For little
inner loops the differences can be greater.

Allow me to grep for "cycles" in a polynomial multiplication program I
was just playing with...  I wrote a bunch of different functions to do
the same time-critical things in different ways and timed them all
("cc -O5 -tune ev5 -migrate" versus "gcc -O4 -funroll-loops", pure
ANSI C not using the gcc extensions):

/* Average for 64-bit nums: 258 cycles (with cc), 316 (with gcc) */
/* Average for 64-bit nums: 514 cycles (with cc), 270 (with gcc) */
/* Average for 64-bit nums: 362 cycles (with cc), 367 (with gcc) */
/* Average for 64-bit nums: 502 cycles (with cc), 413 (with gcc) */
/* Average for 64-bit nums: 266 cycles (cc), 198 (gcc). */
/* Average for 64-bit nums: 177 cycles (cc), 233 (gcc). */
/* Average for 64-bit nums: 696 cycles! (cc), 194 (gcc). */
/* Average for 64-bit nums: 303 cycles (cc), 219 (gcc). */

This is just one example, but it is reality as opposed to your passing
on of rumours heard from scare-mongerers.

-- Rob.
