Andrew Harrison <andrew.harrison@uk.sun.com> writes:

>Porting solaris to DEC Alpha boxes is highly unlikely for a number
>of reasons.
>
>[reasons 1-5 deleted...]
>
>6.	DEC would probably not fork out the cash. I can't see
>	Palmer coming cap in hand to Sun.

For better or worse, the fact is that Digital isn't interested
in Solaris, and neither (also for better or worse) is an
increasing segment of the computing world.

Digital is betting on NT.

In terms of capabilities, NT is not yet competition for DU or
Solaris, but it almost certainly will be in the next couple
years. MS has already managed to make the much longer trip from
DOS to NT.

Gates has announced MS will invest $1 billion in NT over the
next year. Yeah, yeah, $900,000,000 of that will be spent on
marketing, but $100,000,000 is still a lot of change.

Beyond that, there's the push from the hardware end--the
marketing-engendered perception that "propreitary RISC"
boxes are overpriced for the performance they offer.

Take a look at

	http://www.compaq.com/newsroom/pr/pr090697a.html

It's Compaq's announcement of their new NT workstations.
Guess who they shoot down with their Pro/E numbers?

From the announcement:

      Using the Pro/E Bench97 benchmarks, the Compaq
      Professional Workstation 6000, with one 300 MHz Pentium
      II processor, 256 MB of memory, a 4 GB hard drive and
      Diamond Fire GL 4000 graphics card delivered
      record-setting performance of 63 minutes. This compares
      to the Sun Ultra 2-2300 configured with two
      top-of-the-line 300 MHz UltraSPARC-II processors, 256 MB
      of memory, a 2 GB hard drive and Creator 3D graphics
      which scored 77 minutes(1). The average street price of
      the Compaq Professional Workstation 6000 configured with
      a 17-inch monitor is $13,018(2), while the Sun Ultra
      2-2300 configured with a 17-inch monitor is $38,295.

On the other hand, the 433a Digital PW2 gets a Pro/E number
of 61 and *is* priced competitively with the Compaq. And
it runs MS NT (which by the crazy logic of the marketplace
is not proprietary, whereas Solaris and DU are. Yeah, go
figure)

And there's little comfort in the server end. Today's
NY Time's business section (and my bet is the Wall St. Journal
as well) carries a full page ad from Compaq for their new
line of servers.  And once again, they harp on "expensive
proprietary RISC systems. Yeah, it's bullshit, but it's
bullshit people are buying.

NT's not there yet and neither is the Intel-based hardware.

But MS, Intel, Compaq and others, companies with a *lot* of
money to invest, want to get there. Digital thinks it can
hitch it's Alpha wagon to NT. It might well be wrong. But
it certainly doesn't seem interested in Solaris.

>Having said all of this you will probably see an announcement 
>for Solaris on Alpha tommorow.

Hmmm. Good hedge. I'll buy it.

	-doug



