In article <5njjjr$j2b$1@gannett.math.niu.edu>, Xcott Craver wrote:
>
>	Of course, if a university buys O2s for the sole purpose of
>web browsing, then they are surely making a terrible mistake.  Can
>you name a university that has done this?  I have a feeling that 
>this is a red herring.  The truth of the matter is that university
>departments can put SGIs to work doing the kinds of thinks that a 
>$3000 Wintel box [pause to chuckle at the ridiculous nature of this
>entire thread] just can't do. 
>

I'd be happy to run my Dual P6-200 w/ 128 MB ram (which set me back
$4500, although I've been told there are cheaper machines out there)
against an O2 with an R5000 for FP and integer performance (an
O2 w/ 1 R5000, 64MB RAM is ~ $4500 with discount, *at best*).  I'm
confident that even with just one processor, the machines would run
neck-and-neck (that's what my benchmarks say).   That's even with
the O2 using IRIX cc -- which produces faster code than gcc.  An O2
with an R10k would probably be significantly faster on FP than
my PPro.

However, if we move out of the realm of CPU, and into the realm of
realtime graphics, video, etc, it gets a lot more complicated.  Since
my OS (Linux) doesn't support hardware acceleration of OpenGL code
yet, the O2 will beat me on anything it handles with hardware acceleration.
I expect this to change in the near future as support for OpenGL
cards and GLX get moved into the X server on Linux.

My point: don't spend lots of money on an SGI unless the features the
SGI offers are  *absolutely necessary* for the sort of work you need
to do.  

