I propose an explanation: Is it possible that Gerstner has a better
understanding of IBM strategy and business plan than you do? Given their
track record over the past several years, I would give him the benefit
of any remaining doubt. Try testing this hypothesis: IBM has accepted
that Win is the desktop standard, at least for the present. Recall also
that IBM is not primarily a desktop PC company (nor are they primarily a
semiconductor manufacturing company). Their focus is on mainframes, and
associated software and services. Their software business alone dwarfs
that of Microsoft. I seriously doubt that Gerstner spends 5 min/day
considering the issues that you take so seriously. They just are not as
important to IBM as you would like to believe. 

You can gain some insights into IBM's view of what IS important by 
reading their annual report. A letter from Gerstner provides an
excellent summary of what he sees as critical. See:
http://www.ibm.com/AnnualReport/1996/htmljs/ChairmansLetter/
No mention of OS/2, PPC, or semiconductors. Also look at their web Home
page -several mentions of PCs, references to new software running on
win95 and new Pentium II microprocessors. No OS/2.

Perhaps he is wrong and you are right, but I wouldn't bet my livelihood
on it, though.

