In article <Pine.SOL.3.93.970610101031.13743E-100000@infernal> R!ch <richardt@clem> writes:

>   I don't know what the latest lies^H^H^H^Hfigures are, but DEC are
>   hardly the most pro UNIX company I can think of.  Just about all their
>   advertising in the UK has been pro NT.  Not really the sign of a pro
>   UNIX company, eh?

I guess I don't understand why so many people feel that a company the
size of Digital can't support more than one operating system.  NT is
pushed in advertising because it is seen as the greatest growth
opportunity in terms of both hardware sales and service contracts.
Has anyone looked at the NT growth numbers in the past couple years
and compared them with UNIX?  I'm not saying I'm necessarily happy
about it, but that's not the point.  But Digital has a couple of its
own operating systems too, and it spends a *lot* of money maintaining
and improving them.  Why doesn't anyone have a problem with, say, HP,
who support both NT on their Intel boxes and UNIX on their
workstations/servers?  Are they pro-NT or pro-UNIX???  How many
operating systems does IBM support?  Lots.  Are they pro-AIX?
pro-MVS?  Which is it?  Can't be both, right?  And in both HP's and
IBM's cases, you generally can't run more than one OS on any given
platform, whereas one can buy a Digital Alphaserver and run VMS, UNIX,
Linux, or NT on it.  Take your pick.  Three of them are even full
64-bit OSes.  Run one today, change in a year to another without
immediately replacing all your hardware.  Seems like a big advantage
to me, not a problem.  In any case, the amount invested recently in
Digital UNIX advertising (very little) in no way reflects the amount
invested recently in Digital UNIX engineering (a lot).  Believe it or
not, there are people in Digital that do realize that NT is not the
answer to all problems, and some of them aren't even in the UNIX
engineering group!  So that's my 2 cents.

Obligatory comment on the original subject: If Sun wants to port
Solaris to Alpha, more power to 'em!  Personally I'd much rather run
Digital UNIX, but I wouldn't be upset to see yet another OS added to
what Alpha systems can run (Linux, Digital UNIX, VMS, NT,..)

Standard disclaimers apply.

	Steve
