Sadly Compaq have got their numbers wrong.

Firstly the Ultra2/300 gets a score of 51 on the PRO-E 
bench97 it ties with the 500 Mhz Alpha for first place although
it came first in 8 of the 11 bench97 benchmarks. (bench97 weights
the results of each benchmark)

Compaq should also lay off the single processor Compaq beats
dual CPU Ultra since the bench97 PRO-Engineer setup disables 
the second CPU on the Sun so it isn't doing anything.

PRO-E is threaded on Solaris it is the only threaded PRO-E
port and if you enable the second processor you get virtually
linear scaling for things like shading. 

Since Sun does not to my knowledge sell a Ultra 2 with a 17inch
monitor I would suspect Compaq's pricing comparision and again
if you compare a dual CPU system with a single CPU system
you are bound to be able to engineer a good cost comparison.

By the way what did they mean by street pricing does this
mean after customer discount if so why did they take Sun's
list price and add to it.

> On the other hand, the 433a Digital PW2 gets a Pro/E number
> of 61 and *is* priced competitively with the Compaq. And
> it runs MS NT (which by the crazy logic of the marketplace
> is not proprietary, whereas Solaris and DU are. Yeah, go
> figure)
> 

As I said the DEC is slower than a single CPU Ultra and
if you allow PRO-E to use the second CPU you get the benefit
so where does this put DEC. 

> And there's little comfort in the server end. Today's
> NY Time's business section (and my bet is the Wall St. Journal
> as well) carries a full page ad from Compaq for their new
> line of servers.  And once again, they harp on "expensive
> proprietary RISC systems. Yeah, it's bullshit, but it's
> bullshit people are buying.
> 

If Compaqs server comparison is as shoddy as their PRO-E
examples then I am sure that they will be getting the
results they wanted.

> NT's not there yet and neither is the Intel-based hardware.
> 
> But MS, Intel, Compaq and others, companies with a *lot* of
> money to invest, want to get there. Digital thinks it can
> hitch it's Alpha wagon to NT. It might well be wrong. But
> it certainly doesn't seem interested in Solaris.
> 

Sadly for DEC all they seem to be doing is canibalising high
margin UNIX/OpenVMS Alpha sales for low margin Alpha/NT 
sales. Their total Alpha revenues are falling according to their
financial results but their unit volume is increasing Alpha
NY volume went up 40%. Not a pretty picture for a company
like DEC with the cost of their sales channel.

Regards

Andrew Harrison
Senior Consultant SunUK



