On Sun, 8 Jun 1997, Rob Young wrote:

> In article <3399D946.6B62@us.ibm.com>, Greg Pfister <pfister@us.ibm.com> writes:
> > Glenn C. Everhart wrote:
[chomp]
> 	Galaxies is about running multiple copies of the OS in a single 
> 	machine (for example, 8 copies running with 4 CPUs per copy inside a 
> 	Wildfire).

Well, just what do we mean by `machine'? And what do we gain from
multiple copies of the OS on one `machine'?

I was not at DECUS, know nothing from the NDA, but my expectations,
from knowing VMS is like this...

The limit is becoming the CPUs/Memory/IO-system bus. Most all of
the current SMP systems have all the CPUs, all memory controllers,
and all IO `things' ( where multiple IO buses may live behind the `thing' )
as peers on a common logical backplane. Let us call this unit a
spoke. Join several with a SCI like interface and a chunk of shared
memory ( that may be in fact local to the spoke, or in the `hub' )
and the node now has multiple independant IO paths to memory. Add
parellel data paths to taste/sizeof(wallet) :) Put *part* of the
IO data structures, the lock manager DS, and the RMS buffers in
shared memory and salt with various other bits of OS cruft to taste
and/or level of implementation.

Now we have no OS I stream traffic across the hub, no local IO
D stream traffic, and DMA can cross or not to taste. All the lock
database is shared, so _VERY_ low latency and high bandwidth.
Unless we WANT it to be local only.
Ditto for the shared buffers; fast lock, read, update, unlock...

The current DEC Memory Channel seems a bit wimpy for this, but
a high end SCI implementation should cut the mustard quite well.

Bit like a cross between the old C.mmp and Cm*.

Cheers
~Paul




