James C. Stutts (stuttjc@eng.auburn.edu) wrote:

: IMHO, if they wanted more of the desktop engineering market, they'd offer an O2
: with better CPU performance and less expensive graphics options.  PC graphics
: options work well enough for most applications.  Even mass-market "game" 3D
: boards will soon offer OpenGL compatibility.

The problem with this approach is that you've just converted the O2,
which is (arguably, for particular application domains) a product with
lots of "added value", into a generic compute box.  And once you're
trying to compete in the generic compute box market, all you can do is
shoot for the best price/performance ratio, and duke it out with PC's
that have 50 times the volume and near-zero profit margins.  I think
SGI is doing the right thing by trying to make better integrated
systems with distinctive feature sets, which in their smaller niche
markets, are worth a somewhat higher price.  You can't make much money
selling generic compute boxes.  Just look at DEC: they have been
struggling to keep their heads above water for the past several years,
despite being uncontested kings of the SPECmark hill.

-- Dave Hinds




