> : >
> : > Sun and DEC held this discussion several years ago. Apparently Sun
> : > wanted too much money from DEC (porting costs, etc). DEC decided it
> : > didn't make much sense to subsidize additional competition for Digital
> : > UNIX.
> : >
> :
> : I also think the business case for multi-platform Solaris is weak. E.g.
> : how many customers does Solaris/Intel have,  how many critical
> : applications have been ported to it ?
> :
> : >
> : > Perhaps not. Bob Palmer has delivered his "unite or die" Grand
> : > UNIXification speech at several Unix conferences (UniForum SFO 1994,
> : > Unix Expo NYC 1995).
> : >
> :
> : However, Digital hasn't concluded any major technical alliance on
> : UNIX lately, e.g. it was late to endorse COSE (or be invited to
> : join it,  I don't know the details on this),  and it might have
> : benefited from collaboration with e.g. SCO or Data General.
> :
> : All Digital's strategic efforts seem to go into making NT a success,
> : hoping some of its success results in Alpha sales. In the mean time,
> : I'd be surprised if Alpha/NT sales are still considerably below
> : UNIX and VMS sales.
> 

Porting solaris to DEC Alpha boxes is highly unlikely for a number
of reasons.

1.	DEC are by far in a way the weakest UNIX vendor, they are
	now 5th behind Sun, HP, IBM and SGI and their market
	share is still declining.

2.	Despite the hype Alpha is relatively un-sucessfull unlike
	SPARC, Intel and MIPS it is not a self sustaining architecture
	relying as it does on DEC's highly expensive fabrication 
	process.

3.	What would Sun gain, after all DEC a supremely un-committed
	UNIX, it has never been flavour of the month, DEC price
	their UNIX systems to sell NT boxes.

4.	Alpha is vunerable why would Sun want to do anything that
	may sustain a high margin alpha platform as opposed
	to a 0 margin NT alpha platform.

6.	DEC would probably not fork out the cash. I can't see
	Palmer coming cap in hand to Sun.

Having said all of this you will probably see an announcement 
for Solaris on Alpha tommorow.


Regards
Andrew Harrison
Senior Consultant SunUK



