In article <339783AB.5532@ucsd.edu>,
John R. Stevens <johnstevens@ucsd.edu> wrote:
>kirill@chinook.physics.utoronto.ca wrote:
>
>> The R10000 (this IS the one you are referring to) has a
>> specfp95 of 19.0 and a specint95 of 10.7 at 200Mhz.
>
>10.7?
>19?
>The 195mhz R1000 SPECint95 is 8.88, SPECfp95 is 10.6
>Two(2)195mhz R1000's SPECint95 9.4, SPECfp95 is 17.4

SPEC is a *system* benchmark, not a chip benchmark.
Systems have been built with a variety of performance levels
using the R10000 cpu.

The SGI Origin 2000 with 195 MHz cpus has a reported value of
9.4 for SPECint95, and 19.0 for SPECfp95.  These numbers were
run using only a single cpu of a multi-cpu system.  I think 
that there is an option for a 1-cpu Origin 2000, but I would
be surprised if anyone actually purchased one like that.

There is some confusion because after we reported the results,
the formatting of the SPEC web pages was changed to show the
number of cpus installed in the system.  If you look
at the detailed reports, it is clear that these were *not* 
parallelized jobs -- just the plain vanilla single-processor
versions.

Siemens-Nixdorf has reported a SPECint95 value of 10.7 using
a 200 MHz R10000-based system.

Pre-release versions of SGI compilers deliver comparable performance
using our current 195 MHz hardware.



>From www.specbench.org:
>SGI 
>Indigo-2 Impact 10000 (195MHz, 1MB L2) 
>This result has been submitted to SPEC, see the disclaimer before
>studying any results. 
> SPECfp95         10.6 
> SPECfp_base95    10.2
> SPECint95        8.88 
> SPECint_base95   8.50
>
>Hmmmm...I don't see 10.7 or 19 anywhere.

The Origin 2000 delivers 19.0 on the single-processor version of
SPECfp95.  Again, the system had more than 1 cpu installed, so that
is what SPEC put in the web page.  If you look at the detailed reports,
it is clear that only 1 cpu was used to run the SPECfp codes.

 
>Who's lumping? The Alpha21164 smokes the R10000, the PentiumII is
>faster than the R5000.

Chips are not systems, and by themselves, chips do not do computing for you.

DEC makes fast cpus and generally nice systems, but the Origin2000
is generally faster and cheaper when running scientific/technical/
engineering codes.



>DEC's Alpha21164 smokes the R10000. Especially the 600mhz version.
>Hell, even two R1000/195's in an Octane can't beat one
>Alpha21164/500.

The 600 MHz 21164 is clearly a very fast processor, but it would be
easy to find jobs and/or workloads for which a 2-cpu Octane was
faster than a DEC workstation with a 600 MHz 21164 cpu.  The Octane
would almost certainly be faster on both integer and FP SPECrate
measures, for example.
