John R. Stevens (johnstevens@ucsd.edu) wrote:
: You can put together a nice 500mhz Alpha21164-based system for
: under $8000. 
: Who's lumping? The Alpha21164 smokes the R10000, the PentiumII is
: faster than the R5000.

  From some of the official (manufacturer's) SPEC numbers I have (sorry I
don't have PowerPC - I've only seen projected SPEC92 numbers at Motorola's
site which won't correlate with SPEC95 numbers):

            600MHz DEC Alpha 21164  180MHz HP PA-8000  200MHz MIPS R10000    
SPECint95              18.0                11.8                10.7
SPECfp95               27.0                20.2                19.0

           533MHz DEC Alpha 21164PC  250MHz UltraSPARC-II  300MHz Pentium2
SPECint95              14.3                10.1                11.6
SPECfp95               17.0                14.0                 7.2

  There's no question (unless any one manufacturer is lying) that the
Alphas are miles ahead of the MIPS processors in terms of performance
here and I don't think SGI have a crown - Alpha's with DEC's 64-bit PCI
cards render quicker than O2s and SGI's solutions and upgrades cost
irrational amounts of money when compared to, say, DEC. Not to mention
IRIX's suid binaries when it comes to security across a network. :-\

  The real price/performance mushroom cloud is that DEC 533MHz 21164PC;
whilst fractionally down on the fp value of the best single-cpu R10000
solution the integer performance is up by 40% and the cost is something to 
rant about - US$495 per cpu, approx $200-$300 for the m/b to support it, 
and as an upgrade to a bog-standard PCI `IBM compatible PC' you just 
require a new PSU and a load of matched SIMMs. The cost is not dissimilar
to buying a decent PPro+board or K6+board in the UK (P2 really being a
waste of everyone's time for price/performance) - these are of course much
slower and have only 25% of the AlphaPC's memory bandwidth.
  For example, upgrading my own little 486 to a 64Mb 533MHz AlphaPC might
hypothetically cost about �800 (UKP - approx US$1200) providing a machine
faster than any single-cpu SGI/HP workstation for most general tasks and
close in terms of heavy fp work for a minute fraction of the cost, and a
comparable 2.1Gb/sec main memory bandwidth competing with the best SGI
workstations. The AlphaPC just put one of the world's fastest single-cpu
workstations at the price of a high-street mid-range PC.

  Yet some people still have more money than sense and buy the SGI
solution. This is partly because once upon a time (3+ years ago) SGI
workstations were a reasonably cost effective workstation buy and some
people still seem to live in that time warp without perusing anything
other than Silicon Surf's marketing pages. In fact many academics are
rather amazed when their cheap �2000 PPro-Linux boxes outperform their
�20,000 SGI boxes at many intensive computational tasks (even with the
lower bandwidth). Yet if they'd thought before they bought, they wouldn't
have been ... 

  Buying SGI has traditionally been almost mandatory at York, but the
Physics dept only use Alpha's - they know the Alpha's are considerably
faster and the fact that they're much cheaper is just an added bonus.

Paul.



