In article <5naahp$k94$1@netty.york.ac.uk>,
Paul Halliday <pih100@york.ac.uk> wrote:

>  There's no question (unless any one manufacturer is lying) that the
>Alphas are miles ahead of the MIPS processors in terms of performance
>here and I don't think SGI have a crown - Alpha's with DEC's 64-bit PCI
>cards render quicker than O2s and SGI's solutions and upgrades cost
>irrational amounts of money when compared to, say, DEC. 

Despite your frenzy to praise DEC, there are many reasons why people
buy SGIs.  We do well when it comes to rich feature sets, tight
integration, scalability, and outright performance.

Quick, can you buy a DEC server that scales up to 128 processors and
GBs/second of I/O throughput (Origin)?

Can you buy a DEC with half the graphics performance or a quarter of
the features of an Onyx2?

Can you buy a desktop DEC that can move over 500 MB/s sustained from
_disk_ to memory (measured just recently on Octane)?

Can you buy a cheap DEC machine with the kind of video, audio,
compression, multimedia, etc capabilities of O2?

What price would you have to pay for these machines if you could buy
them from DEC (or anybody else)?

The performance of a system on a particular application (unless the
app is SPEC) goes way beyond the system's performance on SPEC.  We put
a lot into our processors, system interfaces, operating system,
graphics and video cards that improve each others performance and
scalability but have no effect on SPEC, triangles per second, or other
one dimensional benchmarks.

For many developers and customers, performance, price/performance, and
even performance at a given cost level are better on SGI.  That's why
we still sell systems.  If your code doesn't happen to fit the SGI
bill, then by all means buy a machine that does, but asserting that
anybody who buys SGI is a fool is just plain wrong.

nate



