In article <5n90kd$1c4@news2.zippo.com>, lindahl@pbm.com (Greg Lindahl) writes:
> everhart@arisia.gce.com (Glenn C. Everhart) writes:
> 
>> The scalability advantages will grow as the number of processors gets
>> bigger than 32. Remember that SMP machines can get up to 10 or 12
>> (some would say perhaps 16) profitably, and some specialized applications
>> can get by on loosely coupled systems and use networks.
> 
> Actually, Sun builds SMP machines up to 64 cpus and SGI builds them
> much larger (with non-flat memory), quite profitably.
> 

	Yes, but as Glenn points out this is about scalability.

	Take a look at STREAM for that 64 processor E10000.
	Notice how going from 56 to 63 STREAM increases very little?


------------------------------------------------------------------
Machine ID                ncpus    COPY    SCALE      ADD    TRIAD
------------------------------------------------------------------

Sun_UE_10000                63   6307.0   6391.0   7203.0   7197.0
Sun_UE_10000                56   6183.0   6304.0   7131.0   7128.0
Sun_UE_10000                48   5961.0   6056.0   6861.0   6914.0
Sun_UE_10000                40   5317.0   5374.0   6162.0   6222.0
Sun_UE_10000                32   4397.0   4408.0   5166.0   5188.0


	In fact, it is a 2% COPY increase.  Galaxies will SCALE MUCH better
	as the latency and coherency issues are more effectively dealt
	with.  The E10000 has somewhere between 400 - 500 nsec latency
	in a 64 processor machine, coherency is a (scaling) problem in multiple
	CPU machines.  SGI does quite well with large scientific/engineering
	problems as their NUMA shines in that space.  The E10000 is a very
	fine COMMECIAL box (where the money is) for 1997.  Things change
	dramatically next year with Wildfire and Galaxies.

	While Sun had championship TPC-D numbers in January, the
	following numbers will hardly be worth mentioning this time next year.
	Very seldom do things change by a factor of 10 in a single year.
	Galaxies will do better than that.

