except VMS, Ultrix, and Digital Unix, and possibly some PDP-11 OSes.

Linux is Right Out.


In article <5nb0km$fk4$1@yeppa.connect.com.au>,
uridium@mercury.networx.net.au (Alastair Boyanich) writes:

[[reformatted for legibility]]

> Greetings! ...Im attempting to get 14 DECServer 200/MC terminal
> servers to work again. They were switched off and haven't been used
> in a while. I can find very little information on them save the fact
> that they utilise the Dec LAT Protocol which all say is "secret".

Sigh...  LAT is *not* secret. LAT *cannot* be secret. Portions of the
LAT protocol are patented, and patent law **requires** full disclosure
of the patented material.  Someone reportedly handed out copies of the
protcol spec at a DECUS meeting in the US in the mid-80s. This (as
well as the subject material) may make the patents invalid under
Commonwealth law.  Consult a local solicitor :).

Given network traces of a LAT host talking to a LAT box,
re-implementing LAT takes about one week. Again, consult a local
solicitor about the patent issues before doing this at home.


> I've noticed that the current 900MC's support tcpip, does anyone know
> if the 200/MC also does?

No, the DSRV-100 and DSRV-200 don't do TCP/IP, and it's not likely
they ever will.  DEC never released software for these boxes that did
anything but LAT.  The DS-2000s have a 68k CPU, but they don't have
much memory (128K? 256 K?)  and the downloadable code is full of calls
to a now-undocumented PROM.  If you don't have a MOP-capable boothost
and a boot image for the 200s, you might as well forget them.

From time to time people talk about implementing TCP/IP software for
the DSRV-200, but neither the PROM nor the memory map for the chips in
the box are documented, so...


>Im attempting to get the sods to run under Linux (Please don't spam
>me) and I only want to hang some terminals off one of them. Does
>anyone know a) the specs for these things, b) how to "boot" them, and
>c) How the devil to I network them to a machine running linux?

   a) the specs are Not Available.

   b) You boot them via MOP.  NetBSD and OpenBSD have a mopd
   implementation that may be capable of booting DS100s and DS200s,
   assuming you have a load image.  I don't know about  Linux;
   you may be out of luck there, too.

    c) you don't.  Even if you get the boxes booted, it still doesn't
    help you get a LAT protocol implemntation for _any_ free OS.
    (I once had a LAT implementation that worked on 4.3BSD-Reno; it's
    not redistributable-- outside New Zealand, and possibly not even
    there anymore-- for legal reasons.)

 
> Regards Al Boyanich.

Uh, is there some reason your software forces you to format each
paragraph as a single line??



