Jonathan Stone <jonathan@DSG.Stanford.EDU> writes:
> Sigh...  LAT is *not* secret. LAT *cannot* be secret. Portions of the
> LAT protocol are patented, and patent law **requires** full disclosure
> of the patented material.  Someone reportedly handed out copies of the
> protcol spec at a DECUS meeting in the US in the mid-80s. This (as
> well as the subject material) may make the patents invalid under
> Commonwealth law.  Consult a local solicitor :).

  The whole protocol isn't patented. Key parts are. And the patents reflect
an earlier version of LAT (current is V5.2, I think).

> Given network traces of a LAT host talking to a LAT box,
> re-implementing LAT takes about one week. Again, consult a local
> solicitor about the patent issues before doing this at home.

  True, but LAT is a lot less important now than it was in the past, so it
probably isn't worth the effort.

> No, the DSRV-100 and DSRV-200 don't do TCP/IP, and it's not likely
> they ever will.  DEC never released software for these boxes that did
> anything but LAT.  The DS-2000s have a 68k CPU, but they don't have
> much memory (128K? 256 K?)  and the downloadable code is full of calls
> to a now-undocumented PROM.  If you don't have a MOP-capable boothost
> and a boot image for the 200s, you might as well forget them.

  One of the DECrouters (200? 250) uses the same box as a DS200 but with a
different default boot image name and a larger memory capacity. The DS200
has blank solder pads where the extra RAM goes, so you might be able to
increase the box's memory. You can do a "CHANGE SERVER SOFTWARE" on the
DECrouter and have it boot the DS200 code, but the reverse doesn't work as
there isn't enough memory.

  The MOP server code isn't hard (I wrote a load/dump/remote console im-
plementation [in BASIC!] for Digital of Canada in about 2 weeks of spare
time). The load image is a simple RSX netbootable image, only with 68K
code. The header definition is the same as RSX, though.

> From time to time people talk about implementing TCP/IP software for
> the DSRV-200, but neither the PROM nor the memory map for the chips in
> the box are documented, so...

  The memory map was available (in the DS200 technical manual, which doesn't
seem to be available any longer). You didn't need the PROM if you didn't want
to use the functions it provided (you could talk directly to the hardware).

  A long time ago I got fed up with TSM's restrictions and wrote some code
that was downloaded to the DS200 which then upline dumped the config EEPROM.
Similar code put the EEPROM contents back. So doing stuff on the box is pos-
sible.

  If you want to play, do:

  SET PRIV
  CHANGE PORT 1 ODT ENA
  ODT

  [Note - some versions of the DS200 crash when you do this due to a bug in
the way the watchdog timer is implemented]

  There's two versions of the DS200 - the "classic" and the "cost reduced".
There are some minor hardware differences between the two.

  If you want to work at putting IP on a DECserver, use a DECserver 5x0 -
it's a PDP-11 and all you need is a disk drive in order to boot 2BSD Unix.
Presumably it could also run fuzzball.

