Rob Young wrote:
<interesting description of IBM's SYSPLEX >

The IBM description pretty well matches my experience in a bank back in
'91 and
'92. They would configure the "box" to have multiple computers each with
their
own amount of available CPU. They had a big team for capacity planning.

They were absolutely estatic about ESCON (they claimed to be the first
site to have it) and installed fiber between two buildings. They were
amazed that with a single pair, they could access a disk controller in
the other building. Meanwhile, I built a disasted tolerant cluster of 2
VAXes with 2 fibers and served 4 disks in a shadow set etc.

I have a feeling that Galaxy itself is perhaps near Sysplex, but since
it will be running VMS, the inter-node communications of VMS and VMS
clusters will be the big differentiation between SYSPLEX and GALAXY.

Also, One of the reasons that bank used SYSPLEX was because of the need
of different versions of middleware (CICS/IMS) for various applications
as well 
as the ability to put in one box what was originally in many boxes, thus
saving on maintenance costs and floor space. (when you have nearly the
whole floor already and constantly need to add more disk space, you
can't enlarge the building :-)
I think that they were running over 10 CICS copies on distributed on 3
"MVS" machines, each with extremely distinct uses and priorities.

Thus, the SYSPLEX was a way for that bank to group totally separate
entities into one box and keep them independant from each other. (You
wouldn't want the "batch" version of MVS to interfere with the "online
banking" copy of MVS during daytime) Communications between "MVS"s were
still done through regular SNA channels.

In VMS, the goal of grouping many VMS into one box will not be to save
floor space and still keep very separate VMS copies running, but rather
to enhance the communications between copies of VMS and applications. So
it is quite a different philosophy in a way.

But the underlying principle is about the same I guess. It is just that
VMS allows such a technology to unlock a inter-machine potential that
MVS can't expect to do.



