In article <3398ef10.16111828@news.soca.com>,
John R. Stevens <johnstevens@ucsd.edu> wrote:
>>SPEC is a *system* benchmark, not a chip benchmark.
>>Systems have been built with a variety of performance levels
>>using the R10000 cpu.
>
>I was talking about destop workstations, not servers.  The DEC
>workstations outperform or equal the SGI desktop workstations in
>OPC's benchmarks as well.

Yes and you still missed the point.  Spec measures the chip,
the caches, the compilers and the memory system.  Not just the chip.
You can take the same 195 MHz R10000 cpu and measure its spec
performance in an Indigo2 and an Octane.  The cpu in the Octane's
faster because the memory system is better matched to the R10000
cpu.

Octane Specint is 9.3.  Indigo2 is 8.9.
Octane Specfp is 17.0.  Indigo2 is 10.6.

These are all single cpu numbers.  Same chip (195 MHz R10K).
Same secondary cache size (1MB).  Both are workstations,
not servers.

The moral of the story is -- never look at a spec number and
say "That's as good as the cpu can do."  It might be true, it
might not be true.  A larger or faster secondary cache, a better
memory system, or a new revision of the compilers can make
the same chip perform better.

The obvious corollary is "Never assume that 2 different model
computers with the same CPU will have the same performance."
That applies to all machines, not just SGI machines.

And then of course, people should always keep in mind that
spec numbers are but a single measure of system performance.
But that's a whole 'nother kettle of fish.

	Ray Chen
	rcc@sgi.com



