I think the 3rd and 4th columns tell a big story.  The Sun has a faster
external clock rate with higher bandwidth, a slower internal clock rate
and a significantly smaller CPU cache, and yet maintains close to the same
speed.  But more significantly, the Sun's faster external bus bandwidth
explains why the Suns often perform as well or better than the Alphas.

When Sun gets the clock rate to 500+Mhz, the Sun is going to walk around
like a giant... the current Sun 300Mhz model, remember, is faster than the
Sun 2000 systems that many BIG universities were supporting thousands of
users on without major strain - this *IS* all relative.  Suns are for
serving, Alphas are for math... it's a generalization which works to the
advantage of the industry at large.

