system@niuhep.physics.niu.edu wrote:

> jfmezei <"jfmezei"@videotron.ca.[no.spam]> writes:
> 
> >> > You get VMS and when VMS supports Galaxies ("should be soon":)
> >> > you get the 10x performance with suitable hardware. Some of the
> >> > suitable hardware exists now, and the 10x performance presumes
> >> > a large configuration.
> 
> >Does that 10x performance improvement mean that if you group 5 500mhZ
> >CPUs, instead of getting 5*500=2,500Mhz,
> 
> Given a task with substantial IO and cpu requirements do you know of
> any SMP system that will scale that way?

I think the question was asking for clarification.  
What exactly is 10x of what?


Looking at a lot of benchmarks numbers for a lot of systems,
I would say that systems that scale well often approach
a 50% asymptote: e.g., I might expect a 64-processor
UltraSPARC to give me 32X single-processor throughput
in a CPU-limited system-intensive workload.

(Some systems do much worse than this, and for highly-
parallel tasks without I/O, you can get 64x, but ...)

So, giving Galaxy the benefit of the doubt and assuming
that it scales up perfectly on I/O - system - intensive loads
1:1, the "advantage" over other systems is likely to be
more like 2x than 10x.  (That's a big if, of course.)
That is, I might get by with only 32 processors what
might take 64 on another system.

[Another point I would like to make is that minimizing
the number of CPUs by itself is not necessarily a goal.  
CPUs are cheap enough now that they are usually packaged
on boards with certain ratios of I/O buses, memory,
etc.  The associated external cache probably costs more 
than the CPU.  But, that is kind of an aside.]

[The other point kind of tossed in to the discussion
is how many copies of the OS should be running.  The
discussion suggests that 8 copies running on modes of
4 CPUs each is the "right" way, rather than a single
copy for all 32 CPUs.  I don't accept that.  With
"fancy" locking techniques, you can scale up to 64 
or even more CPUs.  Sure, you are adding overhead,
but so does running more copies of the OS.  I don't
think there is nearly enough information available
to say that 1 copy per 4 CPUs is optimal.]


> BTW 5 cpus is not a large configuration.
> 
> Basically what is being suggested is that if we have a large Galaxy,
> e.g. 8 VMS nodes clustered together, each node consisting of 4 cpus,
> all of this in the same physical container, then that machine will,
> on many tasks, have 10x the performance of any other 32 cpu machine/"cluster"

Doing what?  If the workload is CPU-bound jobs, then 10x is 
logically impossible.  Also, from looking at other I/O-bound
benchmarks, if they follow the 50% rule above, 10x isn't
possible either.  

So, I assume that you are looking at a particular kind of
workload that VMS clusters might excel at: asynchronous I/O 
of clusterwide-lockable data.  And the source of such a 
workload is an RDBMS using VMS-cluster-distributed-locks, right?

But, that brings us right back to the question of whether
the OS needs to do this in the first place, and why RDBMS 
vendors generally do their own locking anyway- so, this seems 
to be a circular discussion.

> At worst, on a few types of tasks, it will have the same performance
> as other 32 cpu machines.
> 
> We shall all have to wait a bit and see how it actually turns out.

Agreed.


