Ron Bean (rbean@earth.execpc.com) wrote --

> Wrist position is also important, and this depends on the overall
> design of the keyboard.

Constant use of one finger and frequent sideways bending of the wrist
seem to be the *main* culprits in computer-input-related injuries.
Ergonomics is (are?) usually given as the main reason for putting
function keys at the top of the keyboard rather than at the side. And
still software is optimized for mouse use, and Ctrl is put where a HUGE
bend is required to get at it....

>                    ....Also, I've heard that job-related stress
> can make it worse, and no magic keyboard is going to solve that.
> (This should not be too surprising, since stress makes a lot of
> things worse.)

So what am I doing right? I'm frequently under humongous stress, I slump
sideways in my chair, I keep my wrists on home-made cushions (supposedly
a big no-no), I've done everything, EVERYTHING, through the QWERTY keys
and my home-row Ctrl key for fifteen years... hey, wait a minute; could
that be it?

Seriously, some people, apparently, are prone to input-related injuries,
and definitely need something more than Ctrl in the home row.

> In any case, as far as I know the original purpose of the chord
> keyboard was to allow you to keep your other hand on the mouse;

I heard of them long before rodents took over. Chording devices were
designed long ago to let reporters, for example, type stories on the
spot.

> IBM had a different idea where they used chords for common letter
> sequences, so that you could type *faster*, but again they
> weren't concerned about RSI's....

I can't believe they are now.

> What's an "air keyboard"?

It's like the "air guitars" college kids used to play while, uh, under
the influence in the late 60s and early 70s -- it's make-believe.

