[...]
> Versions are nothing compared to systems like SCCS or RCS, or modern
> approaches like Atria.  You need to save the right versions, track them,
> keep track of when and why changes were made.

        Ah, but then you're talking  about something entirely different,
    e.g.,  CMS  (VMS's  "project" tracking system  which  includes  such
    things as  generations,  merging,  reference  directories,  just  to
    barely  scratch  the  surface).   This is not what file versions are
    about.  (BTW and AFAIK, CMS pre-dates RCS  and SCCS, and IMHO does a
    better  job...I'll  acknowledge  that some of this is  a  matter  of
    taste.)

>                                                Or be able to go back
> to some point in time: on the filesystem I'm currently using, I can
> cd to one hour ago or to one day ago as snapshots are automatically
> taken.

        Which  is  nice,  but  perhaps   overkill  (I  understand,  your
    application  may  require  it,  but daily  incremental  backups  are
    suitable for most sites...those that do backups at all!).

        No, file versions  are  useful  in  edit-compile cycles, or just
    lengthy  edit sessions.  I'll build up a dozen or so versions of the
    file(s) I'm working on, and only PURGE at the very end.  I also tend
    to keep the original, lowest version  number  until  I'm  completely
    done  and  have  replaced  the  file  in  CMS  (i.e., released it to
    production).  The days  of  1500  block  disk  quotas  and  500 page
    working sets have long since passed.  For anyone doing serious work,
    having  multiple versions of a file take up disk space does not even
    enter the equation.  Being able  to  back  up  to  the  last  edited
    version, or last-but-one or last-but-two, etc., of the file is easy,
    automatic,  and  a life-saver when you've just done a PARAGRAPH FILL
    on some C or Fortran  source  code!   :-}  (Please  don't talk to me
    about undo...that's an entirely different set of issues.)
 
        -Ken
