>>a 100-way SMP box, what can be concluded about MTBF?
> 
> Valid point, so you're saying that the 2 x 2 configuration might be more 
> stable to the extent that half of it would survive a CPU failure (maybe not
> on a 2100, but on some other galaxy-capable machine.)  So for a 100 node 
> machine, break it into a 25 node, 4 CPU/node cluster.  If 1 CPU fails/per
> year, you still have 24 nodes running.  Cute.  Only problem, how do you fix
> that one broken CPU?  I imagine you could restart the other 3 as a 3 CPU
> node and boot it into the cluster, but the dead CPU remains.  It would be 
> interesting to see how one hot swaps a 600 Mhz CPU card without crashing the 
> other remaining 99 nodes, or endangering the service person (99 active 600
> Mhz microwave generators in a box!).

        The point that apparently has been missed or omitted is that the
    systems which  support  32,  64,  or  100  nodes,  will  _also_ have
    hot-swappable  CPU's and memory boards, at least, and probably  hot-
    swappable   everything-that-can-be-swapped.    These   systems   are
    supposed  to come online and run for a _long_ time (like current VMS
    clusters, only better).  Part of  the  idea  of partitioning into 25
    4-cpu  nodes,  as you've noted, is that, given a 1-cpu failure,  you
    lose one 4-cpu node.  The remaining three "good"  cpus  can  be  (a)
    rebooted  as  a  3-cpu node, or (b) reconfigured to donate the three
    cpus to other nodes in  the  cluster  (all  this  on the fly).  At a
    convenient  time,  the failed cpu can be hot-swapped and added to  a
    running node, or the three good cpus removed from where ever they've
    been running, and configured with the 4th cpu to boot  as  the  25th
    node  again.   The _system_, the cluster, stays up and available the
    whole time.

        In  another  article  in  this   thread,  someone  mentioned  or
    questioned  crashing  an SMP system just because a CPU  failed.   In
    VMS, you can (as in other SMP OS's) turn individual CPUs on  or  off
    transparently while running.  No problem.  Processes running on that
    cpu  or  migrated  to  other  cpus, etc.  But if a CPU _fails_, data
    integrity is threatened, so VMS makes the choice to protect the data
    and crash the system.  This doesn't change in Galaxies, but you gain
    (as in VMSclusters)  by  having  the  cluster  continue  and  only a
    relatively  small  portion  of that cluster crash (a  single  node).
    This is one of the issues Glenn Everhart was hinting at.

        In terms of David's  hypothetical micro-Galaxy :-), partitioning
    a  4-node SMP machine into a cluster of two 2-node systems, it might
    then be possible to keep one 2-node system up in  the  face  of  the
    single  cpu failure (you'll need a quorum disk :-), but you lack the
    hot-swap feature: you'll  need  to  take  the  whole  thing down for
    repair  at  some  point.  It's also unclear what  sort  of  hardware
    support (not just the hot-swap feature) will be required to  support
    a  Galaxy.   Maybe all multi-cpu Alphas will be supported, but maybe
    only those with certain system busses, or who-knows-what.

> The downside to having all of those processors in one case would be a
> catastrophic physical failure, not just of the external types (fire/flood/
> earthquake), but also of the internal type - say a power supply catching
> fire and trashing the inside of the box.

        Indeed, and that scares me too.   But  I  guess this calls for a
    split-site, two Galaxy VMScluster, eh?

>                                          Those sorts of failures don't
> happen very often, but I'd much rather lose one separate 4 node machine to
> an internal fire, than all 100 nodes!  A classic case of putting all your
> eggs in one basket - so you'd still want other parts of the cluster outside
> the box and hopefully far enough away to be out of harms way. 

        I guess there's always a trade.  Performance versus availability
    versus safety, etc.  I'd guess that  anyone in a position to install
    a  Galaxy (on Wildfire) would also be in a position to set things up
    with a redundant site for safety and availability  in  the  face  of
    disaster.

        -Ken
