Date: 1997/06/03
Message-ID: <5n2hll$a8s@Starbase.NeoSoft.COM>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 245955987
References: <1997Jun2.213737.1@eisner> <5n1239$rvq@news1.zippo.com>
Organization: NeoSoft Internet Access (+1 888 NEOSOFT)
Newsgroups: comp.arch,comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


As a system manager on systems with a lot of disparate applications, 
I can't tell you how thankful I am for file versions.  Every day I
refer to older versions of files to diagnose application and system
problems.  I suspect that file versioning is one of the (many)
reasons VMS systems are so reliable.  When there is a problem, there
is usually some kind of log file that will give you an idea of what the 
root cause is.

I HAVE done system administration on UNIX and I certainly do remember
times when I wanted that last copy of a configuration file, or times
when two people were editing the same file and one of them clobbered
the others.  vipw is a stupid kludge.  In what way is not having versions 
an advantage?

In article <5n1239$rvq@news1.zippo.com>, Greg Lindahl <lindahl@pbm.com> wrote:
>
>There are other OSes which support versioning, such as Solaris (via a
>special filesystem).  However, few customers seem to be interested in
>it. 

I would suggest that only VMS users really appreciate file versions
these days.

>     There are also applications such Emacs which support full
>versioning, but again few people seem interested. Plan 9 and Network
>Appliance both include efficient ways of making many backups of a
>given file, to give many versions over time. And anyone using CVS has
>a shot at many versions of a file, again in a space-efficient fashion.

Using CVS you have to explicitly ASK for a version.             

>
>The VMS scheme makes a separate copy for each version. I often found
>that typing PURGE was such a reflex that I lost the old versions anyway.

I had that habit too, when disk space was relatively limited.  These days
I would no more delete versions before the nightly backup ran than I would
delete any other file that I wasn't sure I didn't need.

It didn't take me long to get over the habit of typing PURGE all the time.

Really, anyone who argues against the flexibility that OpenVMS versioning
provides is just asking for a restriction on your options.  If you are
CERTAIN that versions are of no use, you can set versioning at the 
directory or the file level to allow only a maximum of 1 version.  
Effectively, VMS file versioning gives one a superset of the capabilities 
of other file systems in this regard.

And don't argue that it's a pain to see all of those old versions when
you do a DIR command, just get in the habit of typing "DIR ;", which
gives the latest version only.




