Hugo,

You are arguing over semantics.  Yes, Intel is not a monopoly in the
dictionary sense, but they do have a monopoly in the economic sense,
specifically in their domination by economies of scale.  Intel has over
twice the market share of its closest competors.  Therefore, all things
being equal, Intel's fixed costs (overhead, research, etc) are 1/2 that of
its competion.

This in itself would not neccessarily be illegal, but Intel uses its power
unfairly.  For example, Intel chips are required by large OEMs.  It simply
is not possible to meet the demands of your customers with using some Intel
chips.  Intel's pricing is based on wether an OEM uses Intel exculisively
or not, its also based on the use of the "Intel inside" logo.  Intel also
gives preference for OEMs buying Intel motherboards instead of designing
their own.

These types of abuse would not be possible, except by Intel's power in the
market.  These practices result in an ineffecent marketplace.

Just my opinion,

TK

