Hugh LaMaster (lamaster@nas.nasa.gov) wrote:
: So, what bugs in various manufacturers CPU's have been 
: documented on products shipped after 1 Jan 96?

Designing chips didn't get any easier on that date. Perhaps the most
significant change was that you can now get errata lists more easily. The
errata list for the Pentium II is interesting reading. Hardly "zero
defects." Most of which are pretty much irrelevant to end users.

Most of the serious hardware bugs I've dealt with were on UNIX workstations
where the demands of the consumer market don't matter. Interesting that in
practice these more expensive boxes may be buggier because there are fewer
users to shake them out and fewer users to amortize the cost of building a
"perfect" product across. (Of course it depends on what aspect of
reliability one is considering. I expect workstation users would be less
willing to accept the PCI performance bugs that have been seen in the
Wintel market.)

On Jan. 1 1997 I got email from someone using software I worked on almost a
decade ago asking if I knew anything about a suspicious core dump on an
R5000 based SGI machine. The faulting PC was sitting on a page boundary
etc. Turns out there's a bug in the R5k first rev (or may first two revs,
can't recall) and the OS wasn't working around it. I turned up an errata on
a chip manufacturer's Web site and the user took this up with SGI
sales. The surprising thing to me is that they were denying any problem
with their hardware despite that the date on the errata being months
before.

(My initiation into the world of broken hardware was a bug in the Sun 3/60
that would randomly crash a process in the presence of heavy paging. Sales
claimed there was no problem. Finally someone got through to a friend in
engineering at Sun who quoted us an ECO number. Then the local sales office
wanted $1,000 a machine to fix the affected systems. The lab I worked for
burned a PAL to replace the erroneous one in each of the 6 or so machines
we had. Academic Computing at Carnegie Mellon had to explain to Sun that if
they didn't fix the 25 or so machines they had, the organization would
never buy another product from Sun again.)

-Z-



