jcoffin@taeus.com (Jerry Coffin) writes:
>For instance, consider a typical microcontroller application - a CPU 
>built into a CD player.  It likely contains about 2 kilobytes of code, 
>which is all it will ever run the entire time exists.  Quite possibly 
>a few million CD players using the same control module will be sold.  
>Once code is written for a particular embedded application, it is 
>rarely if ever upgraded.

2 kB is probably off by a factor of ten or more; your CD player has
a good bit more program ROM than that in it.  But yes, it's small.

Actually, in many cases the next generation, shipped about a year later,
is an upgraded version of pieces of the same code.

>So, we have an _extremely_ small amount of programming involved, even 
>though a huge number of CPUs are sold using that programming.

It takes far longer to produce embedded assembly code that must meet
hard real-time requirements than to produce PC or workstation code;
there's a hell of a lot of engineering time involved in cranking the
stuff out.  I wouldn't be surprised if the effort difference, per kbyte of
object code, is a factor of 100 or more.

(At one time I ground out real-time DSP assembly language speech
compression code, at a time where it took wierd hardware to do 8 million
multiply-adds a second, which I guess is revealing my age.   Total
brain-fry).

