Hello Marcus!

MC> I want to convert my C: drive to NTFS on Win NT4. When I try the convert
MC> utility it tells me that the c:drive is the system partition on an ARC
MC> compliant system and it cannot be converted. Anyone know what I can do?

You have to create at least two partitions, one of them just big enough to make
the boot loader fitting onto it; NT 3.1 required at least 4 MB, NT 3.5 6 MB,
although they weren't completely used.

I created a big Partition of 1040-6 MB and afterwards one more of 6 MB on my
brothers Jensen-Alpha. The little partition, the second one, became D:, the
first one C:. The big one contains the entire system, the small one is for boot
loader only.

You should backup your Data, then repartition and reinstall NT or repartition
installing the drive in an intel-based computer with a DOS utility for
splitting partitions. Following steps are to be taken: first shrink your
partition with the utility, then you can reinstall the drive in your ARC based
machine.

You should choose an amount of shrinking depending on what to do with the FAT
part of the disk. If you don't need it at all, choose about 10 to 16 MB, a
later Version of NT might require more space, so upgrading will be easy. If you
want a minimal cluster size to minimize slack space, you can choose between 32
MB and 127 MB. I recommend putting a backup copy of your firmware and ECU disks
onto that partition too. Check the space your osloader directory requires, less
space will not work at all, of course.

Afterwards, create the second partition under NT, format it as FAT and copy the
osloader subdirectory onto it. You will have to copy the hidden files (*.hal
and such) too. Then shutdown, go to the ARC console and change the environment
variables OSLOADER and OSLOADPARTITION (or something alike, don't remember) to
match your new layout. You should create a new boot menu item for that purpose
rather than changing the existing environment variables and /not/ delete the
old osloader directory as well, until you're sure that all is working
correctly.

As a last step, you have to use the convert utility to change the big FAT
partition to NTFS. The one with the new osloader has to remain FAT, the
osloaderdirectory on the converted drive can be deleted as well as the old boot
menu item.

Viele Gruesse, Christoph




