onwong wrote:
> 
> Using Digital UNIX 3.2C, I have 2 problems:
> 1. How do I automatically start an application from bootup? I have tried
> the /etc/inittab file with:
> xyz:3:boot:/bin/su - username -c "(cd /usr/home/username;./appname)"
> Also no luck with rc3.d script

A script in /sbin/rc3.d should work. It has to be called Sxxyyy where
xx is a number defining the position in the boot sequence and yyy is
an arbitrary name identifying the application. The script is sent a
"start" argument (which can be ignored of course).
The normal approach is to put the script in /sbin/init.d and have a
symbolic link from /sbin/rc3.d. This way, the same script can be used
for shutdown (when it receives a "stop" argument) as well as startup
- you would identify it for use in shutdown by a name such as Kxxyyy
symbolically linking to the script.
Check out the existing scripts in init.d and rc3.d to see what I'm
talking about.

> 2. Is it possible for a user other than root to shut down the system? In
> SCO UNIX I granted a user the shutdown authorisation to be used with
> asroot. But Digital UNIX does not seem to have asroot.
> 
> I appreciate any advice, including good web sites or good books.
> 
> Gary Robertson
