jfmezei wrote:
> Does SCALABILITY **REALLY** matter ??????
> 
> Those large shops who already have an established transaction capability
> on VMS, all they need is for DEC machines to speed up as fast or faster
> than their own growth needs. They don't need to see a whole line of
> machines ranging from 1 VUP to 1000VUP on they day they make the
> purchase. They just need to know that the 500 VUP machine they buy today
> can be replaced with the 600 VUP model that will come out the next year.

There are different sorts of customers.  A Galaxy system would be well
sorted for datawarehouse.  Datawarehouses usually consist of a huge
database, where queries must be answered fast even if a query accesses
most of the database.  Example: In a retail compagny you may make a
datawarehouse in which there is a record for each sale taken
place within the last year.  The size of the database will be some
terabytes.  Such a datawarehouse can be used to find out where to
extend your network of shops.  It might also be used to plan 
marketing campaigns, where it can answer questions like how much
would it cost us if we change the price in that and that way or how
much money do that and that customer group use on one particular
product.  The answers of such queries should be ready in a few hours.

When bouilding a datawarehouse you typically collect data from
different hardware platforms (IBM MVS, UNIS, VMS, Windows NT, ...).
It is therefore not that important that the hardware platform is one you
already have inside the house.  I fact datawarehouses are often build
on special hardware platforms like IBM's SP2, because it can process
huge quantities of data fast.  Galaxy and Wildfire could get a share
of that market.

