That reminds me of an article I read some time ago about a
"cluster" used for "parallel computing".  The "cluster"
was a bunch of workstations connected to an ethernet,
which happened to be running particle physics codes that
ran for weeks at a time.  When one job finished, the users
could start up another.  When all the jobs are done, the
results are aggregated together to get the final answer.

Nice work (for a "cluster") if you can get it.  Likewise, 
sometimes computational chemistry is done this way.

So, obviously, there are some jobs which go beyond 
embarrassingly parallel to the trivially parallel,
and the only clustering software needed is in the
human brain.


For that reason, it is imperative to describe what 
the applications are that we are talking about.



