Organization: I speak ONLY for myself. Nobody else, no organization.
Reply-To: Everhart@Arisia.GCE.Com
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



A pdp11/73 is a reasonably high powered pdp11. With enough disk and 
memory it can run RSX11M or M+, RSTS/E, or RT-11. You'll find some
software for those OSs at sunsite (sorry...don't have the complete
URL) but be aware that RSX SIG tapes started in fall 1977 and
the Sunsite collection covers only 1982 or 1983 on. However, one of
the 1987 tapes had some highlights of the "best of" older RSX tapes
which covers a good bit of interesting material.

I sent the complete RSX SIG tape collection I had to Mentec in 1995
expecting they'd make it available somehow (I was moving to NH from
Penna at the time). They probably still have the stuff, but also
have probably not done anything to get it off the old 9trk tapes
where it was.

The pdp11 is/was one of the best of the 16 bit minicomputers and had
a very large user community at one time, most of whom have moved to
other things. 

The tools, compilers and whatnot on it still will work, though
its interface is pretty much designed for fixed width fonts and use
with text. People pretty well lost interest before X windows appeared
so you won't find much in the way of windowing systems for it. It will
do realtime very nicely though, and is pretty robust since (in rsx at
least) each program runs in totally separated address space. There are
also unix dialects that will run on pdp11; Unix was first developed in
assembly language on a pdp7, then moved to pdp11 where the first C
language was developed. DECUS C, a free C compiler, generates excellent
code on the platform.

Contact mentec.com to see about who buys new ones.
Glenn Everhart



