[ me ]

>>  At http://www.workstation.digital.com/products/auseries/service.html
>>  is a web page from which one can grab a copy of the "DIGITAL Personal
>>  Workstation Service Guide - au-Series".  However, all that's there is
>>  an .exe file which I'm supposed to download.  That's not much help for
>>  those of us who have only UNIX workstations, and nothing on which one
>>  could run an .exe file.

I've had some helpful replies to the above, and the bottom line is this:

The file "dpwsausg.exe" is a self-extracting pkzip archive.  So those of
us who don't do Windows (or DOS, or ...) can just use any of the various
available UNIX programs which know how to extract things from a DOS pkzip
file.  (It would be nice if Digital could *mention* the fact that this is
a pkzip archive, somewhere on that web page.)  Juat a few minor caveats:

(1)  The "readme.txt" file says ...

        The initial page for the service guide is:
              \dpws\service\audpwssg.htm. 

... but that first component should be "dpws_au" rather than "dpws".

(2)  When extracting the stuff under UNIX (or, presumably, any system
with case-sensitive filenames), everything comes out as lower-case (as
one would expect), *except* for dpws_au/service/fru_menu.htm, which is
upper-case (i.e. DPWS_AU/SERVICE/FRU_MENU.HTM).  But the reference to
it is lower-case, which means that if one clicks on the "Replace FRUs"
button, it can't find the URL.  The obvious fix is to manually rename
the file to "fru_menu.htm", and then move it to the "dpws_au/service"
directory.  But it would be nicer if it could be fixed in the original
"dpwsausg.exe" file.

(3)  Finally, for those of us who prefer hardcopy manuals (easier to
refer to when actually servicing something, I feel), it would be nice
if there were a PostScript (or PDF) version available, as Digital does
with most of the other Digital documents I've grabbed?  (I do realize
that I could print out each individual page from my web browser.  But
it would be easier to have a single PostScript version of the entire
document.)

