In article <5mrei9$40s@cynic.portal.ca>, Curt Sampson wrote:
>In article <338B62A4.B014C80B@cs.wisc.edu>,
>Andy Glew  <glew@cs.wisc.edu> wrote:
>>Yeah, RISC has an advantage: maybe 15%. IMHO not enough to be worth
>>a change.
>
>So why did Intel switch to using a RISC core for the Pentium Pro,
>and doing their OOO stuff on those instructions? It appears that
>Intel, at least, thinks that RISC has some sort of advantage.

It's irrelevant how the PPro works internally.  Please distinguish
between "RISC instruction sets" and "RISC-like implementation
techniques".

The point that Andy was making is that he believes a native
RISC macroinstruction set (i.e. user visible instructions)
confers only a 15% performance advantage over a using similar
techniques to implement an x86 instruction set using the same
amount of silicon.

>I don't see why one couldn't decompose VAX or 68K instructions in
>the same way.

One could.  The main difficulty is, I think, instruction decoding.
The VAX has single instructions that are around 40 bytes long.
The 68020 also had some really nasty encodings for the fancy
addressing modes.  In both the VAX and the 68020 you have to
decode operands sequentially, since you can't tell where the next
operand is until you've decoded the current one.  The plain old
68000 was not so bad.  x86 is probably easier to decode than any
of the above--it has prefix bytes which are unpleasant, but after
that you only need to look at 4 bytes to know the length of an
instruction.

It's possible that a VAX or a 68020 with a decoded instruction
cache might be competitive.  But we'll never find out, because
Digital and Motorola are both in the RISC camp now.

The biggest real cost of building an x86 instead of a RISC is
validation.  The x86 has multiple operating modes which change
instruction decoding, it has a complicated protection model, and
it has hairy task switching features which probably nobody ever
uses but which still have to be validated.



