In news:1997Jun1.220402.98837@cc.usu.edu, Paul Hepworth
(pjh@gas.physics.usu.edu) stated --

> The EVIL thing about Windows (and OS/2 for that matter) and the alt key is
> this: I hit the ALT-F... I press Esc... I begin typing data... it goes to
> the menu because the ##$%!#$!@# Esc key did not clear me out of menu mode!
> Arghh!  This is stupid!

I suspect that apologists for all-graphical systems would tell you Alt-F
does two things: it activates the menu bar, and it selects the File menu.
Still, you are right: in W at least (I haven't seen OS/2), if you press
Alt alone, the menu bar is not activated until you *release* the key, and
even if that were not so, Alt-F could easily be interpreted as a single
action.

Is the observed behavior the result of stupidity? Graphics take time.
Leaving aside the question of whether mouse or keyboard operation is
faster, it cannot be denied that if you use the keyboard you get awfully
fast. And you have NOTHING TO DO when waiting for the system to complete
an action. With a mouse, (1) no matter how fast you get, the need to
traverse the screen for many functions makes you accustomed to greater
intervals between actions, (2) you have a sort of "security blanket" to
clutch when waiting for an operation to finish, and (3) you can almost
always occupy yourself by moving the pointer around aimlessly. You don't
notice delays nearly as much.

Commercial all-graphical systems are therefore designed to discourage
keyboard-only operation, and to make it nearly as inefficient as... uh,
to make it inefficient. Stupid? Maybe there's something to the first
adjective you used....

Dan Strychalski
