In article <339315BD.15FB@nas.nasa.gov>, Hugh LaMaster <lamaster@nas.nasa.gov> writes:
> Glenn C. Everhart wrote:
>> 
>> Galaxies is a VMS technology. Not NT, not unix, not anything else.
> 
> Any particular (technical) reason why DEC couldn't produce
> a Unix version?

It is technically feasible for DEC Unix to be modified enough to
do whatever VMS can do.  Whether the result would still qualify to
use the name "Unix" might be questioned, although IBM has gotten
the right to use that name for their MVS operating system.

Aside from the cost considerations, however, I doubt that Galaxies
is what most Unix customers want.  Spending money to build something
not of interest to potential customers would be a bad move.

VMS customers, on the other hand are much more concerned about
reliability, scalability, etc.  (On average, of course there are
exceptions, including those of us who just develop software and
would not feel too bent out of shape if our own machines crashed.)

