Running a copy of the OS per machine is not what's unique about Galaxy.
You need however to consider more of the problem space to understand
the benefits. Real loads require the ability to do I/O as well as
computes, and the ability to have OS services. Clustering provides a way
to do simultaneous I/O from all over the cluster, without causing
data corruption. Only OpenVMS has this; the other things called clusters
are IMO misappropriating the name. You can prevent data corruption by
having a server make storage available to a network too, and at low
loads it can look very like a cluster. However such a thing runs out of
gas when the server can't manage the load any more; it acts as a bottleneck.

Before other vendors can play the game, they need to build a distributed
locking system into their I/O. That is not easy and getting the kinks out
took a few VMS releases some years ago, as those who've been reading this
list awhile will recall...maybe 10 yrs back.

When you combine this capability set with some of the load balancing
and distributing techniques which have been described (and THINK about
their implications awhile!!) you'll realize it is a different animal
from the network with servers or from other vendors MPP systems and that
it avoids bottlenecks which have been plaguing everyone for years.

The price of admission is however the integration of a distributed
synchronization algorithm built into the I/O structures; that gives you
a cluster. The wannabe-clusters of other OSs still have that to pay.

Glenn Everhart

(and yes, I will admit a certain bias toward VMS here...good technology...)



