In article <01bc6f98$fcd99000$64ed6cc0@ws100>, "Dave Peterson" <peterson@vigyan.com> writes:

>I have a handful of old DEC machines, some RISC some VAX.  This has
>prompted me to ask the question, what versions of VAX ULTRIX are out there
>and what works with what?  

V1.0, V1.1, V1.2, V2.0, V2.2, V2.3, V3.0, V3.1, V4.0, V4.1, V4.2,
V4.2{A,B,C}, V4.3, V4.3A, V4.4 and V4.5.

The V4.x versions will tend to be the most common.

This excludes older intermediate hardware releases (usually letter
releases) and a whole slew of workstation releases, though they
always used one of these as the base.

>I am not really interested in version numbers
>and such, but rather in getting straight which hardware platforms are
>supported by which software distributions.  

The requirement doesn't make sense.  What hardware is supported
is very closely associated with the version.  Older versions
won't support hardware that hadn't come out yet.  Some new
releases dropped support for older hardware.  What you really
want is the Software Product Description for the particular
version you have to see what hardware it supports.

>For instance, if I have a VAX
>ULTRIX CD or tape for a VAXStation 3100, will it also run on a MicroVAX II?
> Among the older machines, what is the scoop here?

But you don't have a VAX/ULTRIX for a VAXstation 3100, you have Vx.y
that happens to support the VAXstation 3100.  It also supports all
other hardware platforms listed in the SPD for that version.  With
the exception of versions like Ultrix-32 V1.1 and Ultrix-32m V1.1
where one supported "big" VAX systems and the other supported the
MicroVAX I and II, there weren't hardware specific releases.  Exception
proving the rule are the letter releases that only supported a new
hardware platform until that support was merge into the next
release.

Generally speaking, more recent versions will support all the hardware
platforms released before that version.  If you have a version that
supports the VAXstation 3100, then it will support the MicroVAX II
since the MicroVAX II is the older system.

Exceptions: 

o  Support for the VAX 11/730 was officially dropped around V2.2.  
   All the kernel support and device drivers are still there, but 
   you'd be hard pressed to install the later versions, much less
   run them on the memory tight 730s.

o  The SPDs blindly list the MicroVAX 3100 and VAXstation 3100 as
   supported, but don't qualify by the model number.  There have
   been a lot of MicroVAX 3100 systems released that ULTRIX doesn't
   support.  The VAXstation 3100 models 30, 40, 38 and 48 are the
   only VAXstation 3100s supported.  I think the MicroVAX 3100 list
   is limited to the 30 and 40, though I haven't paid those much
   attention.  VAXstation 3100 systems using the SPX graphics sub-
   system also aren't supported.

o  V4.5 was released after a number of new VAX systems were released,
   but it doesn't support them.  There isn't support for the VAX 6000-600
   and any of the VAX 7000 systems.

o  The VAXstation 8100 was supported at one point (I think), but doesn't 
   seem to be anymore.

o  All the MIPS based systems are supported by one version or another.

