In article <5mvmiq$htt$1@wagner.spc.videotron.ca>, jfmezei <"jfmezei"@videotron.ca.[no.spam]> writes:
|>re: Digital selling Solaris on Alpha

|>What would be easier to do is for Sun to sell Alpha based systems that
|>Sun OEMed from Digital and that ran Solaris. 

A more likely combination would seem to be for Sun to also offer
a line of Alpha based systems.  That way, they are not dependant
upon Digital's timetable for hardware platforms.  The other problem
is that Digital's platforms may have endian-specific assumptions
on the boards/hardware. (I don't know)

|>However this would not solve the problem of software availability since
|>programs would still have to be recompiled to run.

We've demonstrated that this is simply not the case.
All it takes is a SPARC to Alpha binary translator.
A big-endian SunOS/Solaris SPARC V8 to big-endian Solaris Alpha
binary translator would be straightforward, and the translated
code on Alpha would still be faster than the top-of-the-line UltraSPARCs.
Translated SunOS code (running on Alpha) comes close to running as fast
as it would have on the top-of-the-line UltraSPARCs even when doing
all of the byte-swapping to convert big-endian to little-endian.

