In article <MPLANET.338f3ccfamoreira9898e5@desmond.nine.com>, 
amoreira@nine.com says...

[ ... ] 

> Actually, I may be wrong, but I heard that the embedded CPU is by far the 
> larger if one considers shipping volumes. If this is true, microcontroller 
> programmers might not be a minority.

If you think about the programming of a microcontroller vs. the 
programming of a general purpose computer, you'll realize why this 
isn't so.

For instance, consider a typical microcontroller application - a CPU 
built into a CD player.  It likely contains about 2 kilobytes of code, 
which is all it will ever run the entire time exists.  Quite possibly 
a few million CD players using the same control module will be sold.  
Once code is written for a particular embedded application, it is 
rarely if ever upgraded.

So, we have an _extremely_ small amount of programming involved, even 
though a huge number of CPUs are sold using that programming.

By contrast, a general purpose computer will typically hundreds or 
perhaps even thousands of programs throughout its life.  Each of these 
programs will typically be many times larger than even an extremely 
large program for a microcontroller.  Software for a general purpose 
computer is generally in one of two states: orphaned, or being 
upgraded.

-- 
    Later,
    Jerry.



