Larry Kilgallen wrote:
> 
> In article <339315BD.15FB@nas.nasa.gov>, Hugh LaMaster <lamaster@nas.nasa.gov> writes:
> > Glenn C. Everhart wrote:
> >>
> >> Galaxies is a VMS technology. Not NT, not unix, not anything else.
> >
> > Any particular (technical) reason why DEC couldn't produce
> > a Unix version?
> 
> It is technically feasible for DEC Unix to be modified enough to
> do whatever VMS can do.  Whether the result would still qualify to
> use the name "Unix" might be questioned, although IBM has gotten
> the right to use that name for their MVS operating system.

It is more like the question of whether or not Solaris 2.5.1
or Irix 6.4 can be called "Unix".  4.3 BSD purists would say
no, but they still look like "Unix" to me.

> Aside from the cost considerations, however, I doubt that Galaxies
> is what most Unix customers want.  Spending money to build something
> not of interest to potential customers would be a bad move.
> 
> VMS customers, on the other hand are much more concerned about
> reliability, scalability, etc.  (On average, of course there are
> exceptions, including those of us who just develop software and
> would not feel too bent out of shape if our own machines crashed.)

Of course VMS customers are concerned about reliability, 
scalability, etc.  And, of course, so are many Unix customers.
There seems to be a big market for "large" Unix servers, and
since all the non-captive RDBMS's are available for those 
large servers, complete with TPC benchmarks published for 
those servers.  [And for Alphaservers running VMS, too, 
of course.]  That would be kind of strange if the market
didn't exist, wouldn't it?  I don't know of any large database
customers or applications that welcome downtime or tolerate
very much of it, including "internal" customers.  Some Unix
systems (hardware and software) I have seen recently have
had MTBF's on the order of a year.  But, they haven't been
running RDBMS's either, which seem to reduce MTBF by a large
factor.  I would be interested if you have data comparing
downtime on, e.g. Alphaservers, running one of the well-known
RDBMS's (e.g. Sybase, Informix, Oracle) on both Digital Unix
and VMS.




