In article <3389FC4B.77DF@pgec.odu.edu>, Jason Witty <jwitty@pgec.odu.edu> writes:

:...I just got a VaxServer 3300, with no
:additional hardware (terminals, keyboards, etc.) and have no idea how to
:interface it.  Pulling off the front cover reveals a plug-in card which
:looks like some sort of I/O card with the markings MS650-AA, M7621-A,
:KA640-BA, and M7621-BA near the top of the card.

   Those are the bulkheads, and -- if that's the order you see them
   in -- they are not in the correct order.  (Some modules do not
   have attached bulkheads, so the bulkheads may be in a different
   order from the Q-bus modules.   MS650 is a memory module, and the
   KA640 is the CPU.  These are normally in the rightmost slots.
   To the left of the memory and CPU, one sees the other modules.
   M7621 is usually stamped on a Q-bus memory module.

   You will need a DSSI disk with an operating system -- these disks
   are normally in the cavity (or cavities) at the top of the system
   enclosure.  These systems normally run MicroVMS, VAX/VMS, or
   OpenVMS -- these are different names and potentially some different
   packaging for what is now known as OpenVMS VAX.

:  Now, on the card are
:two network connections (one thick and one 10Base2), and a modular
:connector with two arrows under it pointing in opposite directions.  Is
:this the serial port for a terminal interface?  

   The modular jack serial connection is called DECconnect or MMJ,
   and the pinout and adapters are in the OpenVMS Frequently Asked
   Questions (FAQ) posting.

:If so, how can I obtain
:a terminal for it?  Or better yet, does anyone know of a way to
:interface this box using a PC with a 10Base2 card in it?  Thanks VERY
:much for *any* replies, I greatly appreciate it.

   You can attach the PC, via the serial line, as the console to the
   MicroVAX.  Adapters are available to allow the PC to connect to
   DECconnect wiring, or you can solder your own.

   The following is from dim memory -- it's been many years since I have
   worked with a MicroVAX 3300 or 3400 class system...

   Be aware that <BREAK> -- which is effectively a serial line framing
   error -- is interpreted by the MicroVAX console as HALT, and powering
   up or down devices on the console serial line -- such as the PC -- can
   potentially generate framing errors, which are interpreted as HALT.

   There's a paddle switch on the console bulkhead, which controls the
   processing of <BREAK>.  Dot-in-Circle is the International (cryptic)
   symbol for HALT enabled, while dot-not-in-circle signifies HALT is
   disabled -- when disabled, <BREAK> is ignored.

   There's a rotary switch, and the arrow should be selected.  The
   face and the circle-T positions are for the console setup prompts
   and the self-test mode.

   As for the diagnostic LED -- F through 3 are the hardware tests.
   LED 1 is the transfer to the bootstrap.  LED 0 is the display used
   for normal (bootstrapped) operations.  If the LED is stuck in the
   F to 3 range, check the connections, and consider calling for
   service.  If stuck at 2 or 1, there is probably a problem with
   the system disk or the system disk file structure. 

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
