kilgallen@eisner.decus.org (Larry Kilgallen) writes:
>It is possible DEC has something really good, even though it
>seems like "old" approaches to you.  Certainly you are correct
>to avoid excitement over something which is not yet available,
>but over the years VMS has proven to me over and over that all
>operating systems are _not_ created equal.  RSX11 was where
>file versioning originated, so even if there was a patent it
>is long expired.

No, older DEC OSes such as RT-11, which predates RSX-11, had file versions
(I have no idea who first thought of the concept, whether DEC or someone
else, but in my RT-11/RSX-11/VMS days generally found DEC-style versioning
to be of limited value except for using up precious disk space).  And at
the time the general belief was that you couldn't patent a software
concept of this sort, so no one would have considered applying for such a
patent.

>this crucial construct astounds me.  I know other operating
>systems or application programs save one back copy, but I am
>just not that quick at discovering my mistakes.

People on those systems rapidly get used to doing PURGE a lot to
keep from having fifty versions of everything, and in the process
wipe out the very old versions they need.  I know this from my days
as a VMS sysadmin in the early 80s.

Versions are nothing compared to systems like SCCS or RCS, or modern
approaches like Atria.  You need to save the right versions, track them,
keep track of when and why changes were made.  Or be able to go back
to some point in time: on the filesystem I'm currently using, I can
cd to one hour ago or to one day ago as snapshots are automatically
taken.

>But DEC has certainly slowed down their Marketing of VMS !!!

And well they should.  VMS does have some very nice features, particularly
the asynchronous I/O scheme.  But in a world where all Unix is a niche,
VMS is such a tiny niche as to hardly be worth preserving.  Better to
migrate the good stuff to other frameworks that have a chance of survival.




