Karl Lovink RI wrote:
> 
> I own a DECpc 452St and having now curious problems with its EISA
> configuration.
> When I boot the system it complains that EISA slot 0 is invalid. I ran ECU
> and configured the system. After the reboot it still complains about the
> EISA configuration in slot 0. When I stick a EISA board in it, VGA or SCSI
> this board puts up several times in de ECU configuration when viewing or
> editing details. It then also complains when verifying the EISA
> configuration that slot 0, which is the motherboard, has a invalid system
> id and then it uses the hardware system id. I tried to clear the CMOS, no
> result. So I'm lost,
> does anyone have some tips to get the EISA
> configuration correct again.
> 

Karl, Slot 00 is always the motherboard. You can't actually put a card
into this slot (unless you are using a modular bus/CPU setup) as it
normally doesn't physically exist.

If you get complaints that the system ID is invalid, it is more than
likely that the motherboards' NOVRAM is dying. You can replace this;
look for a DALLAS Semiconductor "Black Box" about 2 cm x 1 cm on the
motherboard. After this, you should be able to successfully re-run the
ECU to re-configure from yur previously saved SCI configuration file.

Rob Schofield
