Aaron Sloman See Signature for address wrote:

> On the other hand, my impression is that Solaris is a far more mature
> operating system than Digital Unix, and that people who have had to
> manage or use both prefer Solaris (is that generally true?)
> 

In my experience, yes.  DEC shipped several versions of OSF/1 which were
buggy, including one where the CRON daemon was prone to failure under a
large number of situations (!).  That's one of the reasons they renamed
it - the OSF/1 name was linked to a system which wasn't fully finished.

> Question, why don't DEC and SUN get together to make Solaris available
> on Alphas?
> 
> Maybe DEC could then licence Sun to sell boxes containing Alphas+Solaris
> and Sun could licence DEC to do the same.
> 
> This unification of two major unix players could have a significant
> impact in some of the larger battles going on.
> 
> Or is this suggestion too naive?

Sun is a single company with several business divisions, so in theory
the people writing the operating system are completely divorced from the
people making the hardware.

However, Sparc systems control the market today.  The reasons for this
are multiple, but the main one is that the software the user wants to
run is available for the platform, and the system is mature and
well-supported.

DEC Unix is much less mature, and since DEC are very keen to get NT
running on everything they produce, their commitment to Unix is slightly
suspect.  Their hardware is fast and generally cheaper than Sun's, but
their maintenance contracts are a minefield - as an example, I was
evaluating tenders for a mid-range workstation in my last job (about 3
months ago), and had to include the price of 5-year up-front maintenance
on the hardware.  Sun quoted a single price per year for the workstation
and all peripherals, and would cover additional items free, so I could
add extra disks, tape drives, etc at no additional cost.

DEC charged a single rate for the workstation, which changed after 2 and
4 years.  They then added on a charge for each of the power supply,
keyboard, mouse, monitor, disk drives, tape drives, memory, and the
_power_cable_ all of which changed rates after 2 and 4 years.  

At the end of the day, the Alpha was cheaper initially by about 2K, but
more expensive to maintain by about 1.5K, and then there's the
maintenance channel...

Finally, the DEC sales companies do themselves no favours - I dealt with
the main UK educational reseller, and they were terrible - quotes with
incorrect prices (their Web page was more recent and cheaper), no quotes
at all, two different quotes at the same time, equipment delivered over
4 weeks, pieces missing, systems configured which would not work (wide
SCSI disks with a narrow SCSI controller), etc.


Having said all of that, the basic equation is much simpler - for every
system Sun sells, they may have a 25% profit margin on each component. 
So, for each mid-range workstation, they make a profit of about 4K on
the hardware, and $20 on the OS.  I wonder how they managed to make $2Bn
last year?

Paul.

