No, it's not generally true. I prefer Digital UNIX. You won't get
decent admin tools with either of them without paying for them. So I
rather resorted to freeware solutions and my own scripting to perform
sysadmin tasks.

Both OS'es have their strong sides, but in my opinion DU has the edge.
It's far more modern in many respects, it's BSD in it's heart, while
pretty compatible with SYSV, it is (from 3.2 on) not less stable in my
experience than Solaris 2.5.1, it has a MUCH better filesystem, is subjec-
tively MUCH faster on comparable hardware, DEC licenses are easy to
circumvent in case you can't find that PAK anymore, etc, etc.

On the other hand, Solaris is more popular so many tools and applications
are likely to run earlier on Solaris than on DU, Solstice Enterprise Manager
(a key application for me) runs on Solaris, SUN workstations have a nicer
design, my current employer gets a huge discount from SUN, etc, etc.

