Karsten Nyblad (karsten@tdr.dk) wrote:
: Terry C. Shannon wrote:
: > Galaxies relies on Adaptive Partitioned Multi-Processing, (APMP), a new
: > computing model which enables many copies of OSes to execute
: > cooperatively . Support for node-private I/O and memory (each node in
: > the diagram has its own copy of OpenVMS), as well as shared memory for
: > applications and cluster-wide global sections, create a
: > cluster-in-a-cabinet "grandson of Andromeda" paradigm.

: Hi,

: What is the difference between Galaxies and the IBM VM (Virtual
: Machines) operating system?

I think you're inverting the concept.  As I recall, VM allows many OS to run 
concurrently on ONE MACHINE.  Galaxies allow many copies of ONE OS on many 
machines to cooperate at both the kernel (from VMScluster technology), and 
if I read correctly, at the application level (shared global sections and 
such).

Now, in the real universe a cluster is made up of galaxies, and GALXY was the
TOPS-20 batch system.

