From: Jerry Leichter <leichter@smarts.com>
Subject: Re: Galaxies
Date: 1997/06/02
Message-ID: <3392CE24.720B@smarts.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 245625890
References: <m0wStLZ-000I15C@ivan.iecc.com> <3385F569.41C6@nas.nasa.gov> <1997May28.023046.1@eisner> <EAyv0n.AvM@mv.mv.com> <5mlc9e$ah6@tooting.netapp.com> <1997May30.021200.1@eisner> <338F3805.1EFF@world.std.com> <5mtqm1$22l$1@wagner.spc.videotron.ca>
To: "jfmezei"@videotron.ca.[no.spam]
Organization: System Management ARTS
Newsgroups: comp.arch,comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


| > Galaxies relies on Adaptive Partitioned Multi-Processing, (APMP), a 
| >new computing model which enables many copies of OSes to execute
| > cooperatively....

| Out of curiosity, is this not similar to the VM operating system for
| IBM mainframes that allowed multiple virtual machines to coexist on 
| the same machine with (some) communications between them ? ...

Not really; it's the other way around.

The DEC model takes a single physical machine with multiple physical
processors and subdivides it into multiple pieces, each running a copy
of the same OS.  In effect, you partition one *physical* machine into
multiple *physical* machines.  Any one of the submachines only has
access to its own physical resources (plus, of course, anything that was
set up as shared - particularly, shared memory).

IBM's VM takes a single physical machine, with one or more physical
processors, and uses it to simulate multiple *virtual* machines.  Each
of those virtual machines has, in principle, access to *all* the
physical resources of the underlying real machine (though of course it
only uses what it needs).  The assignment of resources to virtual
machines is done by what's sometimes called a "hypervisor".  There is
only one hypervisor, responsible for all the physical resources, and
ultimately it's the hypervisor, not any of the guest OS's, that deals
with physical hardware.  In the DEC model, there is no hypervisor; each
of the partitioned pieces owns whatever parts of the hardware it's been
granted, and deal with it directly.  (Oh, certainly there is a software
layer that manages the subdivision, and presumably may even be able to
change it dynamically; but it handles setup, not normal operation.  As a
simple example, guest OS's never touch the real mapping hardware under
VM - since that's what keeps the virtual machines separate, it's owned
by the hypervisor, which intercepts guest requests and deals with them
in a globally-correct way.  The different hardware subpieces that DEC is
talking about each have an OS that owns its own memory mapping hardware,
and deals with it directly.)

A better analogy might be to the OS code one saw on hypercubes (back
when people were building hypercubes).  Often, it's nice to allow
multiple users on a hypercube at once; so these systems let you
partition your n-cube into (say) two (n-1)-cubes.  Software handed one
of these (n-1)-cubes (ideally, and actually, on the better systems)
really "owned" half the nodes, and ran exactly as if it were on a
physical machine of half the dimensionality.

							-- Jerry



