| It would seem that shared memory would reduce reliability. If a single
| processor goes bad (but does not fail) and starts to zero out memory
| then the entire system would fail. With distributed memory I don't
| think this failure would harm other systems.

Sure.  On the other hand, when was the last time you saw that particular
failure mode?  (I can recall things of that general sort in the
mid-70's, but not since then.)

There are always (performance/economic) limits to the kinds of failure
modes you can reasonably hope to deal with - and that's assuming that
you are beyond the *theoretical* limits.  Thus:  For a "synchronous
enough" system with no failures possible in the interconnet (one in
which you can time messages out, and are always correct in your
assumption that if a message did't arrive by its deadline, the sender is
dead), "Byzantine" (arbitrary) failure of more than 1/3 of the nodes is
inherently unrecoverable.  In a fully asynchronous system, even failure
of a single node by simple stopping is inherently unrecoverable. 
Neither of these (theoretical) extremes are very interesting for most
real systems, but to avoid them you have to make assumptions (i.e.,
decide that there are some failure modes you just don't care about).

BTW, the "zeroing all memory" failure can be made that much less likely
by configuring "guard bands" of inaccessible memory between shared
blocks.  The "guard bands" would simply not be mapped by any processor. 
For a processor to damage more than one block of shared memory, it would
first have to set up mappings for the guard bands.  A deliberate attack
might do such a thing - but a bug?  In such a setup, a even an
implausible "zero all accessible memory" bug would quickly crash the
processor.  Presumably the other processors would notice and could have
code to recover.

Shared memory does give you closer coupling, so there certainly is
*some* additional vulnerability; but in practice, over effects will
almost certainly dominate, given properly designed code.

							-- Jerry



