| The definition of the flags (condition codes) on both 68K and VAX
| is problematic, since frequently instructions write only a subset
| and leave the rest undefined. x86 does this much less often.

As best I can recall - I don't have a VAX reference here to check - the
VAX doesn't do this *at all*.  (I don't think the PDP-11 did either.) 
Every instruction either sets *all* the condition codes (with some of
them necessarily fixed - e.g., TST can set Zero or Negative, but always
clears oVerflow and Carry) or affects *none* of them.

There are theoretical advantages to setting only a minimal set *if* you
leave the other ones alone, as you can then (theoretically) re-arrange
code when convenient to delay tests.  In practice, I suspect this
happens so rarely that all you are really doing is making it harder for
people to use the ISP - it's just one more complication to learn. 
(Compilers probably don't much care either way - it's just a table
entry.)  I can't think of *any* advantages (from the *programming* point
of view) to leaving some of the condition codes undefined.

							-- Jerry



