This was probably the same guy that gave DEC the wonderful 
advice on SDI vs. SMD.  Here was DEC, co-developer of the very 
successful SMD *standard* and part owner of a very successful
supplier of disks meeting that standard, and then, DEC went
off and developed its own proprietary version, SDI, and sued
anybody who wanted to build a plug-compatible version.  Then,
there was the BI bus.  The list could go on.

Historically, DEC was more profitable when it "gave away"
technology than it was when it became stingy.  Maybe there
is a lesson here.  I wonder how much customer good will
DEC lost during those years when it attempted to force
buyers of its systems to also buy its disk drives &etc &etc.

I can imagine a clone of this attorney knocking on the door
at Netscape right now, telling them to stop giving away
browsers, and at Microsoft, telling Microsoft the same thing.
My prediction is that the company that hires him first will
lose big in the browser wars.

> The patents cited in the intel-suit owe some of their roots to the work
> done on the KC10 and the efforts with pipelined processors.  They also
> owe some bit of inspiration to the efforts associated with numerous
> sytems that were never built for sale (HYDRA, MINNOW, DOLPHIN, etc.. the
> CACHE-8, the REAL PDT, and a few more) AND the folks who came up with
> these break through techniques and technologies and made them real.  The
> creative capabilities of the DEC engineering staff have always been
> phenomenal.  Well done!

Personally, I was disappointed that DEC didn't come out
with at least one more faster DEC/10/20 processor, but
then, there never would have been a Foonly if they had.
But, if you are looking at late 70's early 80's projects,
consider how much about pipelining was already known
in the mid 1960's to early 70's with the CDC 6600/7600/Star,
IBM 360/91 and 360/370/195, and the IBM RISC prototypes. 
Many of the details are documented in Siewiorek, Bell, and Newell.

> As Terry Shannon cited, the increase in VUP performance of the CVax line
>  is tied to these patents.  The brilliance of the technologists who
> applied these concepts to different architectures is missed outside of
> dec but it is a formidable fete!!!

I find this very confusing.  The CVAX was a bust, IIRC;
in fact, the CVAX was cited by RISC proponents as showing
just how bad CISC was, with all the time, money and effort
that went in to such a meager result.  By the time the *NVAX* 
came out, RISC had already won the "hearts and minds" of 
computing, but the NVAX did very well in comparison to RISC.
It didn't get much press, though, because DEC had already
committed to Alpha.


> Makes you wonder what else might be lurking in the lab!!
> 
> > So? If the patent was infringed, it doesn't really matter. Besides, at
> > least a couple of the patents look to me like they could have been for
> > DEC's VAX stuff as well as for Alpha. (One mentions variable length
> > instructions for example.)

There was a moderately detailed look at NVAX in one of the 
electronic design magazines - probably Electronic Design,
at the time it came out.  It might be interesting to compare 
Pentium/PPro features with the NVAX features as described.
The NVAX went amazingly far with the VAX instruction set,
and I could imagine some of it would have been applicable
to Pentium/PPro-like processors.


(I am not a patent attorney and I am not giving any legal
advice on the DEC/Intel patent question or any other legal
question.)

