Rick Hawkins (rhawkins@iastate.edu) wrote --

> Those old ws commands actually made a lot of sense, especially with the
> help screen that was the upper third of the screen until you learned to
> turn it off.  The diamond formed by e-s-d-x provided direction, and keys
> next to them (a,f, etc) extened these dirextions.  So ^s moved a
> character to the left, while ^a would be a whole word; d&f went
> right.  It's been a while, but i belive that ^c generaly
> "amplified"-- ^c-f would be end of line, or paragraph, or some such.

Not bad at all. ^Q, not ^C, is the "amplifier" key (note the use of the
present tense). Q for "quick." The cursor diamond is surrounded by the
"scrolling square": ^W and ^Z move the viewframe a line at a time, ^R and
^C a whole screenful. ^Qw or ^Qz starts dynamically adjustable continuous
scrolling, and ^Qr or ^Qc takes you to the top or bottom of the file.

Putting the "quickener" on the right hand might allow even faster
operation; dunno if it's been tried. All I know is that when I first saw
scroll bars I couldn't help but think, "But... but... but... what
FOR?!?!?"

> ^k was the file operation prefix.  there "s" would be save, "x" would
> exit, "o" would open, etc.  there were also ^j and ^o prefixes. (^j-h
> would reset the help level, for example).

Yup, ^K is the BlocK menu. A file is a block, too, after all. ^O for On-
screen formatting, ^P for Print enhancements, ^J for... Help?!? Yes,
because ^H is an ASCII backspace and the touch typist's best friend, and
the J key is right under your right index finger. ^U for Undo/cancel; ^L
for Last search or search-and-replace, ^N to insert a New line, ^G to
"gobble" the character under the cursor, ^B to "box in" the current
paragraph (fit it to the margins; this later became automatic). Not
everything can or should be on a mnemonic letter, but what can be, is.

> After getting used to the program, you could easily use commands you'd
> never heard of, as it was obvious what such a cmmand would be.  And I
> still don't see how function keys are an improvement; <file-prefix>-s
> makes a lot more sense than "shift-alt-f7" to save a file.

Amen. Once IBM-style keyboards became practically ubiquitous, though,
some developers seemed to think they just *had* to put all the new keys
to use and ignore the old ones -- and as any long-time keyboarder can
see, some designed their keyboard UIs to discourage keyboard use.

WordPerfect Corp. seems to be in the first group. I never considered
using their keyboard UI! Opened the manual to "Cursor Movement," flipped
immediately to "Keyboard Layout, Edit," then went to Setup to unhide the
menu bar, and all was well with the world (except that only function keys
will put formatting codes into search strings :-[).

