Curt Sampson wrote:
> [Responding to me, Andy Glew  <glew@cs.wisc.edu>]
> So why did Intel switch to using a RISC core for the Pentium Pro,
> and doing their OOO stuff on those instructions? It appears that
> Intel, at least, thinks that RISC has some sort of advantage.

Oh, I guess that I wouldn't know anything about that.

Tomasulo, by the way, did give me shit about the P6 internally being
a load/store machine, since he said that most of his out-of-order
designs are internally REG := REG op MEM.


> I don't see why one couldn't decompose VAX or 68K instructions in
> the same way.

Yale Patt and Wen-mei Hwu tried to apply such techniques at Berkeley,
to VAX, in Digital funded research, although they eventually gave up
on the VAX instruction set architecture and rolled their own.

I know of no DEC VAX that was fully OOO, although they may have done
one. For that matter, I know of no DEC patents that apply to any
technology in the Intel chips I worked on, particularly on 
out-of-order. But I do know of Intel patents...

If I had not refused to sign Motorola's pre-employment drug test 
consent form (note that I don't object to drug tests, since I took
one for Intel when I joined P6 - I just read the forms very carefully) 
I probably would have ended up working on a Motorola 68K out-of-order 
implementation. It would probably have used the same sort of 
convert to RISC format.  But, since I had been working for Motorola
before going back to school for my MS, I had studied the matter,
and I am reasonably confident that the x86 was easier.

---

Apart from the flags as mentioned in a previous post, the biggest
difference lies in the addressing modes. The x86 has relatively
simple addressing modes. VAX and 68K have quite complex addressing
modes. (Remember what I said about RAMM (Reduced Addressing Mode 
Machine) being a better term than RISC?)

Each x86 instruction corresponds to a single sequence of RISCy uops:

	ADD reg, mem
		tmp := load( MEM )
		reg := add( reg, tmp )

The addressing modes have only limited orthogonality - you only
have to look one or two instruction bytes ahead, to get the MODRM
bit, and you can tell whether it is a REG,REG or REG,MEM form.

Each VAX/68K addressing mode is potentially a little program of
its own.  Each addressing mode corresponds to a variable sequence
of uops.  Parsing of the second operand cannot really begin until the
first has been understood.

A parallel decoder for multiple VAX instructions would be, umm,
challenging. You could do it by brute force parallelism, but that's
expensive, and has logarithmic decision time and exponential space.

You could use a decoded intruction cache, but DICs don't work
as well as people think.

Sure, I know of ways around the VAX/68K issues.  I didn't say
it would have been impossible to do, just harder. 

