It seems that on objective measures the latest Sun's Ultrasparcs still
cannot match the latest Alphas for speed. E.g. See

    ftp://ftp.cdf.toronto.edu/pub/spectable

from which the following is extracted

System            CPU        ClkMHz  Cache      SPECint SPECfp  Info  Source
Name              (NUMx)Type ext/in  Ext+I/D      95      95    Date  Obtained
Sun Ultra2/1300   UltraSP    100/300 2M+16/16    12.1    15.5   Apr97 Sun intro
DEC 500/500       A21164     48/500  8M+96+8/8   15.0    20.4   Aug96 Digital

And latest DEC 500/600 figures at
    http://www.specbench.org/osg/cpu95/results/

    DEC 600au                                    18.4     21.3
      Primary Cache: 8KB(I)+8KB(D) on chip
    Secondary Cache: 96KB on chip
        Other Cache: 2MB

On the other hand, my impression is that Solaris is a far more mature
operating system than Digital Unix, and that people who have had to
manage or use both prefer Solaris (is that generally true?)


Question, why don't DEC and SUN get together to make Solaris available
on Alphas?

Maybe DEC could then licence Sun to sell boxes containing Alphas+Solaris
and Sun could licence DEC to do the same.

Then Unix users could get the best of both worlds.

This unification of two major unix players could have a significant
impact in some of the larger battles going on.

Or is this suggestion too naive?

Aaron
